; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; checker — the type half of the reference's internal/checker.
;
; ── What this module is FOR, and what a slice of it is ──────────────────────
;
; The checker answers two questions about a bound tree, and the fifth yardstick
; compares both (tests/oracle/types.go, TESTPLAN.md's *The fifth yardstick*):
;
;   C  the SEMANTIC DIAGNOSTICS of one file — GetDiagnostics, which runs the
;      whole check of the file and answers out of the checker's own sorted list
;   T  the TYPE of every node the reference's own baseline walker may ask about —
;      GetTypeAtLocation plus TypeToString
;
; Neither is reachable through any earlier artifact, which is why the yardstick is
; a fifth one rather than a section of the fourth: a type is not in the tree and
; not on a symbol. It is computed on demand, memoised inside the checker and never
; written back, so a port that computes NO types and a port that computes WRONG
; ones compare exactly equal on the first four, in both directions.
;
; ── The dimension's size, and what that decides ─────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ internal/checker is 59 971 lines against the 12 540 of scanner + parser +
; binder — measured at slice 46, which projects 88 000–141 000 lines of Scaly, two
; to three times the whole port to date. So this file will be built over many
; slices, and the SKELETON's job is to make every one of them an ARM: the type
; representation, the intrinsic types, the printer's keyword arms, the file-level
; check, the diagnostic list with the reference's sort, and — the part that earns
; its keep — a REPORT at every arm that is not ported, so tests/triage.py names
; the arm that unlocks the most units next. That is the binder's arrangement
; (§3.5bw) one dimension over.
;
; ── What is NOT ported, and why each omission is a decision ─────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FLOW GRAPH PARAGRAPH THAT STOOD HERE IS SPENT, AND IT IS KEPT AS A
; POINTER RATHER THAN DELETED BECAUSE IT NAMED BOTH OF ITS READERS CORRECTLY. It
; read *the flow graph is not ported, and it is the checker that needs it*, and
; listed the two dependants: NARROWING, which the narrowing engine closed, and
; REACHABILITY, which stayed open twenty-nine slices longer than the graph did —
; `is_reachable_flow_node` has been in this file since that engine landed and
; nothing CALLED it from the statement walk. Slice 184 wrote the caller
; (check_source_element_unreachable). What the paragraph got wrong is the price it
; put on reachability: it priced the chapter at TS7027, which no yardstick can see
; (0 of 64 148 reference C lines), and the chapter's real product is a SECOND VISIT
; to the nodes under a flow-call node. See within_unreachable_code.
;
; ★★ THE RELATER AND THE NODEBUILDER ARE NOT PORTED EITHER, and each is a
; dimension rather than a gap. relater.go (5 006 lines) is assignability, which
; even `var x: string = 1` needs; nodebuilderimpl.go (3 585) is the type PRINTER,
; of which this file ports the keyword arms only — the twenty lines of
; typeToTypeNodeHelper that answer an INTRINSIC — because those are the only type
; shapes anything here can produce.
;
; ★★★ AND THE COUPLING THE SKELETON MEASURED, because it decides how this
; dimension advances: THE C SECTION GATES THE T SECTION. One dump is one
; comparison, so a unit counts as matched only when both halves agree — and the C
; half is complete only when the check of EVERY statement in the unit is ported.
; Measured over the whole corpus: 7 300 of 17 810 units — 41 % — have an EMPTY C
; section, and not one of them can be claimed without porting the checks that PROVE
; it empty. So the
; 1 230 663 type answers of the corpus wait behind its 63 127 diagnostics, and the
; checker dimension advances at the speed of checkSourceElement. ★The escape hatch
; is named here rather than taken: the ORACLE's output would not change by one
; byte if the comparison were SPLIT into two yardsticks (C and T), because the two
; sections are already separate lines — it is compare.py, triage.py, run.sh and
; ctl.sh that would each gain a column (slice 46's rule: all four in one commit,
; or the first battery lies). That is a harness decision with a measured argument
; behind it, and it belongs to whichever slice finds the coupling is actually
; holding the type machinery back.
;
; ── What the harness fixes, and what our side therefore inherits ────────────
;
; The oracle builds a STUB program: no module resolution, the per-case
; @target/@lib/@module directives ignored, and the lib set fixed to lib.es5.d.ts
; (priced at slice 46 as a 3 % diagnostic delta against the reference's default
; closure). Two of those three are symmetric for free here, because this port has
; no program layer at all. The third is not: this side reads NO lib, so every
; global name is unresolved — which is invisible while no arm resolves a name, and
; is the first thing the slice that ports name resolution has to answer. It is
; written down here so that slice does not discover it as a diff.
;
; ★ compilerOptions: the harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation
; and nothing else, so every option this file would read is at its default —
; strictNullChecks false, exactOptionalPropertyTypes false, noImplicitAny false,
; NoUnusedLocals/NoUnusedParameters false (hence checkUnused false at
; getDiagnostics). Each place that matters says which option it is reading and
; what its default answers, so that an options record arriving later is a
; substitution rather than a search.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.Vector
use scaly.containers.String
use scaly.containers.StringBuilder
use tscaly.TsOptionDecls.TsOptionDecls
use tscaly.addressindex.AddressIndex
use tscaly.addressindex.HashChain
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.ast.Symbol
use tscaly.ast.FlowNode
use tscaly.ast.FlowList
; ★ Slice 94 is the first slice in which the CHECKER mints an AST node — the
; synthetic `this.p` of isPropertyInitializedInConstructor — so the payload union
; and the one arm it builds have to be visible here as well as the node itself.
use tscaly.ast.NodeData
use tscaly.ast.ExportAssignmentData
use tscaly.ast.ImportOrExportSpecifierData
use tscaly.ast.TokenData
use tscaly.ast.PropertyAccessData
; slice 117: the synthetic element access getFlowTypeOfDestructuring builds.
use tscaly.ast.LiteralData
use tscaly.ast.ElementAccessData
use tscaly.ast.WrappedTypeData
; slice 120: the synthetic access NAME getFlowTypeInConstructor builds — an
; Identifier or a PrivateIdentifier, which share this payload.
use tscaly.ast.IdentifierData
; slice 134: newCallSignature's synthetic FunctionType declaration — the node
; getOrCreateTypeFromSignature asks the KIND of.
use tscaly.ast.FunctionTypeData
; slice 199: the synthesized `children` PropertySignature
; createJsxAttributesTypeFromAttributesProperty fakes up.
use tscaly.ast.PropertyDeclarationData
use tscaly.scanner.Scanner
use tscaly.parser.Parser
use tscaly.binder.Binder
use tscaly.program.ProgramFiles
use tscaly.program.ResolutionContext
use tscaly.program.JsonValue
use tscaly.buildinfojson.JsonText
use tscaly.program.ModuleResolver
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList
use tscaly.Diagnostics.RelatedInfo
use tscaly.modulespecifiers.SpecifierHost
use tscaly.emitter.NodeBuilder
use tscaly.xxh3.Xxh3
; ★ Slice 113: the Checker is this port's PROGRAM stub (see create's header), and
; a program is what reads the lib off the disk. The oracle's `main` does the same
; three steps — read, parse, bind — before it builds a checker at all.
use scaly.os.File

; getenv(3), for the one thing the lib loader cannot derive: WHERE the submodule
; is. The default is the path from the repository root, which is the directory
; every harness in tests/ runs from; TSCALY_LIB overrides it.
function getenv(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[const_char] extern

; ── ObjectFlags: two bits, deliberately not a table ─────────────────────────
;
; ★★ The reference's ObjectFlags is a 40-entry uint32 enum in
; internal/checker/types.go, and it is NOT generated beside TypeFlags for a reason
; that is about the enum rather than about effort: its bit positions are REUSED.
; `1 << 22` is ObjectFlagsContainsSpread on a type with TypeFlagsObject,
; ObjectFlagsIsGenericTypeComputed on a union or intersection, and the comments in
; the source are what say which — so a flat table of names would read as 40
; independent bits and be wrong in exactly the way a reader cannot see. It also
; carries a FORWARD reference (ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask names
; ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType, declared below it), which gentypeflags.py
; would refuse rather than resolve.
;
; So the two bits the intrinsic block below actually SETS are declared here, with
; their positions from the source, and the table arrives with the slice that needs
; the object types — where the reuse discipline can be ported with it.
define ObjectFlagsNone: int 0

; ── the bits that require TypeFlagsObject, in the reference's own order ──────
;
; ★★ SLICE 66 BRINGS THE TABLE THE NOTE ABOVE PROMISED, and it stops where the
; reuse begins. Bits 0..21 mean one thing each; `1 << 22` and up are the reused
; positions the note names, and they are NOT declared, because the name would be
; the lie the note describes. A slice that needs ContainsSpread declares it beside
; the reader that establishes which TypeFlags it is reading it under.
define ObjectFlagsClass: int 1 << 0                   ; Class
define ObjectFlagsInterface: int 1 << 1               ; Interface
define ObjectFlagsReference: int 1 << 2               ; Generic type reference
define ObjectFlagsTuple: int 1 << 3                   ; Synthesized generic tuple type
define ObjectFlagsAnonymous: int 1 << 4               ; Anonymous
define ObjectFlagsMapped: int 1 << 5                  ; Mapped
define ObjectFlagsInstantiated: int 1 << 6            ; Instantiated anonymous or mapped type
define ObjectFlagsInstantiatedMapped: int ObjectFlagsMapped | ObjectFlagsInstantiated
define ObjectFlagsObjectLiteral: int 1 << 7           ; Originates in an object literal
define ObjectFlagsEvolvingArray: int 1 << 8           ; Evolving array type
define ObjectFlagsObjectLiteralPatternWithComputedProperties: int 1 << 9
define ObjectFlagsReverseMapped: int 1 << 10          ; Object contains a property from a reverse-mapped type
define ObjectFlagsJsxAttributes: int 1 << 11          ; Jsx attributes type
define ObjectFlagsJSLiteral: int 1 << 12              ; Object type declared in JS
define ObjectFlagsFreshLiteral: int 1 << 13           ; Fresh object literal
define ObjectFlagsArrayLiteral: int 1 << 14           ; Originates in an array literal
define ObjectFlagsPrimitiveUnion: int 1 << 15         ; Union of only primitive types
define ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType: int 1 << 16   ; Type is or contains undefined or null widening type
define ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral: int 1 << 17 ; Type is or contains object literal type
define ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType: int 1 << 18      ; Type is or contains anyFunctionType or silentNeverType
define ObjectFlagsCouldContainTypeVariablesComputed: int 1 << 19
define ObjectFlagsCouldContainTypeVariables: int 1 << 20
define ObjectFlagsMembersResolved: int 1 << 21        ; Members have been resolved
; Slice 85. Set for the length of resolveObjectTypeMembers' base loop and cleared
; after it — *member resolution in process*. Its only READER upstream is the cache
; key of getPropertiesOfType's index-signature variant, a chapter away; it is
; written here because the flag is what tells a re-entrant ask that the members it
; can see are half-built.
define ObjectFlagsUnresolvedMembers: int 1 << 29

define ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface: int ObjectFlagsClass | ObjectFlagsInterface

; ── the two REUSED positions slice 94 reads, each under its own TypeFlags ────
;
; ★★ THE TABLE ABOVE STOPS AT BIT 21 BECAUSE THE POSITIONS ABOVE IT MEAN
; DIFFERENT THINGS ON DIFFERENT TYPES, and its own rule is that *a slice that
; needs one declares it beside the reader that establishes which TypeFlags it is
; reading it under*. These are the first two to arrive under that rule, and the
; name carries the condition:
;
;   1 << 25 is ObjectFlagsContainsIntersections on a type with TypeFlagsUnion,
;           and ObjectFlagsIsNeverIntersection on one with TypeFlagsIntersection.
;   1 << 27 is ObjectFlagsIsConstrainedTypeVariable on a type with
;           TypeFlagsIntersection, and IsGenericTypeComputed elsewhere.
;
; ★★★ THE SECOND IS READ ONLY WHERE IT CANNOT FIRE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS
; DECLARED RATHER THAN LEFT OUT. add_type_to_union's constrained-type-variable
; term is `flags & Intersection <> 0 and t.object_flags & this <> 0`. ★★★THE
; SENTENCE THAT FOLLOWED — *"and NOTHING IN THIS PORT MINTS AN INTERSECTION TYPE —
; new_object_type has no arm for one and there is no new_intersection_type"* —
; EXPIRED AT SLICE 121, when `get_intersection_type_ex` arrived, and it was still
; being read as live four slices later at eight sites (§3.5gk). Seven of them
; stop; the guard here is the harmless kind, because the SECOND term settles it
; too. The
; term is written with the real bit anyway: a version that dropped the second half
; would be a different function the day an intersection arrives, and the deletion
; would be invisible.
define ObjectFlagsContainsIntersections: int 1 << 25
; ★★★ BIT 25 IS SHARED, AND THE SHARING IS UPSTREAM'S. `ContainsIntersections`
; is a UNION's bit and `IsNeverIntersectionComputed` an INTERSECTION's, so no
; type can be asked both questions — exactly the way `ContainsSpread` shares bit
; 22 with an object flag (see its own note). Naming both is what keeps the next
; reader from "fixing" the collision.
define ObjectFlagsIsNeverIntersectionComputed: int 1 << 25
define ObjectFlagsIsNeverIntersection: int 1 << 26
define ObjectFlagsIsConstrainedTypeVariable: int 1 << 27
; ★★ AND BITS 26/27 ARE SHARED ONE MORE WAY, upstream's own: on a type with
; TypeFlagsUNION they are `IsUnknownLikeUnionComputed` and `IsUnknownLikeUnion`,
; where the two above are read on an INTERSECTION alone. is_unknown_like_union_type
; is the only reader and it tests `flags & Union` before it looks.
define ObjectFlagsIsUnknownLikeUnionComputed: int 1 << 26
define ObjectFlagsIsUnknownLikeUnion: int 1 << 27

; ── IntersectionFlags (slice 121) ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; getIntersectionTypeEx's second parameter. Both non-zero members turn a
; REDUCTION off, and both are set by exactly one caller: the type node passes
; NoSupertypeReduction for `X & {}`, and NoConstraintReduction comes from the
; relation's own intersection construction.
; ── IndexFlags (slice 121) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; getIndexTypeEx's second parameter. ★NoReducibleCheck exists to break a
; recursion rather than to change an answer: getIndexType asks
; isGenericReducibleType, which asks getIndexType back.
define IndexFlagsNone: int 0
define IndexFlagsStringsOnly: int 1 << 0
define IndexFlagsNoIndexSignatures: int 1 << 1

; ── MappedTypeModifiers and MappedTypeNameTypeKind (slice 125) ──────────────
;
; Two of the reference's own enums, WHOLE for TypeSystemPropertyName's reason:
; the four modifier bits are one word read by one function and the three name-type
; kinds are the arms of one dispatch, so a partial declaration would invite a
; renumbering nothing here could catch.
;
; ★ The modifiers are what `+readonly` / `-readonly` / `+?` / `-?` on a mapped
; type mean, and the pairing is the point: INCLUDE wins over EXCLUDE at every
; reader, and the ABSENCE of both is *inherit from the modifiers type*, which is a
; third answer neither bit spells on its own.
define MappedTypeModifiersIncludeReadonly: int 1 << 0
define MappedTypeModifiersExcludeReadonly: int 1 << 1
define MappedTypeModifiersIncludeOptional: int 1 << 2
define MappedTypeModifiersExcludeOptional: int 1 << 3

; MappedTypeNameTypeKind — what the `as N` clause of `{ [P in K as N]: T }` does.
; FILTERING when N is assignable to P (the keys are a subset of K), REMAPPING
; otherwise, None when there is no clause at all. The distinction decides whether
; the synthesized property keeps the ORIGINAL declarations — a remapped key names
; a different property, so it must not.
define MappedTypeNameTypeKindNone: int 0
define MappedTypeNameTypeKindFiltering: int 1
define MappedTypeNameTypeKindRemapping: int 2
define IndexFlagsNoReducibleCheck: int 1 << 2

define IntersectionFlagsNone: int 0
define IntersectionFlagsNoSupertypeReduction: int 1 << 0
define IntersectionFlagsNoConstraintReduction: int 1 << 1

; ★ ObjectFlagsContainsSpread, slice 119, and it is the OTHER meaning of bit 22 —
; the one this block's header names first. It is set by getSpreadType on a type
; that already carries TypeFlagsObject, so the two readings never meet on one
; value; the pair is the clearest illustration of why the whole enum is written by
; hand rather than generated.
define ObjectFlagsContainsSpread: int 1 << 22

; ★ The four genericity bits, slice 117. IsGenericType is the OR of the other two
; and is written as a literal for ElementFlagsFixed's reason — a global's
; initializer folds inside ONE precedence level, and `1 << 23 | 1 << 24` mixes the
; shift with the `|`.
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericTypeComputed: int 1 << 22
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericObjectType: int 1 << 23
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericIndexType: int 1 << 24
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericType: int 25165824   ; IsGenericObjectType | IsGenericIndexType

; ── AccessFlags (slice 117) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.AccessFlags — the ten bits getIndexedAccessType is steered by. The whole
; table is declared rather than the four this slice reads, because the flags are
; passed THROUGH getIndexedAccessTypeOrUndefined into getPropertyTypeForIndexType
; as one value: a partial table would make the next reader's `accessFlags & X`
; read as a new decision rather than as a transcription.
define AccessFlagsNone: int 0
define AccessFlagsIncludeUndefined: int 1 << 0
define AccessFlagsNoIndexSignatures: int 1 << 1
define AccessFlagsWriting: int 1 << 2
define AccessFlagsCacheSymbol: int 1 << 3
define AccessFlagsAllowMissing: int 1 << 4
define AccessFlagsExpressionPosition: int 1 << 5
define AccessFlagsReportDeprecated: int 1 << 6
define AccessFlagsSuppressNoImplicitAnyError: int 1 << 7
define AccessFlagsContextual: int 1 << 8
define AccessFlagsPersistent: int 1   ; IncludeUndefined

; ── UnionReduction ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.UnionReduction, slice 94. Three values and the reference's own numbers,
; because `unionReduction & UnionReductionSubtype` is a BIT test on them rather
; than an equality — Literal is 1 and Subtype is 2, so Literal does not reduce
; subtypes and None does neither.
;
; ★ getUnionType passes Literal, which is the only value any caller in this port
; passes today; Subtype's callers upstream are inference and the array-literal
; chapter, and the arm that meets one carries `remove-subtypes`.
define UnionReductionNone: int 0
define UnionReductionLiteral: int 1
define UnionReductionSubtype: int 2

; ★ ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags, whose only reader is getPropagatingFlagsOfTypes.
; ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask is NOT declared here for the reason this line has
; carried since it was written — it NAMES two of the reused bits, so it cannot be
; written before they are. Slice 201 declared it at the first point where all nine
; of its members exist, beside ObjectFlagsIsClassInstanceClone.
define ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags: int ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType | ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral | ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType

; ★ ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening arrives in slice 69, where getWidenedType is its
; reader — the note above listed it as having none. It is the gate that decides
; whether widening has anything to do at all.
define ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening: int ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType | ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral

; ★★ ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode is the FIRST of the reused positions to be declared,
; and it is declared the way the table's own note demands — beside its reader and
; with the TypeFlags it is read under. Its reader is getRecursionIdentity (slice
; 81), inside a branch guarded by TypeFlagsObject, which is what makes
; unambiguous there: upstream reuses the bit only for types that are not objects.
; Nothing in this port SETS it (createTypeReferenceEx and the tuple constructors
; do upstream, and both report here), so the guard reading it is written and always
; passes — which is stated rather than hidden, because a guard that cannot fail
; looks exactly like one that was ported wrong.
define ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode: int 1 << 30

; ★★ THE SECOND AND THIRD REUSED POSITIONS, declared on the same discipline and
; with the same reader: `could_contain_type_variables_worker` (slice 86) tests
; both inside a branch guarded by TypeFlagsObject, which is the condition under
; which upstream's reuse is unambiguous. Nothing here SETS either — the object
; rest declaration and the instantiation expression are both chapters away — so
; the test is written and always fails, which is stated rather than hidden for
; ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode's reason.
define ObjectFlagsObjectRestType: int 1 << 23
define ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType: int 1 << 24
; `1 << 25` on a TypeFlagsObject type: a single signature type extracted from a
; potentially broader one. Slice 125 reads it twice in getObjectTypeInstantiation
; — once as part of the instantiation KEY and once to decide whether the fresh
; mapper is merged with the caller's — and nothing here SETS it
; (getSingleSignatureTypeForType has no producer), so both tests are written and
; always fail, on ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode's discipline.
define ObjectFlagsSingleSignatureType: int 1 << 25
; `1 << 26` is ObjectFlagsIsClassInstanceClone on a type with TypeFlagsObject —
; set by getTypeWithThisArgument's clone path. Read by isClassInstanceSide (slice
; 111) and by nothing else here; the clone path this port takes never mints an
; ANONYMOUS type, so the test is written and always fails.
define ObjectFlagsIsClassInstanceClone: int 1 << 26

; ★★★ ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask, DECLARED HERE AND NOT BESIDE
; ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags, because the note there is exact and has not
; expired: the mask names ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType and
; ObjectFlagsSingleSignatureType, two of the reused positions, and a `define`
; folds at plan time against the declarations ABOVE it. Written at the first
; point where all nine of its members exist. Its one reader is compare_types'
; non-reference object arm (slice 201).
define ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask: int ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface | ObjectFlagsReference | ObjectFlagsTuple | ObjectFlagsAnonymous | ObjectFlagsMapped | ObjectFlagsReverseMapped | ObjectFlagsEvolvingArray | ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType | ObjectFlagsSingleSignatureType

; ── WideningKind ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's four-member iota, declared WHOLE because reportImplicitAny
; branches on GeneratorYield and every caller names one of the four. Slice 69
; passes Normal from its single call site; the other three arrive with the return
; type and the generator.
define WideningKindNormal: int 0
define WideningKindFunctionReturn: int 1
define WideningKindGeneratorNext: int 2
define WideningKindGeneratorYield: int 3

; ── thisAssignmentDeclarationKind (slice 120) ───────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's four-member iota, declared WHOLE because
; getWidenedTypeForAssignmentDeclaration branches on three of the four and the
; fourth is its own `nil` answer.
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationNone: int 0        ; not (all) this.property assignments
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationTyped: int 1       ; typed; use the type annotation
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationConstructor: int 2 ; at least one in the constructor; use control flow
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationMethod: int 3      ; methods only; look in base first

; ── CheckMode (slice 70) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's eight-member flag set, declared WHOLE for WideningKind's
; reason: `checkExpressionEx`'s two gates test Inferential and
; SkipGenericFunctions by NAME, `checkExpressionCachedEx` branches on Normal, and
; every caller in the reference names one of the eight — so a partial table would
; make the next arm's test read as a new decision rather than as a transcription.
;
; ★★ ONLY `Normal` IS EVER PASSED HERE, and that is a fact about the CALLERS
; rather than about this table: every call this port makes comes from a statement
; arm or from a declaration's initializer, i.e. from the reference's own
; `checkExpression` / `checkExpressionCached`, both of which pass Normal. The four
; modes that change an answer (Contextual, Inferential, SkipContextSensitive,
; SkipGenericFunctions) are set only by the contextual-typing and inference
; machinery, which reports. ★That is what makes the two gates in
; check_expression_ex provably inert rather than merely quiet — see there.
define CheckModeNormal: int 0
define CheckModeContextual: int 1 << 0            ; Explicitly assigned contextual type, therefore not cacheable
define CheckModeInferential: int 1 << 1           ; Inferential typing
define CheckModeSkipContextSensitive: int 1 << 2  ; Skip context sensitive function expressions
define CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions: int 1 << 3  ; Skip single signature generic functions
define CheckModeIsForSignatureHelp: int 1 << 4    ; Call resolution for purposes of signature help
define CheckModeRestBindingElement: int 1 << 5    ; Checking a type used to determine a rest binding element's type
define CheckModeTypeOnly: int 1 << 6              ; Called from getTypeOfExpression, diagnostics may be omitted
define CheckModeForceTuple: int 1 << 7

; ── TypeFacts (slice 90) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's twenty-seven bits and the forty-two COMBINATIONS built out of
; them, declared WHOLE. Every other flag table in this port is generated
; (tools/gentypeflags.py and its siblings) and this one is hand-written, because
; it does not live in a flags file at all: it sits inside internal/checker/
; checker.go, in the middle of the checker's own declarations, and the generators
; read the enum files. The whole table is transcribed rather than the members with
; a reader, on CheckMode's rule — `getTypeFactsWorker` names forty of the
; forty-two in its own switch, so a partial table would make the next arm read as
; a decision instead of as a transcription.
;
; ★★★ THE COMBINATIONS ARE WRITTEN SYMBOLICALLY AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE — IT
; IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES THEM CHECKABLE. A reader can compare
; `TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts` against the reference line for line; a
; forty-two-row column of decimal numbers can only be trusted. The four masked
; members are the exception the rule has to state: the reference writes them with
; Go's `&^`, and a scalar `define` initializer in this language folds ONE
; precedence level (root CLAUDE.md), so `A & ^(B | C)` is a hard rc-4.
; **They are written as `xor` chains instead, which is EXACT rather than close**:
; every bit removed is set in TypeFactsAll and the removed bits are pairwise
; disjoint, so clearing them and toggling them are the same operation. Verified
; numerically against the reference's own values when this table landed —
; EmptyObjectStrictFacts 83427327, EmptyObjectFacts and UnknownFacts 83886079,
; AndFactsMask 134209471.
;
; ★ TypeFactsAll is the one member written as a NUMBER, for the same folding rule:
; `(1 << 27) - 1` is two levels. The formula is on the line.
define TypeFactsNone: int 0
define TypeFactsTypeofEQString: int 1 << 0
define TypeFactsTypeofEQNumber: int 1 << 1
define TypeFactsTypeofEQBigInt: int 1 << 2
define TypeFactsTypeofEQBoolean: int 1 << 3
define TypeFactsTypeofEQSymbol: int 1 << 4
define TypeFactsTypeofEQObject: int 1 << 5
define TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction: int 1 << 6
define TypeFactsTypeofEQHostObject: int 1 << 7
define TypeFactsTypeofNEString: int 1 << 8
define TypeFactsTypeofNENumber: int 1 << 9
define TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt: int 1 << 10
define TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean: int 1 << 11
define TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol: int 1 << 12
define TypeFactsTypeofNEObject: int 1 << 13
define TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction: int 1 << 14
define TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject: int 1 << 15
define TypeFactsEQUndefined: int 1 << 16
define TypeFactsEQNull: int 1 << 17
define TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull: int 1 << 18
define TypeFactsNEUndefined: int 1 << 19
define TypeFactsNENull: int 1 << 20
define TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull: int 1 << 21
define TypeFactsTruthy: int 1 << 22
define TypeFactsFalsy: int 1 << 23
define TypeFactsIsUndefined: int 1 << 24
define TypeFactsIsNull: int 1 << 25
define TypeFactsIsUndefinedOrNull: int TypeFactsIsUndefined | TypeFactsIsNull
; (1 << 27) - 1 — see the header for why this one is a number.
define TypeFactsAll: int 134217727

; ── slice 128: THE ITERATION AND ASYNC PROTOCOL — IterationUse ──────────────
;
; The reference's `IterationUse`, a bit set the whole iteration chapter is keyed
; on. The eight FLAGS and the nine named COMBINATIONS, verbatim, because every
; one of them is read by name somewhere in this chapter and a caller that spelled
; the bits itself would be a second definition of the same thing.
;
; ★★★ IterationUseCacheFlags IS THE ONE THAT IS NOT A CONVENIENCE. It is the mask
; the iteration-types CACHE keys on — sync/async/for-of and nothing else — so two
; uses differing only in the string or destructuring bit SHARE a row, which is
; the reference's own statement about what the answer depends on. A cache keyed on
; the whole `use` would answer the same and never hit; one keyed on less would
; answer a for-await question with a for-of row.
define IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag: int 1 << 0
define IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag: int 1 << 1
define IterationUseAllowsStringInputFlag: int 1 << 2
define IterationUseForOfFlag: int 1 << 3
define IterationUseYieldStarFlag: int 1 << 4
define IterationUseSpreadFlag: int 1 << 5
define IterationUseDestructuringFlag: int 1 << 6
define IterationUsePossiblyOutOfBounds: int 1 << 7

define IterationUseElement: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag
define IterationUseSpread: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseSpreadFlag
define IterationUseDestructuring: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseDestructuringFlag
define IterationUseForOf: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsStringInputFlag | IterationUseForOfFlag
define IterationUseForAwaitOf: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsStringInputFlag | IterationUseForOfFlag
define IterationUseYieldStar: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseYieldStarFlag
define IterationUseAsyncYieldStar: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseYieldStarFlag
define IterationUseGeneratorReturnType: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag
define IterationUseAsyncGeneratorReturnType: int IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag
define IterationUseCacheFlags: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseForOfFlag

; IterationTypeKind — which of the three slots a caller wants.
define IterationTypeKindYield: int 0
define IterationTypeKindReturn: int 1
define IterationTypeKindNext: int 2

; The members that encode facts about a KIND of type, in the reference's order.
; The presence of a fact means the test is true for some (and possibly all) values
; of that kind.
define TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsEmptyStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsEmptyStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringFacts
define TypeFactsNonEmptyStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsNonEmptyStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQNumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsZeroNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsZeroNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts
define TypeFactsNonZeroNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsNonZeroNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsZeroBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsZeroBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts
define TypeFactsNonZeroBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsNonZeroBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBooleanStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBooleanFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsFalseStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsFalseFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts
define TypeFactsTrueStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsTrueFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsSymbolStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQSymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsSymbolFacts: int TypeFactsSymbolStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsObjectStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQObject | TypeFactsTypeofEQHostObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsObjectFacts: int TypeFactsObjectStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsFunctionStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction | TypeFactsTypeofEQHostObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsFunctionFacts: int TypeFactsFunctionStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsVoidFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsUndefinedFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsFalsy | TypeFactsIsUndefined
define TypeFactsNullFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsFalsy | TypeFactsIsNull
; TypeFactsAll &^ (EQUndefined | EQNull | EQUndefinedOrNull | IsUndefinedOrNull)
define TypeFactsEmptyObjectStrictFacts: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsEQUndefined xor TypeFactsEQNull xor TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull xor TypeFactsIsUndefined xor TypeFactsIsNull
; TypeFactsAll &^ TypeFactsIsUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsEmptyObjectFacts: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsIsUndefined xor TypeFactsIsNull
; TypeFactsAll &^ TypeFactsIsUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsUnknownFacts: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsIsUndefined xor TypeFactsIsNull
define TypeFactsAllTypeofNE: int TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsNEUndefined
; The two masks getIntersectionTypeFacts combines with — an intersection's facts
; are an OR over two bits and an AND over the rest.
define TypeFactsOrFactsMask: int TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject
; TypeFactsAll &^ TypeFactsOrFactsMask
define TypeFactsAndFactsMask: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction xor TypeFactsTypeofNEObject

; ── PredicateSemantics, slice 88 ────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The three-valued answer of the two SYNTACTIC walks the logical and coalescing
; operators use — `getSyntacticTruthySemantics` and
; `getSyntacticNullishnessSemantics`. It is a bit PAIR and not an enum, and that
; is what makes the ConditionalExpression arm one line: a conditional is
; `semantics(whenTrue) | semantics(whenFalse)`, so two branches that disagree
; combine to Sometimes without a case for it.
;
; ★ `Sometimes` is the only value that reports NOTHING, at all four report sites.
; A walk that cannot decide must therefore answer Sometimes — which is why the
; two Identifier arms below answer it when this port cannot resolve the name.
define PredicateSemanticsNone:      int 0
define PredicateSemanticsAlways:    int 1 << 0
define PredicateSemanticsNever:     int 1 << 1
define PredicateSemanticsSometimes: int PredicateSemanticsAlways | PredicateSemanticsNever

; ── the type payloads ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ SLICE 66 IS THE SECOND PAYLOAD, so the union the note below promised
; arrives with it. The reference's hierarchy is four levels of Go embedding —
; `InterfaceType` embeds `TypeReference` embeds `ObjectType` embeds
; `StructuredType` — and §3.1's sum type flattens it: each arm is ONE record
; carrying the slots its own readers ask for, exactly as `NodeData`'s 134 arms do.
;
; ★★★ WHICH SLOTS ARE HERE IS A CLAIM ABOUT READERS, NOT ABOUT THE REFERENCE'S
; STRUCT. §3.5be's rule: a field nobody reads is a claim about the future. Every
; slot below is read by a function this slice ports; every slot NOT here is named
; with the reader that would make it observable, at the arm it belongs to.

; ObjectType.instantiations, as an ASSOCIATION LIST rather than a map — and the
; divergence is worth stating because it makes the port STRONGER than the
; reference here. Upstream keys `map[CacheHashKey]*Type` on `getTypeListKey`, an
; xxh3 hash of the type ids; a hash is what buys O(1) lookup and it buys a
; collision question with it. This slice's lists are one entry long for a
; non-generic class or interface, so a linear scan comparing the ARGUMENT LISTS
; THEMSELVES is both faster and exact — there is no key to collide.
;
; ★ The successor is named: the slice that instantiates generics is the one whose
; lists get long enough for the hash to be worth its collision argument, and it is
; the slice that must port `getTypeListKey` and `keyBuilder` with it.
; ★ The leading `hash` is a filter and not a key: these lists
; are PER TARGET and stay association lists, so the hash only lets the scan
; compare an int before walking two argument lists. Measured on
; `recursiveReverseMappedType`, where `type_lists_equal` under these two scans was
; the head of the profile once the union table was indexed.
define TypeInstantiation
(
    hash: int
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
)

; The map upstream's `instantiations` is, over the port's association list: the
; hash's FIRST row through an address index, the rows of one hash chained in list
; order, caught up with the rows added since (catch_up_instantiation_index#). A
; generic interface (`Event<T>`, `Promise<T>`) collects thousands of rows, and the
; list scan per lookup was a leading item of a 2 800-file profile (2026-09-17).
; `list` names the list the index was built over. ★Since slice 326 the index is
; open-addressed slots of row + 1 probed by the row's own hash (the rows of one
; hash lie in list order along the probe path, so the first equal row still
; answers), made only for a list of ObjectRowChainMin rows or more: an
; AddressIndex per interface took an exclusive page even for one row.
; an instantiation list this long or longer is searched through its hash chain
define ObjectRowChainMin: int 8

define InstantiationIndex
(
    list: ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]
    var slots: ref[Vector[int]]
    var mask: int
    var indexed: int
)

; TupleNormalizer, slice 115 — the reference's own struct, and its two methods are
; Checker methods here because `add` needs addOptionalityEx.
;
; ★ `last_required_index` / `first_rest_index` / `last_optional_or_rest_index` all
; start at -1, which is the reference's initialisation and is load-bearing for the
; tail: `firstRestIndex >= 0 && firstRestIndex < lastOptionalOrRestIndex` is how it
; asks *is there a run to fold*, and a 0 default would answer yes on a tuple with
; no rest element at all.
define TupleNormalizer
(
    var types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    var infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]]?
    var last_required_index: int
    var first_rest_index: int
    var last_optional_or_rest_index: int
)
{
    init() linked







}

; One row of c.tupleTypes, slice 115. The reference's map is keyed by a hash
; (getTupleKey); this is the same list-comparison shape TypeInstantiation uses, and
; the header on the `tuple_targets` field says why.
define TupleTarget
(
    element_infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]]?
    readonly: bool
    target: ref[Type]?
)

; The intrinsics, which carry no payload at all. It exists as an arm rather than
; as a null `data` so that every Type answers a `choose` — the shape ast.scaly's
; TokenData has, for the same reason.
; the intrinsic's name (`any`, `string`, …) lives in its own arm since slice 330:
; every other type carried an empty sixteen-byte slice for it
define NoTypeData (name: Slice[char])

; ObjectType, the reference's plain anonymous object — newObjectType's last arm
; and, in slice 67, the STATIC TYPE OF A CLASS. It carries no slots at all, which
; is a statement about readers rather than an omission: everything the
; reference's ObjectType holds (`members`, `properties`, `callSignatures`,
; `constructSignatures`, `indexInfos`) is written by resolveStructuredTypeMembers
; and read by getPropertiesOfType / getSignaturesOfType / getIndexInfosOfType,
; all three of which report here — and `target` / `mapper` belong to the
; INSTANTIATED anonymous type, whose reader is instantiateType.
;
; ★ It is an arm of its own rather than NoTypeData because the two are different
; answers to the same `choose`: NoTypeData means an INTRINSIC, which is what the
; reference's `AsObjectType()` would refuse to give. An empty arm that can be
; told apart is worth more than a shared one that cannot.
;
; ★★ SLICE 85 GIVES IT ITS ONE SLOT, and the line above named this slice as the
; reader.
;
; ★★★ SLICE 125 ADDS THE OTHER THREE, AND THE TWO NOTES THAT STOOD HERE ARE ITS
; EXPIRY. Both said `target` and `mapper` *belong to the INSTANTIATED anonymous
; type, whose reader is instantiateType* — true while getObjectTypeInstantiation
; reported, and this slice is that function. `instantiations` is the reference's
; `ObjectType.instantiations` map, held as an association list for the reason the
; interface arm's is (see ObjectInstantiation).
define AnonymousTypeData
(
    var resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    var target: ref[Type]?
    var mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    var instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?
)

; ── MappedTypeData (slice 125) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `MappedType`, flattened: its embedded `ObjectType` gives the
; first four slots (the same four AnonymousTypeData carries) and its own eight
; follow. It is an arm of its own rather than a shared anonymous one because
; every one of those eight is a mapped type's alone — `AsMappedType()` upstream —
; and the port's rule for a downcast is that the arm answers null where the
; reference would panic.
;
; ★★ `declaration` is the MappedTypeNode, held as a plain `ref[AstNode]` the way
; every other declaration slot in this file is; the reference's typed
; `*ast.MappedTypeNode` buys nothing here because ast.scaly's accessors take a
; node and ask its kind.
;
; ★★ `contains_error` is written by get_type_of_mapped_symbol's circularity arm
; and read by nothing yet — it is the reference's own slot and the writer is
; ported, so it is carried rather than dropped: a slot with a writer and no
; reader is the shape §3.5be admits, where a slot with neither is not.
define MappedTypeData
(
    var resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    var target: ref[Type]?
    var mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    var instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?

    var declaration: ref[AstNode]?
    var type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    var constraint_type: ref[Type]?
    var name_type: ref[Type]?
    var template_type: ref[Type]?
    var modifiers_type: ref[Type]?
    var resolved_apparent_type: ref[Type]?
    var contains_error: bool
)

; ── ReverseMappedTypeData (slice 160) ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `ReverseMappedType`, flattened the way MappedTypeData is: the
; embedded `ObjectType` gives the first four slots and its own three follow. It
; is the type a HOMOMORPHIC mapped type is inferred BACKWARDS through — the
; source object whose properties still have to be run through the mapping — and
; its members are resolved lazily, one reverse inference per property.
;
; ★ `source`, `mapped_type` and `constraint_type` are never null on a type this
; port mints: create_reverse_mapped_type writes all three in the line after
; new_object_type, which is the reference's own order. They are declared nullable
; because the arm answers null where `AsReverseMappedType()` would panic.
define ReverseMappedTypeData
(
    var resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    target: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?

    var source: ref[Type]?
    var mapped_type: ref[Type]?
    var constraint_type: ref[Type]?
)

; ── the reverse-mapped memo rows (slice 160) ────────────────────────────────
;
; `c.reverseMappedCache` and `c.reverseHomomorphicMappedCache`, both keyed on the
; reference's `ReverseMappedTypeKey{sourceId, targetId, constraintId}`.
;
; ★★★ THE TWO TABLES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE AND THE DIFFERENCE IS `ok`. The
; homomorphic one is read as `if cached := …; cached != nil`, so a null result is
; recomputed; the other is read as `cached, ok := …` and a PRESENT null answers
; `unknownType`. That is why the lookup here returns the ROW and not the type:
; absence and a null result are two answers, and folding them would make a failed
; reverse inference retry on every property of every arrival.
define ReverseMappedRow
(
    source_id: int
    target_id: int
    constraint_id: int
    result: ref[Type]?
)

; ── the three closure STATES of the mapped type (slice 125) ─────────────────
;
; §3.9's answer applied three times. `resolveMappedTypeMembers` and
; `getIndexTypeForMappedType` each build a closure over half a dozen locals and
; hand it to `forEachType` and to
; `forEachMappedTypePropertyKeyTypeAndIndexSignatureKeyType`; a Scaly closure
; cannot escape into a callee, so what the closure captured becomes a record and
; what it DID becomes a method taking that record.
;
; ★★ THE TWO STATES ARE NOT ONE. They share `mapped`, `type_parameter` and
; `name_type` and nothing else: one accumulates SYMBOLS and index infos, the other
; accumulates KEY TYPES, and merging them would make every reader ask which half
; is live.
define MappedMemberState
(
    mapped: ref[Type]
    members: ref[SymbolTable]
    index_infos: ref[Array[ref[IndexInfo]?]]
    type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    template_type: ref[Type]?
    modifiers_type: ref[Type]?
    template_modifiers: int
    should_link_prop_declarations: bool
)

define MappedKeyState
(
    mapped: ref[Type]
    type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    keys: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    ; getIndexTypeForMappedType's callback widens a `string` key to
    ; `string | number`; getApparentMappedTypeKeys' callback does not (slice
    ; 202 — the two shared one state and `keyof { [K in keyof T as K & string]:
    ; … }` answered `string | number` against a target of `string`).
    widen_string: bool
)

; ── the map-over-a-union TAG and its captures (slice 125) ───────────────────
;
; §3.18's shape at the second site in this port. `mapType(t, f)` is called with
; three different closures in this chapter, each capturing something else — the
; mapped type and its homomorphic variable, a conditional root and its mapper, or
; nothing at all — so the mapping is a TAG plus the union of what they capture,
; and `apply_map_type_kind` is the one-line dispatcher.
;
; ★ The three callers: instantiate_mapped_type's `instantiateConstituent`,
; get_conditional_type_instantiation's distribution, and
; get_lower_bound_of_key_type's own recursion over a union.
define MapTypeKindLowerBoundOfKeyType: int 0
define MapTypeKindMappedConstituent: int 1
define MapTypeKindConditionalDistribution: int 2
; getTemplateLiteralType's own union arm (slice 126): it re-enters itself with ONE
; constituent substituted at `index`, so the three captures are the two lists and
; the position.
define MapTypeKindTemplateLiteralSpan: int 3
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralTypeForComparison's own union arm — it maps itself over the
; constituents and captures nothing, so it needs no MapTypeArgs at all.
define MapTypeKindBaseTypeForComparison: int 4
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralTypeUnion's mapper, which is get_base_type_of_literal_type
; itself. Captures nothing either.
define MapTypeKindBaseTypeOfLiteral: int 5
; extractDefinitelyFalsyTypes' mapper — getDefinitelyFalsyPartOfType, slice 135.
; Captures nothing.
define MapTypeKindDefinitelyFalsyPart: int 6
; getContextualTypeForElementExpression's mapper, slice 135 — the ARRAY LITERAL
; element's contextual type. Captures four INTEGERS (the element's index, the
; literal's length, and the first and last spread positions), which is why
; MapTypeArgs grew four int slots rather than reusing `index`: that one belongs to
; the template-literal span.
define MapTypeKindContextualElement: int 7
; getWidenedLiteralType's own union arm, slice 139 — it maps ITSELF over the
; constituents and captures nothing, exactly as the two base-type mappers above
; do. ★The stop it replaces read *mapType over getWidenedLiteralType, i.e.
; getUnionType again* — a note whose two premises (this dispatcher, and the union
; type) had both been in the file for a dozen slices (§3.5eo).
define MapTypeKindWidenedLiteral: int 8
; getMutableArrayOrTupleType's own union arm, slice 139. Captures nothing.
define MapTypeKindMutableArrayOrTuple: int 9

; getTupleElementTypeOutOfStartCount's distribution (slice 142) — two captures,
; the START INDEX as a jsnum bit pattern and the undefined-like type, which is
; nullable BY VALUE upstream (`core.IfElse(..., c.missingType, nil)`) and is the
; whole difference between including undefined and not.
define MapTypeKindTupleElementOutOfStartCount: int 10

; getRestType's own union arm (slice 145) — the OBJECT-REST recursion, which is
; the reference's `mapType(source, func(t) { return c.getRestType(t, properties,
; symbol) })`. Two captures, the omitted property NAMES and the symbol the
; anonymous result is named after.
define MapTypeKindRestType: int 11
; isValidSpreadType's head (slice 145) — `mapType(t, c.getBaseConstraintOrType)`.
; Captures nothing.
define MapTypeKindBaseConstraintOrType: int 13
; checkArrayLiteralDestructuringElementAssignment's rest arm (slice 145) —
; `mapType(sourceType, func(t) { return c.sliceTupleType(t, elementIndex, 0) })`.
; The captured index rides `index`, which is already a field.
define MapTypeKindSliceTuple: int 12
; checkObjectLiteral's SPREAD tail (slice 147) — *remap the raw emptyObjectType fed
; in at the top into a fresh empty object literal type, unique to this use site*,
; which is the reference's own comment. Its two captures are the LITERAL NODE and
; the accumulated state, because the callback calls `createObjectLiteralType()`.
define MapTypeKindEmptyObjectLiteralRemap: int 14

; MapTypeKindContextualIndexInfoValue (slice 151) — the closure of
; getContextualTypeForObjectLiteralElement's third arm, whose one capture is the
; NAME TYPE the index info is looked up with. ★The reference goes through
; mapTypeEx rather than getApplicableIndexInfo *"because it performs potentially
; expensive intersection reduction"*, which is a comment about cost and is
; transcribed as the shape it names.
define MapTypeKindContextualIndexInfoValue: int 15

; MapTypeKindThisTypeFromContextual (slice 158) — getThisTypeFromContextualType's
; closure, which captures nothing: it looks for a `ThisType<T>` in the constituent
; and answers its one type argument. ★The INTERSECTION arm returns the FIRST
; constituent that carries one and `nil` otherwise, so it is not a fold over the
; whole intersection — a `A & ThisType<X> & ThisType<Y>` answers X.
define MapTypeKindThisTypeFromContextual: int 16

; MapTypeKindJsxChildElement (slice 158) — getContextualTypeForChildJsxExpression's
; closure, whose one capture is the CHILD INDEX: an array-like `children` type is
; indexed at that position, anything else is the answer as it stands. ★It reuses
; MapTypeArgs' `index` field, which MapTypeKindTemplateLiteralSpan already carries
; — the two kinds never share a call.
define MapTypeKindJsxChildElement: int 17

; MapTypeKindSimplified (slice 166) — getSimplifiedIndexedAccessTypeWorker's
; mapped-object closure, whose one capture is the `writing` flag. ★It gets a
; field of its own rather than borrowing `for_constraint`: that one is the
; conditional distribution's `forConstraint` and the two kinds are asked from the
; same chapter, so a shared slot would read as one flag with two meanings.
define MapTypeKindSimplified: int 18

; MapTypeKindSpreadLeft / MapTypeKindSpreadRight (slice 170) — getSpreadType's two
; own distributions, `mapType(left, t => getSpreadType(t, right, …))` and its
; mirror. ★TWO kinds rather than one plus a side flag: the captured type is the
; OTHER operand, and which side that is decides the argument ORDER of the
; recursive call — a bit read at the call site would be a second thing to get
; right at every reader.
define MapTypeKindSpreadLeft: int 19
define MapTypeKindSpreadRight: int 20

; MapTypeKindStringMapping (slice 171) — getStringMappingType's own union arm,
; `mapType(t, func(t) { return c.getStringMappingType(symbol, t) })`. Its one
; capture is the intrinsic's SYMBOL, which is what decides which of the four
; mappings runs; it gets a slot of its own rather than borrowing `rest_symbol`
; or `spread_symbol` for the reason MapTypeKindSimplified did not borrow
; `for_constraint`.
define MapTypeKindStringMapping: int 21

; MapTypeKindConstraintFilter (slice 179) — getInferredType's return-type
; salvage: `mapType(inferredType, func(t) { return IfElse(compareTypes(t,
; constraintWithThis) != False, t, neverType) })`, i.e. every constituent that
; does not satisfy the instantiated constraint replaced by `never` so that the
; union reduction drops it. Its two captures are the CONSTRAINT and the
; inference context's COMPARER kind, and both get slots of their own: the
; comparer is an int rather than a func value for §3.18's reason, and the
; constraint cannot borrow `check_type` because that slot means the
; conditional's own check type at the arm two above.
define MapTypeKindConstraintFilter: int 22

; MapTypeKindInstantiationExpression (slice 181) — getInstantiationExpressionType's
; UNION arm, `c.mapType(t, getInstantiatedType)`. ★It re-enters the OUTER of the
; two closures, not the part-wise one, which is what gives every constituent a
; FRESH `hasSignatures`/`hasApplicableSignature` pair while the two
; `hasSomeApplicableSignature`/`nonApplicableType` slots stay shared. That
; asymmetry is the whole reason the state is split across two records here.
define MapTypeKindInstantiationExpression: int 23

; MapTypeKindNormalizedTupleVariadic (slice 201) — createNormalizedTupleTypeEx's
; own distribution, `[A, ...(X | Y | Z)]` into `[A, ...X] | [A, ...Y] | [A, ...Z]`.
; ★The three captures are the tuple TARGET, the element list and the INDEX of the
; variadic slot being distributed; the object flags ride along because the
; recursion is the same entry point. It cannot borrow `types` above — that slot
; is the template-literal span's — nor `index`, which the slice-tuple kind uses.
define MapTypeKindNormalizedTupleVariadic: int 24
; narrowTypeByTypeFacts' closure: `mapped` carries the implied type, `index` the facts.
; It goes through map_type on purpose — mapType walks a union's ORIGIN, so the enum
; `Color` narrowed out of `Color | string` comes back as the enum's own union object
; (slice 206; `TypeGuardWithEnumUnion`).
define MapTypeKindNarrowByTypeFacts: int 25
; getNarrowedTypeWorker's two inner closures over the CURRENT type: `mapped` carries the
; candidate constituent `n`, `for_constraint` carries checkDerived. Through map_type for the
; reason §3.5ik gives: mapType walks a union's ORIGIN, and `Array.isArray(expression)`
; narrows `Expression` back to its named member `BooleanLogicExpression` only that way.
define MapTypeKindPickMoreSpecific: int 26
define MapTypeKindIntersectIfGeneric: int 27

; IntrinsicTypeKind — the five names `type X<T> = intrinsic` is legal for. ★A
; TABLE upstream (`intrinsicTypeKinds`, a map from name to kind); five names
; here, for the reason type_alias_intrinsic_is_legal's header gives: the VALUE
; is read at four sites, all in the string-mapping chapter, and the fifth
; reader asks only for membership.
define IntrinsicTypeKindUnknown: int 0
define IntrinsicTypeKindUppercase: int 1
define IntrinsicTypeKindLowercase: int 2
define IntrinsicTypeKindCapitalize: int 3
define IntrinsicTypeKindUncapitalize: int 4
define IntrinsicTypeKindNoInfer: int 5

define MapTypeArgs
(
    mapped: ref[Type]?
    type_variable: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    root: ref[ConditionalRoot]?
    check_type: ref[Type]?
    for_constraint: bool

    ; MapTypeKindTemplateLiteralSpan's three (slice 126).
    texts: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]?
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    index: int

    ; MapTypeKindContextualElement's four (slice 135).
    element_index: int
    element_length: int
    first_spread_index: int
    last_spread_index: int

    ; MapTypeKindTupleElementOutOfStartCount's two (slice 142).
    start_index: u64
    undefined_like: ref[Type]?

    ; MapTypeKindRestType's two (slice 145). MapTypeKindSliceTuple adds none —
    ; its one capture is `index` above.
    rest_properties: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    rest_symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; MapTypeKindEmptyObjectLiteralRemap's two (slice 147).
    objlit_node: ref[AstNode]?
    objlit_state: ref[ObjectLiteralState]?

    ; MapTypeKindContextualIndexInfoValue's one (slice 151).
    key_name_type: ref[Type]?

    ; MapTypeKindSimplified's one (slice 166).
    simplify_writing: bool

    ; MapTypeKindSpreadLeft / MapTypeKindSpreadRight's four (slice 170) — the
    ; OTHER operand of the spread plus the three arguments getSpreadType carries
    ; unchanged through its recursion.
    spread_other: ref[Type]?
    spread_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    spread_object_flags: int
    spread_readonly: bool

    ; MapTypeKindStringMapping's one (slice 171) — the intrinsic's symbol.
    string_mapping_symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; MapTypeKindConstraintFilter's two (slice 179).
    filter_constraint: ref[Type]?
    filter_comparer: int

    ; MapTypeKindInstantiationExpression's two (slice 181).
    inst_expr_node: ref[AstNode]?
    inst_expr_state: ref[InstantiationExpressionState]?

    ; MapTypeKindNormalizedTupleVariadic's four (slice 201).
    tuple_target: ref[Type]?
    tuple_element_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    tuple_variadic_index: int
    tuple_object_flags: int
)

; getInstantiationExpressionType's SHARED captures — the two the reference's two
; nested closures both write. §3.21's shape.
define InstantiationExpressionState
(
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    var has_some_applicable: bool
    var non_applicable: ref[Type]?
)

; …and its PER-CALL captures: `hasSignatures` and `hasApplicableSignature` are
; declared inside getInstantiatedType, so each constituent of a union gets its
; own pair while the record above is threaded through unchanged.
define InstantiationExpressionPart
(
    var has_signatures: bool
    var has_applicable_signature: bool
)

; addSpans' captured state (slice 126) — §3.21. The builder is a SLOT because the
; reference resets it when a span opens and a StringBuilder here has none: a fresh
; builder is put in its place instead.
define TemplateSpanState
(
    var sb: ref[StringBuilder]
    new_texts: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]
    new_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
)

; inferFromLiteralPartsToTemplateLiteral's captured state (slice 126) — §3.21
; again, and this one is the reason that rule exists: `addMatch` captures THREE
; mutable locals and is called from four places in one loop.
define TemplateMatchState
(
    var seg: int
    var pos: int
    matches: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
)

; ── ConditionalInstantiation (slice 125) ────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `ConditionalRoot.instantiations`, whose upstream key is
; getConditionalTypeKey(typeArguments, alias, forConstraint). An ENTRY for
; ObjectInstantiation's reason.
define ConditionalInstantiation
(
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    alias_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    for_constraint: bool
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
    hash: int
)

; ── ObjectInstantiation (slice 125) ─────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `ObjectType.instantiations`, whose upstream key is
; getTypeInstantiationKey(typeArguments, alias, singleSignature). It is an ENTRY
; rather than a bare type for §3.16's reason: two thirds of the key are not on
; the answer — a mapped type's instantiation may be a UNION (the homomorphic
; distribution), which carries neither the argument list nor the alias the key
; was built from.
define ObjectInstantiation
(
    hash: int
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    alias_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    single_signature: bool
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
)

; InterfaceType, flattened. The first three slots are the embedded ObjectType's
; and TypeReference's; the last three are InterfaceType's own.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, each with its reader: `mapper` (instantiateType) and `node`
; (getTypeArguments' deferred path, which is unreachable while
; resolved_type_arguments is set at construction).
;
; ★★ THE FIVE `declared*` SLOTS THIS LINE USED TO NAME ARRIVED IN SLICE 85, and
; the deferral had named its own expiry date — *"resolveDeclaredMembers, behind
; checkIndexConstraints"* — exactly as slice 66's base-type deferral did one slice
; earlier. They are ONE reference here rather than five slots (see
; DeclaredMembers), and `resolved` beside it is the embedded StructuredType's.
;
; ★★ THE THREE BASE SLOTS BELOW ARRIVED IN SLICE 67, AND SLICE 66 HAD NAMED THIS
; SLICE AS THEIR READER. The line above used to carry `baseTypesResolved` /
; `resolvedBaseTypes` / `resolvedBaseConstructorType` with the reader
; "getBaseTypes, which is where BOTH call sites of this slice now stop" — so the
; deferral names its own expiry date, and this is it. A limit that is fixed and
; still written down as a limit is worse than no note at all.
define InterfaceTypeData
(
    var target: ref[Type]?
    var resolved_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    var instantiations: ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]?
    var instantiation_index: ref[InstantiationIndex]?

    ; allTypeParameters is outer + local + thisType, in that order, and the two
    ; counts below are what slice the three apart — which is why
    ; outer_type_parameter_count is carried even though only TypeParameters()
    ; reads the array in this slice.
    var all_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    var outer_type_parameter_count: int
    var this_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getBaseTypes' memo and its two answers. `base_types_resolved` is a BIT
    ; rather than "the list is non-null", because the reference's answer for an
    ; interface with no `extends` is an EMPTY list and asking again is what the
    ; bit prevents — a null list would make the memo miss for exactly the 97 % of
    ; the corpus that has no base type at all.
    var base_types_resolved: bool
    var resolved_base_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass' memo. `undefined_type` is a real answer here
    ; (the class has no `extends`), which is why the reference tests it against
    ; nil rather than keeping a second bit.
    var resolved_base_constructor_type: ref[Type]?

    ; resolveDeclaredMembers' memo and the embedded StructuredType, slice 85.
    var declared: ref[DeclaredMembers]?
    var resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?

    ; ★★★ THE FIVE TUPLE SLOTS, SLICE 115, AND THEY ARE HERE BECAUSE THE
    ; REFERENCE'S STRUCT PUTS THEM HERE: `type TupleType struct { InterfaceType;
    ; elementInfos; minLength; fixedLength; combinedFlags; readonly }` EMBEDS the
    ; interface type, so a tuple target's field list is this one followed by these
    ; five. §3.5n's rule is *read the reference's struct*, and the reference's
    ; answer is an extension rather than a sibling.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ALTERNATIVE COSTS TWELVE ACCESSORS AND EACH MISS IS A SILENT NULL. A
    ; separate `TupleTypeData` arm would need a Tuple case added to `this_type_of`,
    ; `target_of`, `resolved_type_arguments_of`, `base_types_resolved_of`,
    ; `resolved_base_types_of`, `resolved_base_constructor_type_of`,
    ; `mark_base_types_resolved`, `set_resolved_base_types`,
    ; `set_resolved_base_constructor_type`, `type_parameter_count_of`,
    ; `all_type_parameters_of`, `declared_members_of` and `attach_declared_members`
    ; — and each of those answers null or false for an arm it does not name, which
    ; is the port's own choice for a downcast the reference would panic on. Sharing
    ; the arm makes every one of them correct for a tuple BY CONSTRUCTION, which is
    ; exactly the property the reference's embedding has.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PRICE IS THAT AN ORDINARY INTERFACE CARRIES FIVE DEAD SLOTS, so the
    ; DISCRIMINATOR is the FLAG and never the payload: every reader below asks
    ; `object_flags & ObjectFlagsTuple` first, the same question the reference's
    ; `AsTupleType()` is guarded by at each of its call sites. A reader that asks
    ; the payload arm alone gets an interface's zeros and cannot tell them from a
    ; zero-arity tuple's.
    var element_infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]]?
    var min_length: int
    var fixed_length: int
    var combined_flags: int
    var readonly: bool
)
{
    init() linked






















}

; TypeReference — createTypeReferenceEx's product. A SEPARATE record rather than a
; shared one, on §3.5n's rule: the slot lists are not identical, and an arm that
; borrowed InterfaceTypeData would carry three slots whose absence of a writer is
; exactly what tells a reader it is not an interface.
;
; ★★★ IT WAS CALLED `TypeReferenceData` UNTIL SLICE 86 AND THAT NAME IS TAKEN BY
; ast.scaly — see TypeParameterTypeData's header for what the collision costs. This
; one was HARMLESS by luck (both records are `{ptr, ptr}`, so the collapsed LLVM
; type fits either) and it is renamed anyway, because the two facts a reader needs
; are that the name is taken and that a shape agreeing today is not a rule.
; ★★★ THE `resolved` SLOT IS SLICE 96'S AND IT WAS FOUND BY AN exit 21 AT STAGE 2.
; Upstream every object type embeds `StructuredType`, so a TYPE REFERENCE holds a
; resolved-members table exactly as an interface does; here the arms were split and
; only two of them had the slot. Nothing had ever asked a REFERENCE for its members
; — `resolve_type_reference_members` existed, but every caller of
; resolveStructuredTypeMembers stood behind a guard that never sent one — so
; `structured_members_of` answered null for `B<string>`, set_structured_type_members
; unwrapped that null, and the process exited 21 with no source location.
; ★★It was invisible at stage 1 and on all seven fixtures: a GENERIC interface
; accessed through a property is the shape, and the corpus has it six times in
; 18 264 units. **A slice whose chapter reaches a new kind of type owes a stage-2
; run, and this is the entry that says why.**
define ReferenceTypeData
(
    var target: ref[Type]?
    var resolved_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    var resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?

    ; ★★★ THE DEFERRED PAIR, SLICE 115. createDeferredTypeReference builds a
    ; reference whose type ARGUMENTS are not resolved yet and hangs the type NODE on
    ; it; getTypeArguments' second branch reads the node back and resolves them.
    ; They arrive together because a `node` without that branch is a slot nothing
    ; can answer from — which is exactly what the slot's absence used to mean.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND `type A = string[]` IS THE SHAPE THAT FORCED THEM. Every array or
    ; tuple type node written as the right-hand side of a type ALIAS is deferred by
    ; isDeferredTypeReferenceNode's FIRST disjunct (getAliasSymbolForTypeNode names
    ; it), so without this pair the whole alias-of-array family stopped — measured
    ; on slice 115's own array fixture, whose first line is one.
    ;
    ; ★ `mapper` is the deferred reference's own (create_deferred_type_reference_ex
    ; receives it from get_object_type_instantiation); the note that once called it
    ; nil for every reference expired with the deferred instantiation arm.
    var node: ref[AstNode]?
    var mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; ★ `ObjectType.instantiations` — EVERY object kind carries it upstream, a DEFERRED
    ; type reference included: getObjectTypeInstantiation keys the instantiations of a
    ; deferred reference's node on `links.resolvedType`, which IS a Reference. Without
    ; the slot every ask missed, built a fresh deferred reference with a fresh mapper,
    ; and the recursive units instantiated four to five times what the reference does
    ; (`recursiveIndexedAccessSimplification`: TS2589 36× against 11×; slice 215).
    var instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?
)
{
    init(tg: ref[Type]?, args: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?) linked








}

; TypeParameter. `constraint` is written by getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface for
; the `this` type and, since slice 81, by getConstraintFromTypeParameter — which is
; also its first reader; `is_this_type` is IsThisType()'s slot.
;
; ★★ THE LAST TWO SLOTS ARE THE REFERENCE'S ConstrainedType AND TypeParameter
; MEMOS, and the first of them sits one level too low here on purpose:
; `resolvedBaseConstraint` belongs to ConstrainedType, which upstream is embedded by
; EIGHT type kinds (StructuredType — so every object, union and intersection type —
; plus TypeParameter, IndexType, IndexedAccessType, TemplateLiteralType,
; StringMappingType, SubstitutionType and ConditionalType). Seven of those eight
; cannot be MADE by this port (see the base-constraint chapter's header), and the
; eighth is this arm — so a shared slot would have to live on `Type` itself, which
; is a bigger claim than the one reader needs. is_constrained_type is where the
; distinction is written down, and it is the function to move when the second kind
; arrives.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, with readers: `target` and `mapper` (instantiateType). The
; `resolvedDefaultType` this note used to name among them arrived with slice 81.
;
; ★★★ IT WAS CALLED `TypeParameterData` UNTIL SLICE 86, WHICH IS THE NAME
; ast.scaly'S TypeParameterDeclaration PAYLOAD ALREADY HAD — AND THE COLLISION WAS
; SILENT, LOSSY AND FOUR SLICES OLD. A top-level `define` mangles WITHOUT its
; module (`_Z17TypeParameterData`), so the emitter's named-struct table keeps ONE
; of the two: the AST record, `{ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr}`. Under that type
; `mark_this_type`'s `store i1 true` went into the low byte of a POINTER slot, and
; `is_this_type_of`'s `return tp.is_this_type` extracted a `ptr` where an `i1` was
; wanted and was DROPPED — the emitted arm computes the value, branches to the tail
; and returns the literal `false`. **So the predicate answered false for every type
; in the tree, and nothing said a word.**
;
; ★★★ WHAT MADE IT SURVIVE IS THAT IT HAD ALMOST NO READER: `is_this_type_of`'s one
; caller before slice 86 is computeBaseConstraint's early exit, where a wrong
; `false` costs one extra recursion step and no visible answer. Slice 86's
; `MapsThisOnly` is the first reader that DECIDES something, and it decided wrong on
; every class in the corpus — every member minted, none of them by the reference's
; rule. §3.5be says a field with no reader is a claim about the future; **this is
; the sharper form: a field with no reader is not even known to WORK.**
;
; ★ The gate is `tests/run.sh`'s duplicate-`define` scan, added in the same commit.
; The compiler cannot see this today: two modules of one package may declare the
; same top-level concept and nothing is reported.
define TypeParameterTypeData
(
    var constraint: ref[Type]?
    var is_this_type: bool
    var resolved_default_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ★★★ THE CLONE PAIR, SLICE 125. `target` and `mapper` are the reference's own
    ; slots and they are what makes a CLONED type parameter answer a constraint at
    ; all: instantiateAnonymousType's mapped arm mints a fresh parameter for the
    ; mapped type's `P`, and getConstraintFromTypeParameter's FIRST branch is
    ; `if tp.target != nil` — instantiate the ORIGINAL's constraint through the
    ; clone's mapper. Without them that branch cannot be written, and a clone
    ; answers *no constraint* for a `[P in keyof T]` whose constraint is the whole
    ; point.
    var target: ref[Type]?
    var mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
)

; LiteralType — the payload of every string, number, bigint and boolean literal
; type, slice 71.
;
; ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S `value` IS A GO `any` AND IT IS THREE SLOTS HERE, NOT A
; FOUR-ARM UNION, and the precedent is this file's own: TypeResolution's
; `TypeSystemEntity` is likewise an `any` held as slots (see its note). The
; argument is the same and it is stronger here — the reference never *asks* the
; value what it is. Its four readers are `getStringLiteralValue`,
; `getNumberLiteralValue`, `getBigIntLiteralValue` and `getBooleanLiteralValue`,
; each an unchecked type ASSERTION whose correctness is the type's own FLAG. So
; the discriminator is `Type.flags`, exactly as upstream, and a second one in the
; payload would be a thing to keep in step for no reader.
;
; ★ `text` is BORROWED, never copied: every value that reaches here is an
; `AstNode.literal_text_of`, i.e. a run of bytes on the page the parse owns, and
; the checker does not outlive the parse. The bigint arm is the one exception in
; SPIRIT rather than in kind — its digits are computed by ParsePseudoBigInt into a
; buffer this file allocates on the checker's own page.
;
; ★★ `regular_type` and `fresh_type` are the reference's own two slots and they
; are what makes a literal type a PAIR. The regular type is created first and
; points at itself; the fresh type is minted lazily by getFreshTypeOfLiteralType,
; points BACK at the regular one, and points at itself as its own fresh type.
; Both booleans break the pattern by being built eagerly and in the other order
; (see initialize_intrinsic_types).
;
; ★★★ `regular_type` STILL HAS NO READER AND `fresh_type` HAS TWO — and slice 71
; wrote that pair down WRONG BY ONE, which slice 72 is what made visible. That
; slice said `regular_type` is read by getRegularTypeOfLiteralType AND
; isFreshLiteralType. It is not: isFreshLiteralType is
; `t.flags&Freshable != 0 && t.AsLiteralType().freshType == t` and reads the FRESH
; slot. ★A slot's reader list is the kind of claim that reads as checked and is
; not, so it is corrected here rather than left to be re-derived.
;
; ★★ SO THE DOOR SLICE 71 NAMED OPENED FOR HALF OF WHAT WAS BEHIND IT.
; getWidenedLiteralType is ported (slice 72) and asks isFreshLiteralType five
; times, which gives `fresh_type` its second reader beside
; getFreshTypeOfLiteralType. getRegularTypeOfLiteralType is STILL not ported, and
; its argument is unchanged: its only callers are getRegularTypeOfExpression and
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralType, its UNION arm carries a `record_unported`, and a
; work-list row in a function nothing calls is a row that can never appear — the
; product of these slices IS the work list, so a phantom entry in it is worse
; than a missing function.
define LiteralTypeData
(
    ; StringLiteral: the cooked text of the literal. BigIntLiteral:
    ; PseudoBigInt.Base10Value — the absolute value in base 10, no leading zeros,
    ; and EMPTY for zero, which is the reference's own representation and the
    ; reason pseudo_big_int_to_string has a length test rather than a value test.
    text: Slice[char]
    ; NumberLiteral: the IEEE-754 bits of the jsnum.Number.
    bits: u64
    ; BigIntLiteral: PseudoBigInt.Negative.
    negative: bool
    ; BooleanLiteral: the value.
    bool_value: bool
    var regular_type: ref[Type]?
    var fresh_type: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(t: Slice[char], b: u64, neg: bool, bv: bool) linked








}

; ★★★ THE ORDER-OF-DECLARATION AUFLAGE that ast.scaly's NodeData carries applies
; here too and is stated now rather than when it bites: the tag IS the jump-table
; index, and AArch64 needs ≥14 contiguous tags per dispatch level. Five arms are
; far below that, so every `choose` over this union lowers to a compare chain
; today — which is correct and cheap at five. The arm that makes the union big
; enough to care is the one that must re-read the root CLAUDE.md's `Insn` note.
; One row of c.declaredTypeLinks. `interface_checked` is checkInterfaceDeclaration's
; own once-bit and lives here because the reference keeps it in the same links
; record — two questions about one symbol, asked from one place.
;
; ★★★ `index_signatures_checked` ARRIVES IN SLICE 77 AND RETIRES A NOTE SLICE 73
; LEFT STANDING. That slice called the enum's `enumChecked` bit *"a PER-SYMBOL side
; table this port has none of"* — true when it was written and false by the time
; slice 77 read it, because this table landed for `declared_type` in between and
; already carries two once-bits. **A blocker recorded as a property of the port
; rather than of a slice does not expire on its own**; the enum row is still open,
; but not for the reason written down.
define DeclaredTypeLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    var declared_type: ref[Type]?
    var interface_checked: bool

    ; links.indexSignaturesChecked — checkClassOrInterfaceForDuplicateIndexSignatures'
    ; ONLY-CHECK-ONCE bit. It is a REPORT memo and not a work memo, which is what
    ; makes it load-bearing rather than an optimisation: a merged interface has two
    ; declarations, both arms call the function, and diagcheck's relation is a
    ; SUBSEQUENCE — so a second visit's duplicate line is a failure. Same argument
    ; as ValueSymbolLink.function_or_constructor_checked, one dimension over.
    var index_signatures_checked: bool

    ; links.typeParametersChecked — checkTypeParameterListsIdentical's
    ; ONLY-CHECK-ONCE bit, the third of exactly the same shape as the two above and
    ; load-bearing for the same reason: the function is called once per class-like
    ; DECLARATION and a merged interface has several, so without the memo TS2428
    ; would be reported once per declaration per declaration.
    var type_parameters_checked: bool

    ; ★★★ `data.resolvedBaseConstructorType` AS A REPORT MEMO — slice 104, and the
    ; defect it closes is one only STAGE 2 could see. Upstream the field holds the
    ; base constructor TYPE, and its first job is the one every memo in this record
    ; has: `getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass` is called from six places here, and the
    ; second caller must not re-run `checkExpression(baseTypeNode.Expression())`.
    ; This port cannot hold the type — the line after that call is the
    ; `is-constructor-type` stop — so it holds the BIT, exactly as
    ; index_signatures_checked and ValueSymbolLink.function_or_constructor_checked
    ; do, and for the same reason: diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a
    ; second visit's duplicate line is a failure.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS KEYED ON THE SYMBOL WHERE UPSTREAM'S IS KEYED ON THE TYPE, which is
    ; the same key for a class: the type is `getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol(symbol)` and
    ; this record is that memo's own row.
    base_constructor_checked: bool

    ; ★★★ `links.enumChecked` — checkEnumDeclaration's per-SYMBOL once-bit, and it
    ; arrives in slice 116 to close a note this record's own header names. The two
    ; reports it gates (TS2473, TS2432) are about a MERGED enum, so the question is
    ; *has this symbol been checked* and not *is this the first declaration*; the
    ; merged-declaration head one screen up needs no table because its once-guard
    ; is a node identity test (GetDeclarationOfKind), which is exactly the
    ; difference.
    var enum_checked: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?) linked









}

; One row of c.flowTypeCache (slice 120) — see get_type_of_expression's header
; for why this memo is OBSERVABLE and not an optimisation.
define FlowTypeCacheLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

