; One row of c.contextFreeTypes (slice 141) — FlowTypeCacheLink's shape at a
; second per-node memo.
;
; ★★★ TWO READERS ASK IT DIFFERENTLY UPSTREAM and this port collapses them, on
; the reading get_type_of_expression's cache already established:
; getContextFreeTypeOfExpression asks `cached != nil` while
; checkFunctionExpressionOrObjectLiteralMethod asks `cached, ok :=` — PRESENCE —
; and the difference would matter only for a row holding nil. Here a null answer
; means the check STOPPED, and a cached stop would answer a later ask without
; reporting one, so nothing null is ever put in and the two questions have one
; answer.
define ContextFreeTypeLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── the FLOW REFERENCE KEY's part, slice 141 ────────────────────────────────
;
; `getFlowReferenceKey` + `writeFlowCacheKey` build the key of the flow-loop
; cache, and the reference's own comment says what it is: *the id of the symbol
; referenced by the leftmost identifier followed by zero or more property names
; separated by dots. The result is nonDottedNameCacheKey if the reference isn't a
; dotted name.*
;
; ★★★ THE KEY IS upstream's: the parts are written into a byte buffer in the
; order `writeFlowCacheKey` writes them and the 128-bit xxh3 of the buffer is the
; key (`keyBuilder.hash`, `Xxh3.hash128` being the port of the same Sum128). A
; symbol, a type and a node are written as their ADDRESS — the pointer is the
; identity upstream's ids stand for, and unlike an id it needs no counter.
;
; ★ Until slice 353 the port kept the PARTS as records and compared them one by
; one, on two arguments that have both expired: a symbol id was unique only per
; FILE (§3.10) — an address has no such boundary — and a structural comparison
; cannot collide where a hash can, which is the risk upstream accepts at 128 bits.
; The parts cost ~300 bytes a key (a 64-byte record per part plus its list) where
; the hash costs 16: 86 MB of the checker's region over the whole of VS Code. The
; buffer is the key-building function's own and goes back with its frame; a text
; part is consumed as it is written, so no key holds a view of anybody's bytes.

; One row of c.flowLoopCache — the reference's `FlowLoopKey{flowNode, refKey}`
; against the type computed for it. ★A row is written only where the loop
; junction COMPLETED; an in-process junction lives on the stack below instead.
define FlowLoopCacheLink
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    key: ref[Xxh3]?
    t: ref[Type]?
    ; the next row of the SAME flow node, in insertion order
    var next_same_flow: ref[FlowLoopCacheLink]?
)

; c.flowLoopStack — the junctions currently being analyzed, with the antecedent
; types gathered so far.
;
; ★★★ IT IS A CONS LIST FOR ReduceLabelStack's REASON — upstream pushes before a
; recursion and truncates after it, and `Array` here has `add` and `clear` and
; nothing between them. A cons list pushes by minting a head and pops by USING
; THE OLD HEAD in the continuation.
;
; ★★★ AND `types` IS SHARED WITH THE CALLER'S LIST ON PURPOSE, which is the one
; place §3.5fs's rule does not apply: upstream pushes the SLICE
; `antecedentTypes`, keeps appending to the caller's variable, and the append
; happens only BETWEEN recursions — so during the recursion that reads the stack
; the length is fixed and a shared reference is exactly the snapshot Go took.
define FlowLoopInfo
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    key: ref[Xxh3]?
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    next: ref[FlowLoopInfo]?
)

