; THREE rows of c.cachedTypes on ONE record (slice 151) —
; CachedTypeKindPermissiveInstantiation, CachedTypeKindRestrictiveInstantiation and
; CachedTypeKindRestrictiveTypeParameter, all keyed on the same type id.
;
; ★★★ UPSTREAM'S KEY IS (kind, typeId) OVER ONE MAP, SO THREE KINDS ON ONE ROW IS
; THE SAME ASSOCIATION AND NOT A MERGE. WidenedTypeLink's idiom keeps one table per
; kind because each kind's table has a different reader; these three are read by
; one chapter, the scan is linear, and three scans of one list is three times the
; work for an identical answer. ★The slots are disjoint by construction anyway: a
; type parameter is the only thing that ever fills the third, and a type parameter's
; permissive instantiation is the wildcard rather than a row.
;
; ★★★ THE RESTRICTIVE SLOT IS WRITTEN TWICE PER ASK AND THE SECOND WRITE IS THE
; OBSERVABLE ONE. Upstream sets `cachedTypes[{RestrictiveInstantiation, result.id}]
; = result` after caching the answer, so a restrictive instance IS its own
; restrictive instantiation — and its own comment says what that row is FOR: it is
; how a comparison DETECTS a restrictive instance and disables the distributive
; constraint check for it, which would otherwise be *distinctly unsafe*. The reader
; is get_constraint_of_distributive_conditional_type, whose second disjunct asks
; exactly that row.
define InstantiationCacheLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    var permissive: ref[Type]?
    var restrictive: ref[Type]?
    var restrictive_type_parameter: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?) linked






}

; TWO rows of c.cachedTypes on ONE record (slice 166) —
; CachedTypeKindIndexedAccessForReading and CachedTypeKindIndexedAccessForWriting,
; both keyed on the same type id, which is InstantiationCacheLink's argument at a
; smaller key: upstream reads the pair out of one map under two kinds, and one
; linear scan answering both is the same association rather than a merge.
;
; ★★★ THE MEMO IS OBSERVABLE AND IT IS ALSO THE RECURSION BRAKE. The reference
; writes `t` into the slot BEFORE calling the worker and only overwrites it when
; the worker answered something else, so a simplification that re-enters itself
; on the same type sees the identity and terminates — `{ [P in T]: { [Q in U]:
; number } }[T][U]` is the reference's own example of a nested access and the
; worker simplifies the inner one first.
;
; ★★★ AND THE SLOT IS CLEARED WHEN THE WORKER STOPS, which is a deliberate
; divergence and the only one in this chapter. Upstream a worker always answers,
; so the in-progress `t` either stays (nothing simplified) or is overwritten; here
; a stop answers null and would LEAVE the `t` behind — and the next ask of that
; same type would then read the identity out of the cache and record no stop at
; all. That is a silent wrong answer standing in for a wall, which is the one
; thing this port may not trade a memo for.
; One row of the relation's result cache: the reference's
; `map[CacheHashKey]RelationComparisonResult`, one map per relation, as ONE table
; whose key carries the relation. `key` is get_relation_key's hash; the relation is
; one of five ids and the result a bit set below 1 << 7, so the row is 16 bytes.
; It was six ints (48 bytes) with a stored hash and a source/target/state triple
; that every writer had filled with (key, 0, 0) since slice 216 — 126 MB of the
; checker's region over the whole of VS Code (slice 352, the census).
define RelationCacheRow
(
    key: int
    rel: u8
    result: u8
)

define SimplifiedIndexedAccessLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    var for_reading: ref[Type]?
    var for_writing: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?) linked





}

