; checker.WideningContext, whole since slice 154.
;
; ★★★ IT STOOD AS ONE SLOT AND FOUR NAMES, AND THE NOTE WAS RIGHT ON THE DAY.
; It read *four of its five slots are named rather than declared … `parent`,
; `propertyName`, `resolvedProperties`, `childContexts` and the `widenedTypes` memo
; are read by getSiblingsOfContext, getPropertiesOfContext and getChildContext —
; all three behind ONE stop in get_widened_type_of_object_literal — so a slot for
; them is a slot no instrument in this suite can reach*. That stop was the
; frontier's fourth row when this slice opened (+41 units over 55 arrivals), which
; is what a §3.5be name costs when the thing it defers is a chapter and not a line.
;
; ★★ THE TWO MAPS ARE LISTS, and the population is why rather than laziness:
; `childContexts` is keyed by the property names of ONE object literal and
; `widenedTypes` by the types widened under ONE child context, so both are a
; handful of entries and a linear scan is the whole implementation — the same
; shape TypeInstantiation's list already takes for the reference's hashed map.
; ★`widened_types` is written only in a context that HAS a parent, which is the
; reference's own condition and not a size argument: the root context never widens
; one object literal type twice.
define WideningChild
(
