    ; getStringLiteralType. The table is scanned linearly and compared on the
    ; BYTES, which is what a Go map keyed on a string compares.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MINT COPIES THE TEXT ONTO THE CHECKER'S PAGE, AND THAT IS A FIX AND
    ; NOT A PRECAUTION. Upstream's key and value are a Go STRING, an immutable
    ; value; here `text` is a borrowed `Slice[char]` and the row kept it, so a
    ; caller whose bytes were a stack buffer or a frame that later died left an
    ; INTERNED type pointing at dead memory — permanently, and for every later ask
    ; with the same bytes, because the scan above hands that row back. It reads as
    ; a wrong ANSWER rather than a crash: `destructuringControlFlow` printed
    ; `"\uFFFD"` where the reference prints `"x"`, and it printed it only in the
    ; whole unit, never in the five lines around the site, because what the slice
    ; points at is whatever the arena put there next. Slice 195 met the same class
    ; one table over (`add_member_for_key_type_worker`) and fixed it at that ONE
    ; caller; this is the same fix at the table, where it covers all of them.
    ; The copy is on the MINT path only — one per distinct literal.
