    ; ── slice 134: THE TYPE IMPLIED BY A BINDING PATTERN ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A TYPE NOBODY WROTE. `const { a, b = 1 } = x` and `const [p, q] =
    ; y` name no type at all, and the checker manufactures one from the PATTERN —
    ; an anonymous object type for the object form, a TUPLE for the array form —
    ; so that a declaration with neither an annotation nor an inferable initializer
    ; still has something to be. Two callers ask for it and they ask different
    ; questions: widenTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration wants it as the declaration's
    ; own type and REPORTS the implicit `any`s it finds on the way, while
    ; getContextualTypeForInitializerExpression wants it as a CONTEXT for the
    ; initializer and reports nothing — which is what the two booleans are for.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `includePatternInType` IS THE ONE THAT CHANGES THE ANSWER AND IT DOES SO
    ; THREE WAYS: it pushes the pattern on c.contextualBindingPatterns (which is
    ; what makes `const [a, b = a + 1] = [2]` answer nonInferrableAny for the `a`
    ; inside the default rather than recursing), it registers the result in
    ; c.patternForType, and for the array form it CLONES the tuple reference first,
    ; because a pattern may not be attached to a type object other code shares.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CONTEXTUAL-BINDING-PATTERN STACK LANDS WITH THIS SLICE AND NOT LATER,
    ; and check_identifier is where it is read. That early-out has stood in this
    ; file as a containment proof — *"the day that row lands, this branch comes
    ; with it"* — and this is that day: a stack with a writer and no reader would
    ; be a silently wrong answer on exactly the fixture the reference wrote the
    ; branch for.

    ; getTypeFromBindingPattern.
    procedure get_type_from_binding_pattern(mutable this, mutable pattern: ref[AstNode], include_pattern_in_type: bool, report_errors: bool) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked
















