    ; ── the tuple TARGET ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ A TUPLE TYPE IS A REFERENCE TO A SYNTHESIZED GENERIC INTERFACE, and the
    ; reference's own comment is the whole design:
    ;
    ;     interface Tuple<T0, T1, T2, ...> extends Array<T0 | T1 | T2 | ...>
    ;         { 0: T0, 1: T1, 2: T2, ... }
    ;
    ; So `[string, number]` is `Tuple<string, number>` over a target minted here,
    ; and the elements are TYPE ARGUMENTS. Nothing about it carries a symbol —
    ; neither the interface nor any of its type parameters — which is what makes it
    ; a factory rather than a declaration.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND IT IS WHY `resolve_declared_members` MUST NOT RUN ON ONE. That
    ; function's second line is `this.get_members_of_symbol(t.symbol as ref[Symbol])`
    ; — an unwrap, so exit 21 on a symbol-less type. The reference sidesteps it by
    ; setting `declaredMembersResolved = true` before anyone can ask; here the
    ; record's PRESENCE is that bit (DeclaredMembers' own header), so the factory
    ; attaches it and the unwrap is never reached. **A type with no symbol that
    ; reaches the declared-members path is an exit 21 with no source location.**
