    ; is on the ACCUMULATED flags rather than on this element's: once a rest or a
    ; variadic element has been seen, no later element has a fixed INDEX, so it gets
    ; no property. `fixed_length` is then literally the member count at that point,
    ; which is why the reference reads it off the table instead of counting again.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `length` IS THE ONE MEMBER EVERY TUPLE HAS AND ITS TYPE IS THE ARITY
    ; INFORMATION. With no variable element it is the union of the number literals
    ; minLength..arity — so `[string, number?]` answers `1 | 2` — and with one it is
    ; plain `number`. That union is the first thing in this chapter that needs
    ; get_union_type, and it is why the literal-type chapter (slice 71) had to come
    ; first.
    procedure create_tuple_target_type(mutable this, infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]], readonly: bool) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked

























































































    ; ── getTypeFromArrayOrTupleTypeNode ─────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FUNCTION IS A FOUR-WAY FORK AND ONLY ONE ARM BUILDS ANYTHING. The
    ; target is `Array<T>`, `ReadonlyArray<T>` or a synthesized tuple interface;
    ; the empty-generic answer is the reference's own sentinel path (a unit with no
    ; `Array` in scope), the DEFERRED arm is a stop, and the last arm resolves the
    ; element types and makes the reference.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DEFERRED ARM STOPS BECAUSE ReferenceTypeData CARRIES NEITHER `mapper`
    ; NOR `node`, and that is not a slot to add on the way past:
    ; createDeferredTypeReference's whole point is that the type arguments are
    ; resolved LATER, off the node, which is exactly the branch
    ; `get_type_arguments` already reports as `get-type-arguments-deferred`. Adding
    ; the two slots without that reader would be a type whose arguments nothing can
    ; ever answer. ★Its guard is narrow: the node must sit inside a type ALIAS
    ; declaration AND its elements must be able to resolve one, so `type A =
    ; string[]` is NOT deferred and `type A = B[]` with `type B = …` is.
    ;
    ; ★ The zero-element tuple inside the deferred arm answers the TARGET itself,
    ; which is `[]` — a tuple interface of arity zero, whose only member is
    ; `length: 0`.
    procedure get_type_from_array_or_tuple_type_node(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked




























































































