    ; ── slice 95: the type reference ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HEAD OF THE WORK LIST BY BOTH MEASURES, AND IT IS A ROW WITH TWO
    ; PRODUCERS. `get-type-from-type-node 184` is 1 773 events over 190 units at
    ; stage 1 and it is NOT one arm: 1 253 of those events are
    ; `check_type_reference_or_import`, i.e. the CHECK of a type-reference node,
    ; and 520 are the dispatcher's own TypeReference arm. Every count below that
    ; says *arrival* is an arrival at the second of those two, because a probe was
    ; the only thing that could separate them (§3.5dk's rule, met from the far
    ; side: a row is not a chapter's cost, it is the cost of the first thing behind
    ; it — and here the first thing is two things).
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE SPLIT OF THOSE 520 IS WHAT SCOPED THE SLICE. `resolve_name` was
    ; called with the TYPE meaning at each arrival and the answers counted:
    ; **255 MISS (49 %)** — the globals table, §3.11 — **162 a TYPE PARAMETER
    ; (31 %)**, 35 not an identifier, 26 a type ALIAS, 24 an INTERFACE, 17 an ENUM
    ; and 1 a CLASS. So the resolvable half is 187 of 520, the type parameter is
    ; two thirds of it, and the four stops below are not a hedge — each is a
    ; measured row.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MISS NEEDS NO NEW STOP, AND THAT IS THE SLICE'S CHEAPEST FINDING.
    ; `resolveTypeReferenceName` hands `resolveEntityName` a MESSAGE, and
    ; resolve_name's own tail already refuses to report TS2304 without the globals
    ; table (slice 91's argument, at its line). So the 255 misses stop at
    ; `resolve-name-not-found`, one chapter earlier, and this one owes nothing for
    ; them.
    ;
    ; ★★★ BUT `getUnresolvedSymbolForEntityName` IS **NOT** A CHAPTER WITH NO INPUT,
    ; AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS PARAGRAPH SAID IT WAS. The claim followed from the
    ; miss stopping — and there is a second way to reach the tail that does not go
    ; through a miss at all: `resolveEntityName` answers nil WITHOUT stopping when
    ; the name node IS MISSING (`let x: ;`), which the parser produces from a
    ; recovered parse. Measured on the stage-1 corpus: **14 events over 5 units**,
    ; and the reduction is two lines. So the unresolved-symbol table is a ROW, with
    ; a number, and the tail below reports rather than pretending nothing arrives.
    ; ★★★**A containment proof derived from one caller's behaviour is a claim about
    ; that caller, not about the callee** — §3.5en's finding two from a third
    ; direction, and this time the instrument that refuted it was the stop histogram
    ; the same slice was reading for something else.

    ; isConstTypeReference, whole.
    function is_const_type_reference(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked


















    ; ast.IsAssertionExpression — `x as T` and the older `<T>x`.
    function is_assertion_expression(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked







    ; getTypeReferenceName, whole. ★The ExpressionWithTypeArguments arm answers nil
    ; for an expression that is not a simple qualified name, and that nil is an
    ; ANSWER — resolveTypeReferenceName turns it into unknownSymbol — so it is not
    ; a stop.
    function get_type_reference_name(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]? linked















    ; ast.IsEntityNameExpression, whole — an identifier, or a property access whose
    ; name is an identifier and whose expression is itself one.
    function is_entity_name_expression(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked

















    ; ── resolveEntityName ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE IDENTIFIER ARM IS THE WHOLE OF IT HERE, AND THE OTHER TWO ARE ONE
    ; STOP RATHER THAN TWO. `KindQualifiedName` and `KindPropertyAccessExpression`
    ; both go to `resolveQualifiedName`, which resolves the LEFT with the NAMESPACE
    ; meaning and then reads the namespace's exports — the module-symbol chapter,
    ; not this one. 35 arrivals at stage 1.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NAMESPACE FORK INSIDE THE IDENTIFIER ARM IS A GATE WITH A REPORT AND
    ; NO INPUT, and it says so rather than being left out: its only caller is
    ; `resolveQualifiedName`, which stops one line above, so the fork cannot be
    ; entered from anywhere in this port. §3.5en's finding two is exactly why it is
    ; written as a stop and not omitted — *a left-out arm and a stopped arm are the
    ; same code until the day the input arrives, and then one of them reports and
    ; the other one answers*.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MESSAGE IS THE DEFAULT CODE FOR THE REASON getResolvedSymbol'S HEADER
    ; ALREADY GIVES: `getCannotFindNameDiagnosticForName` forks five ways by the
    ; identifier's TEXT, every fork is read only inside `onFailedToResolveSymbol`,
    ; and that callback is a stop. Porting the fork would be machinery whose one
    ; reader is unported.
    procedure resolve_entity_name(mutable this, mutable name: ref[AstNode], meaning: int, ignore_errors: bool, dont_resolve_alias: bool, mutable location: ref[AstNode]?) @page true @direct true returns ref[Symbol]? io reads checker_allow_umd_global_access linked

































































































































    ; getAliasDeclarationFromName.
    function get_alias_declaration_from_name(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]? linked























    ; ── The alias family: a module specifier, an alias target, an export table ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT MAKES THE 285-LINE MODULE RESOLVER PORTABLE IS THE ORACLE'S OWN
    ; STUB, AND IT IS A FACT ABOUT `tests/oracle/types.go` RATHER THAN A
    ; SIMPLIFICATION: `GetResolvedModule` answers nil for every specifier, so
    ; `resolveExternalModule` reduces to its FAILURE path — the `@types/` report,
    ; an ambient-module lookup in globals, and TS2307. Every branch that reads
    ; `resolvedModule` is dead BY VALUE, which is why each is written as its own
    ; guard rather than left out; the day a program layer arrives, they are the
    ; work list.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THREE OF THE FOUR FORKS UNDER `moduleNotFoundError != nil` ARE DEAD
    ; FOR A REASON THAT IS NOT THE STUB, so each is named here rather than at its
    ; line. The `.json` fork asks `!GetResolveJsonModule()`, and that derivation
    ; answers TRUE under this harness — ResolveJsonModule is unset, the emit module
    ; kind is ES2022, so `GetModuleResolutionKind()` falls to **Bundler** and the
    ; option is on. The extensionless-ESM fork asks `mode == ResolutionModeESM`,
    ; and the stub's `GetModeForUsageLocation` is None. The AlternateResult fork
    ; asks `resolvedModule != nil`. What is left is `c.error(errorNode,
    ; moduleNotFoundError, moduleReference)`.

    ; core.NodeCoreModules, as a predicate rather than a map. The reference builds
    ; the set as *unprefixed ∪ "node:"+unprefixed ∪ exclusivelyPrefixed*, so the
    ; question factors: strip a leading `node:` and ask the unprefixed table, or
    ; ask the five prefixed-only names whole.
