    ; were separated by compare_type_lists, which compares ARITY first and therefore
    ; ordered the one-element tuple before the two-element one in either direction.
    ; The reference orders `readonly` AFTER mutable and only then by arity.
    function compare_tuple_types(t1: ref[Type], t2: ref[Type]) returns int linked



























































    ; ★★★ CompareTypes. THE LIST OF ARMS THIS FUNCTION LEFT OUT WAS A CONTAINMENT
    ; CLAIM AND IT EXPIRED ONE PRODUCER AT A TIME. What stood here read *this port
    ; can MAKE a type for six of them, and the other eight are containment rather
    ; than omission — nothing here mints an Intersection, an Index, an
    ; IndexedAccess, a Conditional, a Substitution, a TemplateLiteral, a
    ; StringMapping or a UniqueESSymbol type, so an arm for one could not be
    ; reached by a test* — and every one of those eight has a `new_*` producer in
    ; this file today. Two of them were even answered in the body (slice 135 wrote
    ; the Intersection and the UniqueESSymbol arms) while the header went on
    ; asserting they could not arrive; the other six were genuinely missing, so
    ; every union holding a template literal, a `keyof`, an indexed access, a
    ; conditional, a substitution or an `Uppercase<T>` came out in ID order where
    ; the reference orders by the type's own data. ★Measured on the smallest
    ; witness there is: `` `foo${string}` | `${number}foo` `` printed in source
    ; order here and `` `${number}foo` | `foo${string}` `` in the reference, because
    ; slices.Compare over the TEXTS puts `""` before `"foo"`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ A CONTAINMENT CLAIM IS TRUE ON THE DAY IT IS WRITTEN AND NOTHING MARKS
    ; THE DAY IT STOPS BEING TRUE (§3.5hd). The one thing that would have caught
    ; this is the claim naming its own refuter — which is why the list above is
    ; replaced by arms rather than by a newer list.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE OBJECT ARM IS THE ONE WITH REAL DEAD SUB-ARMS AND THEY ARE PROVEN
    ; DEAD, not assumed: a TUPLE type is refused by new_object_type (it reports),
    ; and a DEFERRED type reference cannot exist because create_type_reference
    ; always writes resolvedTypeArguments — which is the same fact
    ; get_type_arguments' `get-type-arguments-deferred` stop records from the other
    ; side. `mapper` is not carried at all. So the reference's four-way sub-switch
    ; collapses to: both references, order by the type ARGUMENT lists; one
    ; reference, it comes first; neither, they are two anonymous types with the same
    ; symbol and the same object-type KIND, and the id decides.
    ;
    ; ★ `t1.checker != t2.checker` is a panic upstream and is not written: a
    ; Checker here is built per file and there is one.
    procedure compare_types(mutable this, mutable a: ref[Type]?, mutable b: ref[Type]?) returns int reads symbol_id_lock linked


















































































































































































































































































    ; `slices.Compare(t1.texts, t2.texts)` over two []string — element by element
    ; with strings.Compare, and the SHORTER list first when one is a prefix of the
    ; other. ★That is not compare_type_lists' shape, which compares LENGTHS first:
    ; slices.Compare orders `["a"]` before `["a", ""]` and compare_type_lists would
    ; order by count. The one caller is the template-literal arm.
    function compare_text_lists(this, l1: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]?, l2: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]?) returns int linked







































    ; compareTypeMappers. ★THE KIND ORDERING IS THE REFERENCE'S `iota` AND NOT AN
    ; INVENTION: Unknown 0, Simple 1, Array 2, Merged 3, and every other mapper
    ; upstream inherits TypeMapperBase.Kind, which answers Unknown — so the six
