    ; the nil is the CALLER's and a second caller would have another.
    procedure requires_adding_implicit_undefined(mutable this, mutable parameter: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked











    procedure is_required_initialized_parameter(mutable this, mutable parameter: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked




















    procedure is_optional_uninitialized_parameter_property(mutable this, mutable parameter: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked














    ; declaredParameterTypeContainsUndefined. ★The reference's own comment says why
    ; the error type counts: *allow error type here to avoid confusing errors that
    ; the annotation has to contain undefined*.
    procedure declared_parameter_type_contains_undefined(mutable this, mutable parameter: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked
















    ; parameterToParameterDeclarationName — the DECLARATION's own name node when
    ; there is one, and the symbol's name otherwise.
    ;
    ; ★★ A BINDING PATTERN IS A STOP RATHER THAN A NAME. cloneBindingName rebuilds
    ; the pattern with every initializer removed, so `function f({a, b} = x)` names
    ; its parameter `{ a, b }` — a second name shape with its own printer, and one
    ; whose ELEMENTS are not the ones the source wrote.
    procedure write_parameter_name(mutable this, ps: ref[Symbol], mutable pd: ref[AstNode]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked



































    ; The binding pattern as a NAME, recursively — a nested pattern is an element's
    ; name like any other.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TRAILING COMMA IS THE SOURCE'S AND IT IS NOT DECORATION (slice 183).
    ; Both pattern lists are emitted with LFAllowTrailingComma, and `emitList`
    ; writes the separator after the last element when the NodeList HAS one — so
    ; `function f({ a, })` names its parameter `{ a, }` and `function f({ a })`
    ; names it `{ a }`. Eleven units printed the comma-less spelling for both.
    ; It is the LIST's own range that answers, exactly as it does for the grammar
    ; checks (`list_has_trailing_comma`), and it is asked HERE rather than at the
    ; elements because a hole writes nothing at all: `[, b,]` is two elements, the
    ; first an OmittedExpression, and the last comma is the list's.
    procedure write_binding_pattern_name(mutable this, mutable pattern: ref[AstNode], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked







































    ; NodeList.HasTrailingComma for a list held by a node of ANY file — the
    ; printer's twin of `list_has_trailing_comma`, which asks the checker's own
    ; `file` and therefore answers only about the unit under check. A parameter
    ; whose type is being printed can be declared in the lib, so the range is
    ; looked up on the OWNER's source file.
    ;
    ; ★ A missing row answers FALSE and reports nothing: every list this printer
    ; reaches was parsed from a real file, and a pattern whose row is absent is a
    ; pattern with no trailing comma in every shape the corpus has. It is the same
    ; polarity `list_has_trailing_comma` chose and for the same reason — the answer
    ; feeds a decision where false is the quiet side.
    procedure node_list_has_trailing_comma(this, mutable owner: ref[AstNode], list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns bool linked














    ; One element of a pattern. ★An array HOLE is an OmittedExpression and writes
    ; nothing at all, which is what makes `[, a]` come out with its leading comma
    ; and no name in front of it.
    procedure write_binding_element_name(mutable this, mutable element: ref[AstNode], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked


































    ; ── the printed COMPUTED property name of a binding pattern (slice 211) ──────
    ;
    ; `cloneBindingName` VISITS the pattern and the printer prints what the visit
    ; returns, so a computed property name is not the source slice the identifier
    ; arms take: `[class { static x = 1 }.x]` comes out as
    ; `[class {    static x = 1;}.x]`. The class members are a MULTILINE, INDENTED
    ; list (LFClassMembers) and the type writer's newline is the EMPTY string, so
    ; every member line begins with four spaces per indent level and ends with the
    ; trailing semicolon, and the closing brace sits at the OUTER level
    ; (`classWithStaticFieldInParameterBindingPattern` ×3). The arms below are the
    ; printer's shapes this needs; every other expression prints as its source text,
    ; which is the printer's own answer wherever an emission is token-for-token its
    ; source. ★A METHOD member falls to the source text too: printing its body is a
    ; statement emitter this file does not carry, and no unit prints one.
    procedure write_printed_expression(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], indent: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked

































    ; emitClassExpression: modifiers, `class`, the name, type parameters, heritage
    ; clauses (each ` extends X, Y` with its own leading space), ` {`, the members
    ; one per indented line, `}` at the outer level.
    procedure write_printed_class_expression(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], indent: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked
















































































    ; emitClassElement, the PROPERTY arm written out: modifiers, name, postfix token,
    ; type annotation, ` = ` initializer, the trailing semicolon.
    procedure write_printed_class_element(mutable this, member: ref[AstNode], indent: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked






























    ; A property name through the printer: a computed one is `[` expression `]`,
    ; every other kind its source text.
    procedure write_printed_property_name(mutable this, name: ref[AstNode]?, indent: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked



















    ; emitModifierList: each modifier (or decorator) followed by one space.
    procedure write_printed_modifiers(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked




















    ; A `<…>` list of type parameters or type arguments, comma-separated, each item
    ; its source text; nothing at all for an absent list.
    procedure write_printed_node_list(mutable this, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked
























    ; Four spaces per level — the text writer's indentation.
    procedure write_indentation(level: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked










    ; The node's source text from its first token to its end.
    procedure write_node_source_text(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked















    ; ★★★ A SYNTHESIZED IDENTIFIER HAS NO SOURCE AND THE PRINTER IS NOT
    ; DeclarationNameToString. Upstream this path CLONES the identifier and hands
    ; it to the emit printer, which writes a synthesized node's own text; the
    ; scanner function write_declaration_name transcribes answers `(Missing)` for
    ; the empty range such a node carries, and that is right where it is used — in
    ; a DIAGNOSTIC. The two are separated here rather than in that function for
    ; exactly that reason.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SHAPE THAT SEPARATES THEM IS THE JSDOC REPARSER'S `@this` PARAMETER:
    ; it has a name, an Identifier reading `this`, and no source range at all, so
    ; `/** @this {string} */ function f(a) {}` printed
    ; `((Missing): string, a: number) => void`. Slice 112 is where it became
    ; visible, because until the return type was inferred the unit stopped.
    procedure write_identifier_name(mutable this, name: ref[AstNode]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked












    ; The identifier NODE's text — `idText` on a synthesized clone. ★A null node is
    ; the `(Missing)` word write_identifier_name answers, because the two callers
    ; above reach this through a qualified name's right half.
    procedure write_identifier_node_text(name: ref[AstNode]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked








    ; The symbol's own NAME, which is the fallback wherever a declaration cannot
    ; supply one. ★It is the raw name and not write_symbol_name's declaration text:
    ; upstream this is `b.newIdentifier(parameterSymbol.Name, …)`.
    procedure write_symbol_own_name(ps: ref[Symbol], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked





    ; getEffectiveParameterDeclaration — the parameter declaration among the
    ; symbol's own, and the JSDoc parameter tag when the symbol is not transient.
    ; ★The second arm is a JS shape whose declarations this port does not bind as
    ; parameters, so it answers null there; written as the reference's line.
    function effective_parameter_declaration(ps: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[AstNode]? linked





























    ; isOptionalParameter, whose three arms need three different things.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE INITIALIZER ARM COLLAPSES TO THE SIGNATURE'S OWN `minArgumentCount`,
    ; and that is READ OFF getMinArgumentCountEx rather than assumed. It is called
    ; with `StrongArityForUntypedJS|VoidIsNonOptional`, and under those two flags the
    ; function (a) skips the untyped-JS early return, (b) has no tuple rest to widen
    ; from, and (c) RETURNS BEFORE the void loop and before the memo write. What is
    ; left of it is the literal field. ★So this must NOT call get_min_argument_count,
    ; which is the same function under `MinArgumentCountFlagsNone` and answers a
    ; DIFFERENT number — its whole void loop is the difference.
    ;
    ; ★ The IIFE arm is written at its own line (slice 174).
    procedure is_optional_parameter(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked





















































    ; indexInfoToObjectComputedNamesOrSignatureDeclaration, whose first half needs
    ; `indexInfo.components` — the slot Signature's neighbour IndexInfo deliberately
    ; does not carry (see its header: the two writers are getObjectLiteralIndexInfo
    ; and getUnionIndexInfos, neither of which exists here). With no components the
    ; function IS its last line, so this is
    ; indexInfoToIndexSignatureDeclarationHelper and nothing else.
    ;
    ; ★ `getNameFromIndexInfo` is the DECLARATION's own parameter name, or `x` when
    ; there is no declaration — which is the reference's own fallback and not an
    ; invention.
    procedure write_index_signature(mutable this, mutable info: ref[IndexInfo], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked


    procedure write_index_signature_ex(mutable this, mutable info: ref[IndexInfo], elide: bool, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked











































    ; ONE ACCESSOR of the pair: the declaration of that kind, its signature read
    ; through the symbol's mapper when there is one, and the member. ★A missing
    ; declaration is not a failure — upstream writes only the accessors that
    ; EXIST, which is how a getter-only property prints as one member.
    procedure write_accessor_member(mutable this, mutable sym: ref[Symbol], decl_kind: int, form: int, mutable mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked

























    ; addPropertyToElementList, which appends its OWN terminators because it can
    ; produce SEVERAL members: an overloaded method contributes one MethodSignature
    ; per call signature, which is how `{ (x: string): string; (x: number): string; }`
    ; and a two-overload method both come out.
    ;
    ; ★★★ FOUR OF ITS FORKS ARE CLOSED BY THE FLAG MASK AND ONE BY THE PORT.
    ; WriteClassExpressionAsTypeLiteral removes the prototype filter and both
    ; tracker reports; `isExpanding` is false (maxExpansionDepth −1) so the second
    ; prototype filter is dead too; and the late-bound-name block reports through
    ; the tracker, which typeToString does not read. The ACCESSOR fork is the one
    ; this port cannot take and it stops.
    ;
    ; ★★★ shouldUsePlaceholderForProperty IS LIVE SINCE SLICE 160, AND THE NOTE
    ; THAT STOOD HERE WAS RIGHT UNTIL THE DAY IT WASN'T. It read *"the reverse-
    ; mapped stack, whose only filler is a reverse-mapped type —
    ; resolve_structured_type_members already refuses those, so the placeholder
    ; cannot arrive"* — a claim about a STOP, and the stop is gone. A reverse-
    ; mapped property whose printing has already been entered, or which is nested
    ; inside a mapping over a non-anonymous type, is written `any` rather than
    ; descended into, so without this the printer follows a reverse mapping as
    ; deep as the type goes and prints a name the reference does not.
    procedure write_property_member(mutable this, mutable sym: ref[Symbol], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads checker_exact_optional_property_types linked







































































































































































































    ; shouldUsePlaceholderForProperty — *use a placeholder for a reverse mapped
    ; property we have (1) already descended into, (2) that is a nested reverse
    ; mapping within a mapping over a non-anonymous type, or (3) that is a deeply
    ; nested property originating from the same mapped type*.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THREE CONDITIONS ARE NOT ONE RULE. (1) is termination for a cycle,
    ; (3) is termination for an infinitely deep reverse mapping, and (2) is a SIZE
    ; budget with the reference's own reasoning attached — a deep reverse mapping
    ; over a library type such as `Window` would print pages, while an anonymous
    ; source usually came from an expression the reader wants to see.
    ;
    ; ★ Upstream asks `ReverseMappedSymbolLinks.Has(prop)` before every read. The
    ; three slots live on ValueSymbolLink here (see its own note), so `Has` is the
    ; slot the branch is about to read being non-null — which is the same question
    ; and one lookup rather than two.
    procedure should_use_placeholder_for_property(mutable this, prop: ref[Symbol]) returns bool linked

































































    ; The symbol stack's pop, which is pop_type_stack's twin for the same reason:
    ; `Array` has no pop, so one shorter is rebuilt.
    procedure pop_symbol_stack(this, mutable stack: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]) linked




















    ; filterType, at the ONE predicate this port calls it with — `!Undefined`.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS WRITTEN AS THE PREDICATE AND NOT AS A DELEGATE because the reference's
    ; `f` is a closure and this port has one caller; the day a second predicate
    ; arrives, the shape to reach for is a second function beside this one rather
    ; than a delegate parameter (§3.9e's rule for lookAhead, met again).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `core.Same` SHORT CIRCUIT IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION HERE — IT IS WHAT KEEPS
    ; A NON-OPTIONAL METHOD OUT OF getUnionTypeFromSortedList. A property whose type
    ; is already the signature-bearing type filters to itself and must come back as
    ; the SAME type, not as a freshly interned union of one.
    ;
    ; ★ The `origin` block is skipped: UnionTypeData carries the slot but nothing
    ; WRITES it (its own header), so `origin != nil` is false and `newOrigin` stays
    ; nil.
    procedure filter_undefined_out(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]? linked


































    ; getPropertyNameNodeForSymbol, then classifyPropertyName, then the printer.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE QUOTING RULE IS THE WHOLE OF THIS FUNCTION AND IT IS NOT "quote what
    ; is not an identifier". classifyPropertyName is three-way: an identifier is
    ; written bare, a NUMERIC-LITERAL name that is not string-named and is
    ; non-negative is written bare as a number, and everything else is quoted. That
    ; is why `{ 1: number; }` has no quotes and `{ "peer-a": string; }` does.
    ;
    ; ★★ `stringNamed` IS AN `every` OVER THE DECLARATIONS and it flips the middle
    ; arm off: a property declared `{ "1": number }` is string-named, so its numeric
    ; text is QUOTED even though it would classify as numeric. Both spellings occur
    ; in the corpus.
    ;
    ; ★ getPropertyNameNodeForSymbolFromNameType, in front of all of it, reads
    ; valueSymbolLinks' `nameType` — a late-bound name. The slot is not carried, and
    ; a symbol that has one is exactly a computed name, which write_symbol_name
    ; already reports one level up.
    ;
    ; ★ The `__#private` rewrite and the PrivateIdentifier clone are two more shapes;
    ; the corpus's `#y` parameter shows the clone path is reachable, so it is written.
    procedure write_property_name_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable sym: ref[Symbol], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked


















































































































































































































































    ; `b.isSingleQuotedStringNamed(d)` — the declaration's name is a string literal
    ; whose SingleQuote token flag is set. ★It is the STRICTER sibling of
    ; declaration_is_string_named and not a refinement of it: the reference asks both
    ; as separate `every` folds over the declarations, so a symbol declared once with
    ; `'a'` and once with `"a"` is string-named and NOT single-quoted.
    ; ★★★ `GetNameOfDeclaration` in BOTH predicates (§3.5hr): the slot accessor
    ; answered false for every assignment declaration, and classifyPropertyName
    ; un-quotes a numeric-looking name only when it is NOT string-named — which is
    ; what printed `decl5["101"]`'s member as a bare `101`. Takes `this` for the
    ; accessor.
    function declaration_is_single_quoted_string_named(this, d: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool linked












    ; `b.isStringNamed(d)` — the declaration's name is a string literal. ★Its two
    ; computed-name arms ask checkExpression for the name type; a computed name has
    ; already stopped one level up, so what is left is the literal test.
    ; isStringNamed, whole — and its first two arms are SLICE 129's, because they
    ; are the ones a COMPUTED name takes.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT DECIDES A PRINTED FORM AND NOT A TASTE. `{ ["404"]: v }` prints
    ; `{ "404": v }` and `{ [100]: v }` prints `{ 100: v }`, and the whole difference
    ; is this predicate: classifyPropertyName un-quotes a numeric-looking name ONLY
    ; when it is not string-named, so a string-typed computed name has to answer TRUE
    ; here or the quotes vanish. The port had the third arm alone (a literal
    ; `"404":` in the source), which was every declaration that could reach it while
    ; every computed name stopped.
    ;
    ; ★ It CHECKS — `checkExpression` on the name's expression — so it is a method
    ; and not a static, and the type it asks for is memoized by the computed-name
    ; check that has already run in this chapter.
    procedure declaration_is_string_named(mutable this, mutable d: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked































    function name_is(data: Slice[char], lit: Slice[char]) returns bool linked













    ; scanner.IsIdentifierText — the first rune must start an identifier and every
    ; later one must continue it. ★The LanguageVariant argument selects
    ; IsIdentifierPartEx, whose JSX arm admits `-` and `:`; this call site passes
    ; Standard, which is the plain predicate the scanner already exports.
    function is_identifier_text(data: Slice[char]) returns bool linked

















    ; isNumericLiteralName — `jsnum.FromString(name).String() == name`, which is the
    ; reference's own spelling and NOT a syntactic test: it accepts `Infinity`,
    ; `-Infinity` and `NaN` deliberately, and refuses `0xF00D` and `0170015` because
    ; their round trip is not their text.
    function is_numeric_literal_name(data: Slice[char]) returns bool linked
















    ; TypeAlias.ToTypeReferenceNode — the Any arm's printer, and it is NOT
    ; symbol_to_type_node with a different caller. That one goes through
    ; write_symbol_name, which reads the symbol's DECLARATIONS; an unresolved
    ; symbol has none, and the reference's line here is
    ; `b.symbolToEntityNameNode(t.Symbol())`, which reads `symbol.Name` and walks
    ; the PARENT chain. So `A.T` prints as `A.T` out of two synthetic symbols that
    ; were never declared anywhere.
    procedure write_alias_type_reference(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked








































    ; symbolToEntityNameNode — the parent chain joined by dots, LEAF LAST.
    procedure write_symbol_entity_name(symbol: ref[Symbol], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) linked










    ; symbolToTypeNode, and the ONE of its two branches this entry point can take.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CHAIN IS ONE ELEMENT LONG HERE — write_symbol_name's header proves it
    ; from the nil enclosing declaration — so createAccessFromSymbolChain runs with
    ; `index == 0` and `parent == nil`, which removes the export-alias lookup, the
    ; indexed-access fork and the computed-name recursion in one step. What is left
    ; is getNameOfSymbolAsWritten, and the type-argument list is nil because the
    ; caller passes none.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE IMPORT-TYPE BRANCH IS PORTED SINCE SLICE 129, AND THE STOP THAT STOOD
    ; HERE NAMED THE WRONG BLOCKER — §3.5dk from the outside. It read *the specifier
    ; comes from getSpecifierForModuleSymbol, which is `modulespecifiers` and its
    ; whole preference apparatus*; that function's SECOND guard is
    ; `if b.ctx.enclosingFile == nil`, and this printer's context has no enclosing
    ; declaration at all (write_symbol_name's header proves it), so the reference
    ; returns from that guard and never reaches GetModuleSpecifiers.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE GUARD ANSWERS IS `StripQuotes(symbol.Name)`, AND ITS CONDITION
    ; HOLDS FOR BOTH ARMS THIS BRANCH CAN SEE — which is why the file-name fallback
    ; under it is unreachable here rather than skipped. A module symbol's name is
    ; QUOTED on both roads into this branch: `declare module "m"` carries the quotes
    ; in the source, and a source FILE that is a module is named
    ; `"<path without its extension>"` by bind_source_file_as_external_module (whose
    ; header states the same pair of bytes is how the checker recognises one).
    ; IsAmbientModuleSymbolName is a test for exactly that pair. The unquoted case
    ; keeps the stop, so an arm the argument says cannot arrive still cannot answer.
    ;
    ; ★ The `file`/`equivalentSymbol` pair the reference computes FIRST is dead on
    ; this path: its only reader is the `file == nil` guard one line above, which the
    ; same name test answers — and its helper
    ; (getFileSymbolIfFileSymbolExportEqualsContainer) lives in
    ; symbolaccessibility.go, which this project does not port.
    ;
    ; ★ The literal is `b.newStringLiteral(specifier)` with NO EmitFlags, and
    ; typeToStringEx prints through createPrinterWithRemoveComments — NOT the
    ; NeverAsciiEscape printer — so the specifier is ASCII-ESCAPED, which is
    ; write_ascii_escaped_literal_name and not the string-literal TYPE's writer.
    ;
    ; ★ is_ambient_module_symbol_name is the one already in this file (slice 104's,
    ; beside the globals merge) — §3.5ed: the grep that finds it asks about the
    ; reference name's distinctive MIDDLE, `AmbientModuleSymbolName`, not about a
    ; snake_case whole this slice would have invented. StripQuotes is then the
    ; two-byte window `nd + 1` / `nl - 2`, because the pair it accepts in general
    ; (`'`, `"` or a backtick, the two agreeing) is already known to be `"` here.
    ; ── symbolaccessibility.go: the chain that NAMES a symbol (slice 183) ─────
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY A WHOLE CHAPTER OF AN EXCLUDED FILE IS PORTED HERE. `coverage.sh`
    ; lists `symbolaccessibility.go` under NOT_A_GOAL beside the declaration
    ; emitter, and that exclusion is about the DENOMINATOR, not about the exit
    ; criterion: the yardstick compares the text `TypeToString` produces, and this
    ; machinery decides that text whenever two constituents of one printed list
    ; carry the same name. `[c, m.c]` came out `[c, c]` and `Foo.Yep | Bar.Yep`
    ; came out `Yep | Yep` — a name that says two different types are one. **A file
    ; excluded from the coverage denominator can still own an answer the criterion
    ; compares, and the two lists are maintained for different questions.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT RUNS ONLY UNDER THE FULLY-QUALIFIED FLAG, AND THE FLAG IS SET BY A
    ; COLLISION. `lookupSymbolChainWorker`'s gate is `enclosingDeclaration != nil ||
    ; flags&UseFullyQualifiedType != 0`, and `TypeToString` passes a nil enclosing
    ; declaration — so with the flag off the chain is the symbol alone and nothing
    ; below this line can change a name. `mapToTypeNodes` turns the flag on for the
    ; entries of a list whose single-identifier names collided (see
    ; write_qualified_on_collision), which bounds the blast radius of this chapter
    ; to exactly the lists that print one name twice.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE NIL ENCLOSING DECLARATION COLLAPSES `someSymbolTableInScope` TO
    ; ONE TABLE. Upstream it walks `location.Parent` from the enclosing declaration
    ; collecting locals, module exports and class/interface member tables, and ends
    ; with `callback(c.globals, …)`. With a nil start the loop body never runs, so
    ; the GLOBALS table is the whole walk. This is written as the reduction rather
    ; than as the general form with an unreachable loop, and the reduction is a
    ; claim about the CALLER: this printer has no enclosing declaration to thread
    ; and no field to hold one. The day one arrives, this function is where the
    ; walk goes — it is named `some_symbol_table_in_scope_globals` so that a reader
    ; looking for the general form does not find a half of it wearing the whole
    ; name.

    ; ── mapToTypeNodes' NAME-COLLISION PASS (slice 183) ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS IS THE SECOND ENTRY POINT THE PRINTER'S OWN CONTAINMENT PROOF SAID
    ; WOULD EXPIRE IT, AND IT IS INSIDE THE PRINTER. The note in the three
    ; name-writing arms reads *"`Checker.TypeToString(t)`, which is the ONLY entry
    ; point the oracle uses, is `typeToString(t, nil)` … the flag is not in
    ; `AllowUniqueESSymbolType|UseAliasDefinedOutsideCurrentScope`. So the else
    ; branch runs for EVERY symbol, the chain is one element long"* — and it ends
    ; *"the day a second entry point appears, all four collapses expire together."*
    ; The premise was about the CALLER, and what sets the flag is the printer
    ; SETTING IT ON ITSELF: `mapToTypeNodes` re-renders the colliding entries of one
    ; list with `UseFullyQualifiedType` on. **A containment proof that quantifies
    ; over entry points is not proof about a function that changes its own
    ; context.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT IT IS FOR, in upstream's own words: *to avoid printing types like
    ; `[Foo, Foo]` or `Bar & Bar` where occurrences of the same name actually come
    ; from different namespaces.* `[c, m.c]` printed `[c, c]` here and
    ; `Foo.Yep | Bar.Yep` printed `Yep | Yep` — a name that claims two types are
    ; one, which is worse than a long name.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PORT NEEDS TWO PASSES WHERE UPSTREAM NEEDS ONE REWRITE, because
    ; upstream holds NODES it can replace in place and this printer writes TEXT.
    ; Each constituent is therefore rendered into a scratch builder of its own, the
    ; colliding ones are rendered a SECOND time with the flag on, and the pieces are
    ; joined. The cost is one builder per constituent on a path no measurement has
    ; ever named; the alternative — a predicate that decides qualification without
    ; rendering — would be a second copy of the whole naming fork.
    ;
    ; ★ `isBareList` is upstream's truncation switch and does not reach this half:
    ; the truncation budget is asked by the callers, which already report where the
    ; reference elides.

    ; typesAreSameReference — two occurrences of one name are innocent when they
    ; name the same thing, and identity is asked three ways: the type, its symbol,
    ; its alias.
    function types_are_same_reference(a: ref[Type]?, b: ref[Type]?) returns bool linked






















    ; The pass itself: given one list's types and the identifier-type-reference
    ; name each rendered under (an empty slice where it rendered as anything else),
    ; mark the entries that must be rendered again fully qualified.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS NOT *"a name that appears twice"* — it is a name that appears twice
    ; for types that are NOT all the same reference. `A<string> | A<number>` keys
    ; both constituents on `A` and leaves both alone, because the two share a
    ; symbol; upstream's `arrayIsHomogeneous` is that test and dropping it would
    ; qualify every generic union.
    procedure mark_name_collisions(this, types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]], names: ref[Array[Slice[char]]], mutable marks: ref[Array[int]]) linked
















































    ; The two-pass list writer. `mode` selects the constituent renderer — 1 for a
    ; union, 2 for an intersection — because Scaly has no closure to hand one in
    ; and a delegate would put an indirect call on every constituent of every name.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TRUNCATION TEST STAYS AGAINST THE REAL SINK. It asks how much has been
    ; written so far, and the scratch builders hold the pieces, so the check is made
    ; on `out` before each piece is appended — which is where the loop this replaces
    ; made it.
    procedure write_constituent_list(mutable this, mutable list: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]], n: int, sep: Slice[char], mode: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked
























































































































    procedure write_constituent(mutable this, mutable ct: ref[Type]?, mode: int, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked






    ; getQualifiedLeftMeaning — the meaning a QUALIFIER carries. Value stays Value;
    ; everything else asks for a namespace, because `A.B` can only be reached
    ; through an `A` that has members.
    function qualified_left_meaning(right_meaning: int) returns int linked






    ; needsQualification over the one table in scope. ★★ THE EARLY `return true` IS
    ; NOT A QUALIFY — upstream's callback answers *stop walking*, and it answers it
    ; in two places with opposite verdicts: a table whose entry IS this symbol stops
    ; with `qualify` still false (the name resolves to us, so the bare name is
    ; right), and a table whose entry is a DIFFERENT symbol of the same meaning
    ; stops with qualify TRUE. Folding the two into one boolean answer would lose
    ; the difference, which is why the walk is spelled out rather than inlined.
    ; ── slice 221: THE ACCESSIBLE SYMBOL CHAIN UNDER AN ENCLOSING DECLARATION ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE PRINTER HAS AN ENCLOSING DECLARATION FOR ONE PURPOSE HERE. The
    ; checker's TypeToString passes nil, and every reuse arm of the node builder
    ; is gated on it — but addPropertyToElementList sets it to the PROPERTY'S
    ; DECLARATION around getPropertyNameNodeForSymbol, and a unique-symbol
    ; property name is serialized through symbolToExpression → lookupSymbolChain,
    ; which under an enclosing declaration is the ACCESSIBLE chain from there:
    ; `{ [Op.equal]: string }` prints `[OpTypes.equal]` (the interface reached
    ; through the variable declared with its type), `[NS.eq]`, `[K.ks]`, and a
    ; local `[loc]`. Before this slice the port's chain machinery read the GLOBALS
    ; table only (the enclosing-declaration-is-nil specialisation), and the
    ; unique-symbol arm stopped on every container that was not a module.
    ;
    ; someSymbolTableInScope is a callback walk upstream; here it is materialised
    ; once per ask into a table list (`collect_scope_tables`), which the two
    ; consumers — needsQualification and getAccessibleSymbolChain — iterate. The
    ; walk: the locals of every container that can have them (a global source
    ; file's excluded), the exports of a module or module-file, the TYPE members
    ; of a class or interface, a named class expression's own name, then globals.
    ; The visited set of (symbol, table) pairs is the reference's
    ; visitedSymbolTablesMap, shared by the nested asks canQualifySymbol makes.
    procedure needs_qualification(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol], meaning: int) returns bool io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked















































    function should_resolve_alias_for_qualification(this, s: ref[Symbol]) returns bool linked






