    procedure get_non_missing_type_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked













    ; getNameOfSymbolFromNameType — the name a symbol carries on its LINK rather
    ; than on a declaration, which is how a late-bound member and a mapped-type
    ; property are named at all.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT ANSWERS THE EMPTY STRING FOR THREE DIFFERENT REASONS and the caller
    ; must tell that apart from a name, so the answer is the out-cell `wrote` and
    ; the RESULT is the usual *no stop was recorded*. Writing into `out` and then
    ; measuring its length would not do: the empty answer is decided BEFORE
    ; anything is written, and every arm below is a single append.
    ;
    ; ★ The three arms are the reference's, in its order: not an identifier and
    ; not a numeric name → `valueToString`, which QUOTES a string and writes a
    ; number bare; a NEGATIVE numeric name → `[-1]`, because the minus cannot ride
    ; on a numeric-literal node; anything else → the name itself.
    procedure write_name_of_symbol_from_name_type(mutable this, symbol: ref[Symbol], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder], mutable wrote: ref[bool]) returns bool linked

























































    ; EscapeInternalSymbolName — the 0xFE sentinel a synthetic name carries becomes
    ; the two underscores a reader sees. Every other name is written as it stands.
    procedure write_escaped_internal_symbol_name(mutable out: ref[StringBuilder], name: Slice[char]) linked













    ; getNameOfSymbolAsWritten, reduced by the two flag arguments above and by
    ; nothing else.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE COMPUTED-PROPERTY FORK asks the LATE-BOUND name — a different table —
    ; and is entered by a symbol whose first named declaration has a computed name,
    ; which a class member can have. It was a stop until the name-type writer above
    ; arrived. ★The three ANONYMOUS arms below it (`(Anonymous class)`,
    ; `(Anonymous function)`) are names too and are written: the `encounteredError`
    ; they set is a nodebuilder flag whose only reader is the caller's error path,
    ; and typeToString passes IgnoreErrors.
    procedure write_symbol_name(mutable this, symbol: ref[Symbol], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked














































































    ; scanner.DeclarationNameToString — `(Missing)` for an empty range, and the
    ; node's own SOURCE TEXT otherwise. ★It is the source and not the scanned
    ; identifier text, which differs for `\u0041bc`; the ReparserTransformedLiteral
    ; fork above it is a JS-only shape and is a stop.
    procedure write_declaration_name(mutable this, name: ref[AstNode]?, mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked























    ; ── the ENTITY NAME's text (slice 148) ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; scanner.GetTextOfNodeFromSourceText with `includeTrivia = false`, which is
    ; what scanner.GetTextOfNode is. ★★ IT IS write_declaration_name WITHOUT THE
    ; TWO `(Missing)` ANSWERS, and the difference is load-bearing rather than
    ; cosmetic: DeclarationNameToString substitutes that WORD for an empty range
    ; while this one answers the EMPTY STRING, and both callers below fork on the
    ; LENGTH of what comes back — a `(Missing)` would be nine characters of text
    ; where the reference has none, which is the difference between TS2532 and
    ; TS18048.
    procedure write_text_of_node(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], mutable out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool linked
























    ; The same source range as a VIEW. ★★It exists so the `== "undefined"` test
    ; below reads the bytes write_text_of_node writes rather than a second
    ; derivation of them, and the two genuinely differ: `\u0075ndefined` SCANS as
    ; `undefined` and READS as `\u0075ndefined`, so asking the scanner's text
    ; would put TS18050 on a name the reference gives TS18048.
    procedure source_text_of_node(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode]) @page true @direct true returns Slice[char] linked






    ; ast.EntityNameToString, composed over a StringBuilder because what its two
    ; callers fork on is the LENGTH of the answer and not the answer: the type
    ; dump compares `C pos end code`, so the text itself is never printed and only
