    ; ancestor chain; the two kinds it looks for are the ones ported here.
    function node_is_in_jsdoc_name_reference_context(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked
















    ; ast.IsTypeDeclarationName — an IDENTIFIER that is the name of a type
    ; declaration. It is here rather than in `ast` because IsTypeDeclaration is (it
    ; reads the import/export type-only arms), and because GetNameOfDeclaration is
    ; on the binder.
    function is_type_declaration_name(this, name: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked









    ; ── GetTypeAtLocation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getTypeOfNode, and it is a CHAIN and not a kind switch because several of its
    ; predicates read the node's PARENT rather than its kind: `let x = 1` and
    ; `{ 1: v }` hold the same node in two spaces, and only the context tells them
    ; apart. Fifteen predicates in a fixed order, ending in errorType.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 109 REPLACED THE KIND INVENTORY WITH THE CHAIN, AND THAT IS THE
    ; WHOLE SLICE. Through slice 108 the ten predicates the port could not evaluate
    ; stood behind `kind_has_ported_type_arm`, a hand-written list of the kinds for
    ; which every one of them is provably false — three kinds, all of them tokens
    ; and empty statements. The list was the honest thing to do while the predicates
    ; were missing, because a chain that SKIPS a predicate it cannot evaluate falls
    ; through to errorType and answers `any`, which is 43 % of the corpus's type
    ; answers and therefore the single easiest way for this port to look right. The
    ; predicates are ported now (`ast.is_expression_node`,
    ; `is_declaration_name_or_import_property_name`, `is_type_declaration_name`,
    ; `try_get_class_implementing_or_extending`, `is_binding_pattern`,
    ; `is_in_right_side_of_import_or_export_assignment`, `is_meta_property`,
    ; `is_import_attributes`), so each arm is ASKED and the inventory is gone.
    ;
    ; ★ Where an arm's ANSWER is not ported the arm reports with the node's kind as
    ; the detail — which is a different and much smaller set than the kinds the
    ; inventory used to hold back, because most arms answer through machinery this
    ; port already has (get_type_from_type_node, get_declared_type_of_symbol,
    ; get_type_of_symbol, get_type_for_variable_like_declaration, check_expression).
    procedure get_type_of_node(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]?) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked

















































































































































































































    ; ── ast.GetMeaningFromDeclaration ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ IT LIVES HERE AND UPSTREAM HAS IT IN internal/ast, and the reason is this
    ; port's own layout rather than a preference: its ModuleDeclaration arm asks
    ; GetModuleInstanceState, which the reference keeps in `ast` and slice 31 put on
    ; the BINDER (it needs the visited set and the ancestor chain that the binder
    ; carries). The `ast` module is declared before `binder`, so a function in
    ; ast.scaly could not reach it — a module-level name from a module declared
    ; LATER is a hard `unknown identifier` and, in call-argument position, a
    ; silently dropped call. So the one arm that needs the binder decides where the
    ; whole function goes, and it goes with its dependency.
    ;
    ; Its reader is the type walk's exclusion for an IDENTIFIER: a name that
    ; declares nothing into the VALUE space is not asked for its type, with the
    ; type-alias name as the one exception.
    procedure get_meaning_from_declaration(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]?) returns int reads shared_lock linked






































