    procedure check_source_elements(mutable this, mutable nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) io reads checker_allow_unreachable_code linked





























    ; checkSourceElement's whole body is the save/restore of two registers around
    ; checkSourceElementWorker plus `c.instantiationCount = 0`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 154 GAVE IT THE FIRST REGISTER. The note here read *currentNode is
    ; read by the instantiation-depth machinery and withinUnreachableCode by the
    ; reachability check — neither of which exists here … so the saves have nothing
    ; to save yet*, and the instantiation-depth machinery landed in slice 86, seven
    ; slices before the sentence was last true. It cost two limiters their error
    ; and made them stops instead — see instantiate_type and get_conditional_type.
    ; ★★★ SLICE 184 GAVE IT THE SECOND, AND THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE — *the
    ; reachability check produces nothing this yardstick compares* — is why it took
    ; twenty-nine slices: it is TRUE of the diagnostic and FALSE of the chapter.
    ; See within_unreachable_code's own note for the measurement and the route.
    procedure check_source_element(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]?) io reads checker_allow_unreachable_code linked












    ; ── slice 184: the UNREACHABLE-CODE chapter ─────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ast.IsInstantiatedModule. ★The `preserveConstEnums` argument is not taken:
    ; its one caller here passes `c.compilerOptions.ShouldPreserveConstEnums()`,
    ; which this port carries as the `preserve_const_enums` field, and a parameter
    ; that can only ever be that field is a parameter whose call sites cannot
    ; disagree.
    procedure is_instantiated_module(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool reads shared_lock linked









    ; isSourceElementUnreachable, whole. Its precondition is
    ; is_potentially_executable_node, which the ONE caller below asks first.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO BRANCHES ANSWER THE SAME QUESTION FROM OPPOSITE SIDES AND ONLY
    ; ONE OF THEM IS CHEAP. NodeFlagsUnreachable is the BINDER's answer, written
    ; from currentFlow while binding, and it is a decision already made; the
    ; `else` asks the CHECKER, which walks the flow graph from this node's own flow
    ; node — and that walk is what makes this chapter visible to the yardstick at
    ; all (see within_unreachable_code). A port that answered the flag alone would
    ; be right about every statement after a `return` and silent about every one
    ; the binder could not decide.
    ;
    ; ★ The const-enum and module arms are not a refinement of *unreachable*: a
    ; non-instantiated module and a const enum EMIT NOTHING, so being unreachable
    ; is not a fact about them worth reporting. Both read the same option.
    procedure is_source_element_unreachable(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads shared_lock linked




















    ; `n.CanHaveStatements()` — the five kinds that own a statement LIST.
    function can_have_statements(k: int) returns bool linked












    ; checkSourceElementUnreachable, whole — with ONE deliberate divergence, and
    ; the divergence is the diagnostic.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REPORT IS NOT WRITTEN, AND THE PRICE IS MEASURED RATHER THAN
    ; ARGUED. Upstream ends with
    ;
    ;     c.addErrorOrSuggestion(c.compilerOptions.AllowUnreachableCode == core.TSFalse, diagnostic)
    ;
    ; so TS7027 is an ERROR only when the option is explicitly false and a
    ; SUGGESTION otherwise — and suggestions are not in GetDiagnostics, which is
    ; the only list either yardstick reads. This oracle fixes its options to
    ; `{SkipDefaultLibCheck: TSTrue, NoErrorTruncation: TSTrue}`, leaving
    ; AllowUnreachableCode at TSUnknown, so the error branch cannot be taken.
    ; Measured over the whole stage-2 corpus: **0 of 64 148 reference C lines carry
    ; code 7027.** ★The expiry is one line long and belongs to the INSTRUMENT, not
    ; to this file: the day tests/oracle/types.go sets AllowUnreachableCode to
    ; TSFalse, or honours a case's `// @allowUnreachableCode` option, this
    ; function owes the diagnostic and its span (upstream takes
    ; GetTokenPosOfNode(startNode) through endNode.End(), which is why the forward
    ; scan below computes an END it does not otherwise need).
    ;
    ; ★★★ EVERYTHING ELSE IS PORTED BECAUSE IT DECIDES HOW OFTEN THE REACHABILITY
    ; WALK RUNS, and the walk reports. The `reported_unreachable` test sits in
    ; FRONT of is_source_element_unreachable, so it SUPPRESSES walks; the forward
    ; scan ADDS them, on the statements after this one, and marks each so the
    ; enclosing loop's later arrivals take the suppressing branch. Leaving either
    ; half out changes the diagnostic COUNT of a unit in one direction each.
    ;
    ; ★ Upstream's BACKWARD scan is commented out in the reference (region
    ; diagnostics are not supported there yet) and is therefore not written here
    ; either; `first` stays the node's own offset, as it does upstream.
    procedure check_source_element_unreachable(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked

































































    ; the diagnostic: GetTokenPosOfNode(startNode) through endNode.End(), an error only
    ; under `allowUnreachableCode: false` (addErrorOrSuggestion)
    procedure report_unreachable_code(mutable this, start_node: ref[AstNode], end_node: ref[AstNode]) reads checker_allow_unreachable_code linked







    ; ── slice 78: the JSDoc pass in front of the switch ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE STEP THAT STOOD AS A BLANKET STOP IS THREE FUNCTIONS, AND WHAT DECIDES
    ; ITS SIZE IS THAT THE REPORT IS ONE FUNCTION DEEP RATHER THAN ONE STEP DEEP.
    ; checkJSDocComment's whole body is a switch over THREE kinds — JSDocLink,
    ; JSDocLinkCode, JSDocLinkPlain — calling resolveJSDocMemberName; every other
    ; comment part, and a JSDoc with no parts at all, is a no-op in the reference.
    ; So a part that is not a link needs no code here and is not a hole: doing
    ; nothing IS the answer, the same statement kind_has_no_check_arm makes one
    ; screen down. **The old gate read NodeFlagsHasJSDoc, which is set for every
    ; documented node**, so it stopped a population two orders larger than the
    ; reference's own step ever reports on: 110 units of stage 1, of which 3 hold a
    ; link.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE OTHER CANDIDATE FOR *what decides the size* WAS MEASURED AND
    ; REFUTED, which is worth more than the sentence it replaces. This note first
    ; read *the one that decides its size is EagerJSDoc* — the eager accessor reads
    ; the cache and never parses, so a TS file's plain doc comments are invisible to
    ; it — and control g02 swaps the LAZY accessor back in with nothing moving at
    ; all. The two provably coincide: a comment holding a link is always in the
    ; cache, because a link is what makes with_jsdoc parse eagerly. See
    ; `Parser.eager_jsdoc`, which is still what this asks and says why.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHY THE LINK ARM STILL STOPS THE WHOLE NODE, rather than recording and
    ; walking on. resolveJSDocMemberName is `resolveEntityName(…, ignoreErrors:
    ; true, …)` and then, for a qualified name, getTypeOfSymbol / getPropertyOfType
    ; / getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol — none of it ported, and the first of them is the
    ; entity-name chapter this port reports at 23 units elsewhere. Its documented
    ; PURPOSE is to record the reference for checkUnusedIdentifiers, which is inert
    ; under this harness (§3.5be's argument, twice over: both options unset and
    ; every report a suggestion), so the honest thing to say is that the machinery
    ; is missing rather than that the effect is nil — and abandoning is what the
    ; four link-bearing units already did. ★Control g05 walks on instead and prices
    ; the difference.
    ;
    ; ★ The DETAIL stays the node's kind rather than the link part's, so the
    ; `check-jsdoc-comments <kind>` rows of the work list keep meaning what they
    ; meant before the pass existed and the two histograms stay comparable across
    ; the slice.
    procedure check_jsdoc_of_node(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], k: int) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked






























    ; checkJSDocComments, whole — `for _, comment := range node.Comments()`.
    ; Answers true when a part stopped, which is this port's shape for a step the
    ; reference runs to the end.
    procedure check_jsdoc_comments(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]?, k: int) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked
















    ; checkJSDocComment, whole. The reference's own comment at the line states the
    ; scope: *this performs minimal checking of JSDoc nodes to ensure that @link
    ; references to entities are recorded for purposes of checking unused
    ; identifiers.*
    ; ★★★ THE LINK ARM IS CODE SINCE SLICE 143 AND THE NOTE ABOVE IT HAD EXPIRED.
    ; It read *resolveJSDocMemberName is `resolveEntityName(…)` … none of it
    ; ported* — `resolve_entity_name` has been in this file since the entity-name
    ; chapter, and with it the whole of the reference's step. The ANSWER is still
    ; thrown away, because its documented purpose is to record a reference for
