    ; ── slice 154: the entity-name half of tryGetElementAccessExpressionName ──
    ;
    ; tryGetNameFromEntityNameExpression, whole. `obj[SomeEnum.A]` and
    ; `obj[SOME_CONST]`: the name is not in the SOURCE at that position, it is the
    ; VALUE of a constant, so the function resolves the entity name to a symbol,
    ; insists the symbol is a constant variable or an enum member, and then asks
    ; the symbol's type — its annotation first, its initializer second — for a
    ; literal it can print.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE OUT-SHAPE IS get_accessed_property_name's AND SO IS ITS LIFETIME RULE:
    ; the answer is a borrowed run that must outlive the call. Two of the three arms
    ; of try_get_name_from_type answer memory that already does; the number arm is
    ; the one that does not, and it is the only one that copies (see there).
    ;
    ; ★ resolveEntityName is called with ignoreErrors — the reference's `true` — so
    ; an unresolvable name is a false and never a diagnostic; this is a name LOOKUP
    ; in the service of narrowing, not a check.
    procedure try_get_name_from_entity_name_expression(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], mutable out_name: ref[Slice[char]]) returns bool io reads checker_allow_umd_global_access linked
































































    ; tryGetNameFromType — the reference's two cases, which are
    ; getPropertyNameFromType's three arms under a different name and a different
    ; order. ★It is written over that function rather than beside it because the
    ; sets coincide EXACTLY: `UniqueESSymbol | StringOrNumberLiteral` is
    ; TypeFlagsStringOrNumberLiteralOrUnique, which is what
    ; is_type_usable_as_property_name asks, and `evaluator.AnyToString` of a string
    ; or number literal's value is the same text the property-name arm writes.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE COPY IS CONDITIONAL AND THE CONDITION IS PROVENANCE, not tidiness. A
    ; string literal's text and a unique symbol's name already live on the
    ; checker's page; a NUMBER has no stored text at all, so its arm writes into
    ; `nb` — a stack buffer that dies with this frame — and only that arm is copied.
    ; ★copy_name_to_page's own note argues the opposite for a name STORED in a
    ; symbol (*a name that is sometimes borrowed and sometimes owned is the shape
    ; §3.5i warns about*), and the two are not in conflict: what this out-cell
    ; promises is that the run OUTLIVES THE CALL, which both provenances satisfy,
    ; where a symbol's name has to outlive the checker.
    procedure try_get_name_from_type(mutable this, t: ref[Type], mutable out_name: ref[Slice[char]]) returns bool linked














