    ; getTypeAtFlowCall (slice 123). ★★ ITS TWO ANSWERS ARE DIFFERENT KINDS OF
    ; THING: an ASSERTION narrows the antecedent type, and a callee returning
    ; `never` makes this node unreachable — which is why the second one answers
    ; `unreachable_never_type` rather than a narrowing. Everything else answers nil
    ; — *this call does not affect the reference* — and that is not a stop.
    procedure get_type_at_flow_call(mutable this, mutable f: ref[FlowState], mutable flow: ref[FlowNode], mutable out_incomplete: ref[bool]) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked















































































    ; getTypeAtFlowCondition. The never early-out is REAL work and is written: it
    ; is what makes a reference after `if (false) { … }` answer at all.
    ; newFlowType, and it is not a constructor: this port carries a flow type as a
    ; (type, out_incomplete) pair, so what the reference's constructor DOES is the
    ; only thing left to port — the substitution below.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AN INCOMPLETE `never` BECOMES `silentNever`, AND get_type_at_flow_condition's
    ; own comment says this is the *only* place a silent never type is ever
    ; generated: a type guard met while a loop junction above is still being
    ; analyzed *could* be reachable once the type is complete, so the walk switches
    ; to the never that does NOT report when operators are applied to it. Without it
    ; a loop that narrows in one branch reports on the other — measured on
    ; `controlFlowWithIncompleteTypes` (TypeScript #11000), where the port invented
    ; two TS2339 and answered `string | number | boolean` for `string | number`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS DEAD CODE UNTIL SLICE 141 AND THAT IS WHY ONE SITE HAD IT AND SIX
    ; DID NOT: before the loop label, NOTHING in this port could set `incomplete`,
    ; so the substitution was unobservable everywhere (§3.5be) and the assignment
    ; arm's copy of it was the only one anybody had reason to write. **A propagating
    ; site needs no call — the value went through here where it was created** — so
    ; the seven are exactly the reference's seven `newFlowType` call sites.
    function new_flow_type(this, t: ref[Type], incomplete: bool) returns ref[Type] linked









    procedure get_type_at_flow_condition(mutable this, mutable f: ref[FlowState], mutable flow: ref[FlowNode], mutable out_incomplete: ref[bool]) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked








































    ; getTypeAtSwitchClause (slice 123) — the four-way fork over what the switch
    ; SUBJECT is: the reference itself, `typeof` of it, the literal `true`, or an
    ; expression that may carry a discriminant.
    procedure get_type_at_switch_clause(mutable this, mutable f: ref[FlowState], mutable flow: ref[FlowNode], mutable out_incomplete: ref[bool]) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked











































































































    ; getTypeAtFlowBranchLabel.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EARLY-OUT IS THE REFERENCE'S OWN: *"if the type at a particular
    ; antecedent path is the declared type and the reference is known to always be
    ; assigned … there is no reason to process more antecedents"*. Where it fires,
    ; the union never comes up.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 141 SUPPLIES THE TAIL AND WITH IT subtypeReduction, whose two notes
    ; here said it *"is not computed and cannot be: its only reader is the union
    ; call"* and that a one-member list is answered directly. Both are gone: the
    ; union call is written, so the flag has a reader — and the one-member shortcut
    ; was NOT equivalent, because getUnionOrEvolvingArrayType also finalizes an
    ; evolving array, recombines the unknown union and restores the declared type's
    ; identity. ★The bypass branch was the last stop in this function and slice
    ; 159 closes it — the exhaustiveness chapter the note named had been in the
    ; file since slice 123, which is the narrowing engine's own slice.
    procedure get_type_at_flow_branch_label(mutable this, mutable f: ref[FlowState], flow: ref[FlowNode], mutable antecedents: ref[FlowList], mutable out_incomplete: ref[bool]) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked


















































































































































    ; getTypeAtFlowLoopLabel — the cache, the key that keys it, and the in-process
    ; stack that makes a back edge terminate. Slice 141.
    ;
    ; ★ The SINGLE-antecedent loop label never reaches this function — the dispatch
    ; collapses it, exactly as upstream — so what arrives here is a loop with a real
    ; back edge.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST ANTECEDENT IS THE NON-LOOPING PATH AND EVERYTHING TERMINATES ON
    ; THAT. The reference says it twice, and the second time is the load-bearing
    ; one: an inner loop reached from an outer loop's back edge may find an EMPTY
    ; in-process list, in which case the analysis of the inner loop restarts — and
    ; it terminates because the first antecedent of the outer junction is the path
    ; that leads to the top, so the second visit finds a non-empty list.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY THIS STACK IS NOT THE GUARD FOR EVERY CYCLE, which
    ; slice 141 measured rather than assumed: with a probe on every entry, the shape
    ; `while (1) { bar = ~foo(...bar); }` reached this function sixty times with the
    ; in-process stack EMPTY at every arrival, because the re-entry happens while the
    ; FIRST antecedent is being analyzed — where upstream pushes nothing either. See
    ; get_type_at_flow_assignment's automatic branch for the stop that answers it and
    ; for what the successor has to find.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FLOW TYPE CACHE IS CLEARED AROUND EVERY LOOPING ANTECEDENT and the
    ; reason is in the reference's own comment: a value appearing in the loop cache
    ; during that walk is a sign that control flow analysis was RESTARTED and
    ; completed by checkExpressionCached, and the answer can then simply be picked
    ; up. Both halves are ported: the clear, and the pick-up test after the call.
    procedure get_type_at_flow_loop_label(mutable this, mutable f: ref[FlowState], mutable flow: ref[FlowNode], mutable out_incomplete: ref[bool]) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked
















































































































































    ; getTypeAtFlowArrayMutation, whole — slice 141.
