    ; ── the key property, and why it costs almost nothing here ──────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ getKeyPropertyName IS A GUARD BEFORE IT IS A LOOKUP, and the guard is
    ; the whole function on this corpus: computeKeyPropertyNameAndMap answers
    ; `missing` unless the union has TEN OR MORE constituents of which ten are
    ; object-like. The map behind it is a *speed* structure for very large
    ; discriminated unions — `getConstituentTypeForKeyType` is an O(1) route to a
    ; constituent the ordinary filter finds by walking — so the arms that read it
    ; all have a non-key path directly beneath them.
    ;
    ; ★ It is NOT memoised here where the reference memoises it on the union type,
    ; and the reason is that the computation is now the guard: three field reads
    ; and a count. The memo comes back with the candidate half.
    ; ★★★ THE MEMO IS BACK SINCE SLICE 130, AND IT HAS TO BE: the map is what
    ; getConstituentTypeForKeyType reads, and a second reader must see the SAME
    ; map — the entries are keyed by type identity, so rebuilding it would be
    ; correct but the duplicate sentinel makes it a decision, not a lookup. The
    ; note that stood here (*not memoised, the computation is now the guard*) was
    ; true only while the candidate half was a stop.
    procedure get_key_property_name(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type], mutable out_name: ref[Slice[char]]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked













    ; getConstituentTypeForKeyType — the O(1) route the map exists for. The
    ; reference's `unknownType` value is the DUPLICATE sentinel: two constituents
    ; carrying the same key select neither.
