    ; OVER `t.AsIntersectionType().types`. The stop that stood here was tagged
    ; `some-type` after the combinator rather than after anything missing, and the
    ; walk it stood in front of is the walk this same function already does one
    ; level up: `some_type_is_generic_with_union_constraint` folds the UNION, this
    ; folds the INTERSECTION, and the two are the reference's two `core.Some` call
    ; sites. Nothing was needed for it.
    ;
    ; ★ It answered TRUE while it stopped, which is the conservative half of a stop
    ; on a predicate — and conservative in the direction that costs the caller a
    ; distribution it did not need, never a wrong narrowing.
    procedure is_generic_type_with_union_constraint(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns bool io reads checker_no_unchecked_indexed_access linked
































    ; getNarrowableTypeForReference, WHOLE since slice 159, with isConstraintPosition
    ; and hasContextualTypeWithNoGenericTypes below it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ROW WAS THE CONTEXTUAL-TYPE CHAPTER AND SLICE 158 IS THAT CHAPTER —
    ; §3.5eo, and it is the sharpest form of the polarity §3.5gm names: the note
    ; read *"what is not [answerable] is the DISJUNCTION that guards it, whose
    ; second half is the contextual type chapter"*, which was an exact and honest
    ; statement of a dependency, made one slice before the dependency arrived.
    ; A note that names what it waits for cannot say when the wait is over.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE isNoInferType PEEL IS WRITTEN NOW FOR THE SAME REASON: its own half
    ; of the note said *"nothing in this port mints one"*, and
    ; `get_substitution_type` at the bottom of this file does — it is the producer
    ; `is_no_infer_type` was written against. The peel is `baseType` and not
    ; `getSubstitutionIntersection`: `NoInfer<T>` must narrow as T, not as the
    ; intersection with its `unknown` constraint.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DISJUNCTION IS SHORT-CIRCUITING AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS IT CHEAP.
    ; `isConstraintPosition` is four kind tests on the PARENT and answers most
    ; references without a type question at all; only its element-access arm and
    ; the contextual half ask the checker anything. So the expensive term is asked
    ; exactly where the reference asks it, and a `some_type_is_generic_with_union_
    ; constraint` of false — the answer for every non-generic reference there is —
    ; never reaches either.
    procedure get_narrowable_type_for_reference(mutable this, mutable t_in: ref[Type], mutable reference: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked






























    ; isConstraintPosition.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ELEMENT-ACCESS ARM IS THE ONLY ONE THAT ASKS A TYPE, AND ITS
    ; NEGATION IS THE POINT — the reference's own comment: *when both obj and x are
    ; of generic types T and K, we want the resulting type to be T[K]*. So an
    ; element access is a constraint position UNLESS substituting would destroy the
    ; indexed access, which is why the term is a conjunction of two positives under
    ; one `not`.
    procedure is_constraint_position(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type], mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked












































    ; someType(t, isGenericTypeWithoutNullableConstraint) and its predicate — the
    ; NEGATIVE twin of the union-constraint pair above, and written the same way
    ; for the same reason: `someType` distributes over a union and the predicate
    ; itself recurses into an INTERSECTION, so the two levels are not one loop.
    procedure some_type_is_generic_without_nullable_constraint(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns bool io reads checker_no_unchecked_indexed_access linked
























    procedure is_generic_type_without_nullable_constraint(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns bool io reads checker_no_unchecked_indexed_access linked
































    ; hasContextualTypeWithNoGenericTypes.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE JSX EXCLUSION IS A CIRCULARITY BREAK, in the reference's own words:
    ; *computing the contextual type for a child of a JSX element involves
    ; resolving the type of the element's tag name, so we exclude that here to
    ; avoid circularities.* It is the TAG NAME position specifically, not any node
    ; under a JSX element.
    ;
    ; ★ A nil contextual type answers FALSE and not true: the question is *is
    ; there a context and is it non-generic*, so no context is no permission to
    ; substitute.
    procedure has_contextual_type_with_no_generic_types(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked







































    ; removeOptionalityFromDeclaredType — the FOUR terms in the reference's own
    ; order, which is what keeps the fourth from being asked.
    procedure remove_optionality_from_declared_type(mutable this, mutable declared_type: ref[Type], mutable declaration: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked



























    ; parameterInitializerContainsUndefined. ★★★THE SECOND `computed` TEST IS THE
    ; REFERENCE'S OWN AND IS NOT REDUNDANT: checkDeclarationInitializer under it can
    ; re-enter this function for the same declaration and answer first, and the
    ; reference declines to overwrite what that inner call recorded.
    procedure parameter_initializer_contains_undefined(mutable this, mutable declaration: ref[AstNode]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked







































    ; checkIdentifier — the work list's largest row, taken to its three walls.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE STOP THAT STOOD AT THE DISPATCH WAS ONE ARGUMENT AND THE ARGUMENT WAS
    ; ABOUT ONE LINE. Slice 79 wired resolveName's one safe caller and wrote that
    ; this one *"passes a nameNotFoundMessage, so a MISS reports TS2304 — one
    ; invented diagnostic per lib name, corpus-wide"*. That is true of REPORTING the
    ; miss and not of meeting it: resolve_name's tail STOPS there instead, which
    ; loses a real TS2304 where the corpus has one and can never invent. The
    ; argument was for the report, and the report is the only thing that had to go.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT IS BEHIND IT IS THREE WALLS AND NOT ONE, WHICH IS THIS SLICE'S FIRST
    ; MEASUREMENT. Over stage 1's 651 arrivals: 400 MISS (61 %) — the globals table,
    ; §3.11 — 30 are a missing node, 75 stop inside the walk, and 146 RESOLVE. Of
    ; those 146, 121 are Variables, and a Variable in a non-assignment position
    ; falls through to getFlowTypeOfReferenceEx, i.e. `flow-graph`. So the arms that
    ; ANSWER are the ones the reference answers ABOVE the flow: a function, a class,
    ; an enum, a namespace, and a variable in a DEFINITE assignment position.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND THE REPORTS ARE THE PRODUCT RATHER THAN THE TYPE. Nine codes hang off
    ; the assignment-target block below and none of them needs a flow graph or a lib:
    ; they are decided by the resolved symbol's FLAGS.
    procedure check_identifier(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked





























































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































    procedure check_expression_worker(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked






































































































































































    ; The two string-literal `case` labels, which share a body in the reference and
    ; therefore share one here (§3.5bb: a shared record buys a shared BODY only —
    ; here it is the labels that are shared, and the body is the same one).
    ;
    ; ★★ isSkipDirectInferenceNode IS PROVABLY FALSE AND IS THEREFORE NOT ASKED.
    ; It is a membership test in `c.skipDirectInferenceNodes`, a set whose only
    ; writer is the inference machinery's `skipDirectInference` path; nothing here
    ; infers, so the set is empty and the blockedStringType arm cannot be taken.
    ; ★It is one of the few places in this port where the honest port of a test is
    ; NO test — the set does not exist, so a test against it would be a test
    ; against a literal false. The reader that changes that is the inference
    ; dimension, and it arrives with the set.
    procedure string_literal_arm(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]? linked


    ; `getFreshTypeOfLiteralType(getBigIntLiteralType(jsnum.NewPseudoBigInt(
    ; jsnum.ParsePseudoBigInt(node.Text()), false)))`, which is three routines
    ; deep and is written out because two of the three do something to the digits.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ParsePseudoBigInt IS THE STAND-IN SLICE 44 NAMED THIS ARM AS THE READER
    ; OF, and it is earned here (§3.5p). The scanner's own call on it is earned in
    ; the same commit — see set_bigint_token_value, and the note there for why the
    ; two together are belt and braces rather than a doubled conversion.
    ;
    ; ★★ NewPseudoBigInt TRIMS LEADING ZEROS AND ZERO IS THE EMPTY STRING, which is
    ; not tidiness: `PseudoBigInt`'s own comment says *"The value zero is
    ; represented as an empty string"*, and pseudo_big_int_to_string reads exactly
    ; that — a length test, not a value test. A port that stored `"0"` here would
    ; print `0n` correctly and compare UNEQUAL to a zero that came the other way.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `negative` WAS FALSE AT THE ONE CALL SITE AND IS NOT ANY MORE, and the
    ; note that stood here — *"a minus sign is a PrefixUnaryExpression, so the
    ; literal itself never carries one"* — was the whole reason slice 102's
    ; prefix-unary arm could reuse this function rather than write a second one:
    ; `-1n` is exactly that PrefixUnaryExpression, and the reference's arm is
    ; `getBigIntLiteralType(NewPseudoBigInt(ParsePseudoBigInt(text), true))`. The
    ; slot was filled from a parameter rather than hard-coded, so the second caller
    ; cost a parameter and nothing else.
    procedure bigint_literal_arm(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], negative: bool) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? linked
























    ; checkGrammarNumericLiteral, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS REPORT IS A SUGGESTION AND CANNOT REACH A C SECTION, which is why the
    ; whole function is ported and its measurement is zero: the last line is
    ; `c.addErrorOrSuggestion(false, …)`, and with isError false that sets
    ; CategorySuggestion and appends to `c.suggestionDiagnostics` — a list
    ; GetDiagnostics does not read. TS80008's own generated line says `Suggestion`.
    ; It is ported rather than skipped because the arm around it is ported, and a
    ; skipped step under a ported arm is the shape §3.5ae warns about: invisible
    ; from both sides.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO TEXTS ARE DIFFERENT TEXTS AND THE REFERENCE SAYS SO. The
    ; fractional test reads the RAW source (`scanner.GetTextOfNode`) because
    ; `node.Text` of `1100000000000000000000` is the scanner's cooked `1.1e21`,
    ; which contains a `.` the source does not — so asking the cooked text would
    ; return early on a literal that must be reported. The VALUE is read from the
    ; cooked text, because that is the one jsnum parses. Two questions, two texts,
    ; and swapping them is a wrong answer in both directions.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE COMPARISON IS ON THE BIT PATTERN and it is exact rather than
    ; approximate: jsnum_from_string answers an IEEE-754 double's BITS, and for two
    ; non-negative finite doubles the unsigned ordering of the bits IS the ordering
    ; of the values. MaxSafeInteger = 2^53 - 1 is 0x433FFFFFFFFFFFFF. A literal is
    ; never negative (the minus sign is a PrefixUnaryExpression) and never NaN, so
    ; the two exclusions the bit trick needs are properties of the input.
    procedure check_grammar_numeric_literal(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode]) linked























    ; The suggestion sink. It counts and drops, and BOTH halves are the point: a
    ; suggestion must not reach the C section, and a suggestion site that did
    ; nothing at all would be indistinguishable from an unported one.
    ;
    ; ★ The counter is the only way a control can prove this function ran — see
    ; slice 70's g-rows.
    procedure record_suggestion(mutable this, code: int) linked


    ; checkGrammarBigIntLiteral, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS REPORT IS DEAD UNDER THIS HARNESS AND THE PROOF IS ONE COMPARISON:
    ; `c.languageVersion < core.ScriptTargetES2020` with languageVersion at the
    ; DERIVED DEFAULT ES2025 = 12 and ES2020 = 7. So TS2737 cannot appear, and the
    ; function's answer is false for every input. ★It is the fourth reading of an
    ; unset option in this file (see ScriptTarget's note) and the second that kills
    ; a branch outright rather than steering one.
    ;
    ; ★ The literalType test is two kind comparisons and is ported because it is
    ; the reference's own short-circuit: a bigint in `type T = 1n` or `type T = -1n`
    ; is exempt whatever the target is, so it is not part of what the target kills.
    procedure check_grammar_bigint_literal(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool reads shared_lock linked



















    ; ── slice 88: THE BINARY EXPRESSION ─────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkBinaryExpression is three lines; checkBinaryLikeExpression is the
    ; chapter — two operand checks, a pre-pass for the logical and coalescing
    ; operators, and a switch over sixteen operator groups.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO GROUPS CAN ANSWER AND FOURTEEN CANNOT, AND ONE MISSING TYPE DRAWS
    ; THE LINE. `booleanType` is the SEVENTH line of NewChecker that
    ; initialize_intrinsic_types does not transcribe — it is
    ; `getUnionType(trueType, falseType)`, and that note already said so — so every
    ; comparison and equality arm has no value to return; and every arithmetic arm
    ; reaches isTypeAssignableToKind, which is isTypeAssignableTo, which is the
    ; assignability RELATION that slice 70 named as a dimension. What is left is
    ; `=`, which answers rightType, and `,`, which answers rightType.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE HISTOGRAM SAYS THOSE TWO ARE THE CHAPTER. The row was opened as
    ; a histogram the way slice 70 opened its own — `record_unported` was pointed at
    ; the OPERATOR kind for one run instead of at KindBinaryExpression — and the
    ; forty-one operators the corpus holds behind this stop are not evenly spread:
    ; **`=` alone is 245 of the 413 stop events and 98 of the 142 units**, `,` is
    ; six more units, and the whole remaining thirty-nine operators together are
    ; thirty-eight units. ★The two arms that can answer are the two that need no
    ; type at all, which is not a coincidence to pass over: the row was never
    ; waiting on the relation, it was waiting on the DISPATCH.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AN OPERAND'S STOP DOES NOT ABORT THE FUNCTION, and that is what the slice
    ; is worth rather than a detail of style. `x = 1` checks an Identifier on the
    ; left, which stops at check-identifier (the globals table, §3.11) — so
    ; `leftType` is nil for most of the corpus — while the reference's sequence
    ; reaches checkAssignmentOperator regardless, and TS2364 is decided by the
    ; NODE. Bailing out at the first nil would have made this whole chapter
    ; invisible behind a table it does not need. It is the discipline
    ; check_variable_declaration's tail already carries, one level down.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHERE A nil TYPE *IS* LOAD-BEARING IT IS TESTED, at three places and each
    ; for its own reason: the silentNever short-circuit compares an identity that a
    ; nil cannot have, checkTruthinessOfType reads flags off the type, and
    ; checkForDisallowedESSymbolOperand asks maybeTypeOfKind. Each skips rather
    ; than guesses — skipping loses a report, and diagcheck's subsequence relation
    ; is green on a LOSS and red on an INVENTION.

    ; checkBinaryExpression, whole.
    ;
    ; ★ The three slots are non-optional in BinaryExpressionData; the accessors
    ; answer nil only through their union DEFAULT arm, which the caller's own kind
    ; test has already excluded. The report is that claim's holder rather than a
    ; live path — a row that would appear if it were false.
    procedure check_binary_expression(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked






















    ; ── the two UNARY expressions (slice 102) ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THEY ARE HERE BECAUSE THEY ARE checkArithmeticOperandType'S OTHER TWO
    ; READERS, and that is what makes this a chapter rather than two arms: the
    ; `++`/`--` arm of the prefix expression and the whole of the postfix one are
    ; the SAME four lines the binary arithmetic arm's tail is, over one operand
    ; instead of two. `getUnaryResultType` between them is the third reader of
    ; numberOrBigIntType. A slice that wired the binary arms and left these would
    ; have minted a field for one of its three consumers.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE LITERAL FOLD IN FRONT OF THE SWITCH IS THE PREFIX ARM'S OWN PRODUCT
    ; AND IT IS THE HALF NO RELATION TOUCHES: `-1` is a NumberLiteral type of value
    ; −1, not `number` and not a negation applied later, and `-1n` is the one place
    ; in the whole port where a PseudoBigInt is minted with its sign set. Both
    ; answer BEFORE the operator switch, so every `-1` in the corpus leaves this
    ; function without asking a single question about kinds.

    ; getUnaryResultType, whole.
    procedure get_unary_result_type(mutable this, mutable operand_type: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked


























    ; The `++`/`--` tail both unary expressions end in — three lines shared because
    ; the reference writes them twice, character for character.
    ;
    ; ★ The `ok` guard is checkArithmeticOperandType's own, which under this port
    ; is FALSE for every input (see that function's header and §3.5ev's finding
    ; one), so `checkReferenceExpression` is unreachable from here today. It is
    ; written rather than deferred because the day the relation's union half lands
    ; it becomes live with no edit, and because a guard whose reason is another
    ; function's wall belongs beside the call it guards.
    procedure check_increment_operand(mutable this, mutable operand: ref[AstNode], mutable operand_type: ref[Type]) io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked











    ; checkPrefixUnaryExpression, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `+` OPERATOR'S TS2736 IS THE ONE REPORT HERE WHOSE MESSAGE ARGUMENT IS
    ; A TYPE NAME, and it is dropped for report_relation_error's reason: the name
    ; decides nothing, the C section carries pos/end/code, and asking the printer
    ; could only turn a report into a row.
    procedure check_prefix_unary_expression(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? io reads shared_lock linked



























































































    ; checkPostfixUnaryExpression, whole — and it is checkPrefixUnaryExpression's
    ; increment arm with no switch in front of it.
    procedure check_postfix_unary_expression(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked


















    ; ── the DESTRUCTURING ASSIGNMENT (slice 145) ─────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `[a, b] = xs`, `({a} = o)`, `({a, ...rest} = o)` and every nesting of them,
    ; plus the for-of head that is one of those. Six functions, mutually recursive
    ; through checkDestructuringAssignment, and the whole chapter was ONE stop with
    ; two rows (the object-literal kind and the array-literal kind).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LEFT SIDE IS NOT AN EXPRESSION AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE CHAPTER.
    ; `{a} = o` parses as an object LITERAL, so the ordinary binary path would ask
    ; for its type and check assignability against it; the reference leaves
    ; checkBinaryLikeExpression before that and re-reads the literal as a PATTERN,
    ; taking each element's type out of the SOURCE by property name or index and
    ; recursing. Nothing here asks the pattern for a type.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THREE HEADS OF checkDestructuringAssignment ARE THREE DIFFERENT
    ; TARGETS AND EACH ONE MOVES `sourceType`. A shorthand `{a = 1}` checks its own
    ; default through checkBinaryLikeExpression and then strips undefined off the
    ; source; a `[a = 1]` element is a BinaryExpression whose whole self is checked
    ; and whose LEFT becomes the target, stripping undefined again; anything else is
    ; the target as it stands. Only after that is the target's kind dispatched on.
    ;
    ; ★★ EVERY SLOT THE REFERENCE DEREFERENCES UNCONDITIONALLY IS A ROW HERE, not a
    ; silent `null`: `destructuring-slot-missing` names the kind. A shorthand's Name
    ; and EqualsToken, a default's Left, a spread's Expression and the two element
    ; lists are all non-nil in any tree the parser produces, so the row should never
    ; fire — which is precisely why it must be a row and not a bail. A `null` with no
    ; stop is an answer, and answering nothing about a pattern the walk did enter is
    ; the shape §3.5cz calls an unmeasured claim.
    ;
    ; ★ markPropertyAsReferenced is CALLED in the reference and is not ported, for
    ; the reason its neighbours already carry: its entire effect is
    ; `symbolReferenceLinks.Get(target).referenceKinds |= SymbolFlagsAll`, and every
    ; reader of that table is in the checkUnusedIdentifiers family, which this port
    ; does not have. ★★It cannot interact with the one row that does name the table
    ; (check_unused_renamed_binding_elements' `symbol-reference-links`): that drain
    ; asks about a BINDING ELEMENT's symbol, and this writer only ever marks a
    ; CLASS MEMBER — `prop.Flags&ClassMember == 0` returns on its first line.
    procedure check_destructuring_assignment(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], mutable source_type_in: ref[Type], check_mode: int, right_is_this: bool) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked




























































































    ; checkObjectLiteralAssignment. ★The EMPTY pattern is the one arm that answers
    ; something other than the source: `({} = o)` under strictNullChecks is a
    ; non-null assertion and nothing else, which is why the report it can make is
    ; checkNonNullType's and not this function's.
    procedure check_object_literal_assignment(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], mutable source_type: ref[Type], right_is_this: bool) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked






















    ; checkObjectLiteralDestructuringPropertyAssignment — one property of an object
    ; pattern, and the reference's own note is the reason `all_properties` is a
    ; parameter of its own: *if property cannot be a SpreadAssignment, then
    ; allProperties does not need to be provided.*
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ELEMENT TYPE IS TAKEN TWICE OVER AND THE SECOND READ IS THE FLOW'S.
    ; getIndexedAccessTypeEx answers the DECLARED element type; getFlowTypeOfDestructuring
    ; then narrows it through the synthetic `o["x"]` reference, which is the machinery
    ; slice 117 built. Skipping the second read would lose every narrowing a
    ; destructuring assignment inherits from its source.
    ;
    ; ★ `AccessFlagsAllowMissing` is conditional on hasDefaultValue and nothing
    ; else: `{a = 1} = o` may read a property `o` does not have, `{a} = o` may not.
    procedure check_object_literal_destructuring_property_assignment(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], mutable object_literal_type: ref[Type], property_index: int, all_properties: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, right_is_this: bool) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked


























































































































    ; checkArrayLiteralAssignment.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO ELEMENT TYPES, AND THE SECOND ONE IS ASKED LAZILY BECAUSE ASKING IT
    ; REPORTS. `possiblyOutOfBoundsType` comes from one
    ; checkIteratedTypeOrElementType with PossiblyOutOfBounds set — which is also
    ; the call that CHECKS the source is iterable at all, so it must happen once and
    ; unconditionally. The in-bounds type is the same question without that flag,
    ; and under noUncheckedIndexedAccess it is asked only when a SPREAD element
    ; needs it: a second call would run the iterability check, and its reports, a
    ; second time.
    procedure check_array_literal_assignment(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], mutable source_type: ref[Type], check_mode: int) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_no_unchecked_indexed_access linked













































    ; checkArrayLiteralDestructuringElementAssignment — one element of an array
    ; pattern.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARRAY-LIKE ARM SYNTHESIZES AN EXPRESSION AND THE REFERENCE SAYS WHY:
    ; *we create a synthetic expression so that getIndexedAccessType doesn't get
    ; confused when the element is a SyntaxKind.ElementAccessExpression.* The access
    ; NODE is what an indexed-access report points at, and for `[o[0]] = xs` the
    ; element is itself an element access — pointing the report at it would name the
    ; wrong access.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REST ELEMENT'S TYPE IS A TUPLE SLICE WHERE IT CAN BE AND AN ARRAY
    ; OTHERWISE, and the test is `everyType(sourceType, isTupleType)`: slicing a
    ; tuple keeps the remaining elements' individual types, while
    ; `createArrayType(elementType)` collapses them into one. The port already had
    ; the predicate as every_type_is_tuple.
    ;
    ; ★ A HOLE — `[, a] = xs` — is an OmittedExpression and the whole function is
    ; skipped for it, which is not the same as checking it against `undefined`.
    procedure check_array_literal_destructuring_element_assignment(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode], mutable source_type: ref[Type], element_index: int, mutable element_type: ref[Type], check_mode: int) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_no_unchecked_indexed_access linked

































































































    ; checkReferenceAssignment — the LEAF of the chapter: the target is an ordinary
    ; reference, so it is checked as an expression and the source is checked against
    ; it. ★The two diagnostics are chosen by the target's PARENT and not by the
    ; target: inside a `{...x}` the message names the object-rest form.
    procedure check_reference_assignment(mutable this, mutable target: ref[AstNode], mutable source_type: ref[Type], check_mode: int) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked

























    ; checkBinaryLikeExpression. The head, the pre-pass and the switch.
    ;
    ; ★★ `errorNode` IS OPTIONAL UPSTREAM AND IS OPTIONAL HERE: checkDestructuring
    ; Assignment's shorthand arm passes nil. The one report in this slice's prefix
    ; that lands ON it — the equality arm's TS2839 — therefore goes through
    ; error_on_node_or_null, which DROPS a nil, and that is the faithful answer
    ; rather than a leniency: `c.error(nil, …)` builds a diagnostic with no file,
    ; which lands in the GLOBAL list and never appears among the reference's own C
    ; lines. The reporter family's header states the same rule from its own side.
    procedure check_binary_like_expression(mutable this, mutable left: ref[AstNode], operator_token: ref[AstNode], mutable right: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?) @page true @direct true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked




































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































    ; `ast.IsParenthesizedExpression(parent) || ast.IsLogicalOrCoalescingBinary
    ; Expression(parent)`, the pre-pass walk's loop condition.
    function is_parenthesis_or_logical_chain(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool linked









    ; `leftType == c.silentNeverType || rightType == c.silentNeverType`.
    ;
    ; ★ A nil operand type cannot BE silentNeverType — nothing answers that type
    ; but the arms that return it — so a nil answers false here, which is the
    ; conservative direction: it lets the arm fall through to its own stop instead
    ; of claiming an answer.
    function either_is(a: ref[Type]?, b: ref[Type]?, t: ref[Type]) returns bool linked














    ; The switch's four operator GROUPS, as four predicates. They are written out
    ; rather than derived from the Kind ranges because the reference's `case`
    ; labels are not contiguous runs: the arithmetic group interleaves each
    ; operator with its compound assignment, and `+`/`+=` sit in a group of their
    ; own between them.
    function is_arithmetic_or_bitwise_operator(k: int) returns bool linked














































    ; The arithmetic arm's shift switch (slice 102) — the six operators whose tail
    ; asks the CONSTANT EVALUATOR. It is not `isShiftOperator` upstream, which
    ; names the three non-assigning ones only; the switch lists all six, so the
    ; predicate does too and carries the arm's name rather than the reference's.
    function is_shift_operator(k: int) returns bool linked














    ; isShiftOperator / isShiftOperatorOrHigher — the reference's five-function
    ; ladder (exponentiation, multiplicative, additive, shift) flattened into the
    ; one predicate that has a reader here, §3.5u's reading of a chain whose links
    ; differ only in which kinds they add.
    function is_shift_operator_or_higher(k: int) returns bool linked




















    ; ast.IsAssignmentExpression(node, excludeCompoundAssignment: true) — the one
    ; polarity this port has a caller for.
    function is_assignment_expression_excluding_compound(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked








    function is_plus_operator(k: int) returns bool linked






    function is_relational_operator(k: int) returns bool linked










    function is_equality_operator(k: int) returns bool linked










    ; The six that produce a UNION: the three operators and their three compound
    ; assignments. It is not ast.IsLogicalOrCoalescingBinaryOperator — that one is
    ; the pre-pass's question and excludes the compound spellings.
    function is_logical_or_coalescing_operator(k: int) returns bool linked






    ; Whether the kind is `&&` or `&&=` — the one of the three logical groups whose
    ; answer is built from the LEFT operand's definitely-falsy part.
    function is_logical_and_operator(k: int) returns bool linked






    ; The FACT each of the six logical spellings asks its left operand for. It is
    ; not a function upstream — the three switch arms name their own constant — and
    ; it is one here because this port asks the question from ONE arm, which is the
    ; §3.5u reading of a Go switch whose arms differ in a single value.
    ;
    ; ★ There is no default: the caller has already established
