    ; ★★★ AND THE STOP USED TO BE A DEFAULT WHERE THE REFERENCE HAS NO ARM AT ALL
    ; (slice 134). The line below said *"a decorator its expression"* and the
    ; reference's `switch` has FOUR arms — TaggedTemplate, the two Jsx kinds,
    ; BinaryExpression, Call/New — and no Decorator among them, so a decorator
    ; walks NOTHING and answers anySignature. Reading the default as unported made
    ; an untyped decorator (`@d` on an `any`) a stop, which is nine units and the
    ; one wall the decorator chapter left behind for no reason. **A stop is a claim
    ; about the reference, and a claim about a `switch` has to name the arms.**
    ; resolveInstanceofExpression. ★★★ THE ONLY BINARY EXPRESSION resolveSignature
    ; IS EVER ASKED ABOUT IS AN `instanceof`, and the reference does not test for
    ; it: checkInstanceOfExpression is the one caller that hands a binary node to
    ; getResolvedSignature, so the arm's kind IS the operator. The port keeps that
    ; — a test here would be a claim about callers rather than about the node.
    ;
    ; ★ The fall-through answer is anySignature and not an error: an `instanceof`
    ; whose right operand is a plain class has no `[Symbol.hasInstance]` method, and
    ; that is the ordinary case rather than a failure. The reference's own comment
    ; is *fall back to a default signature*.
