    ; invocationError and invocationErrorDetails, folded into one procedure — and
    ; the fold is a claim about this port's DIAGNOSTIC, not a shortcut.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REFERENCE BUILDS A CHAIN AND THE DUMP CARRIES ITS HEAD. Every path
    ; through invocationErrorDetails ends in
    ; `NewDiagnosticChainForNode(diagnostic, target, headMessage)`, whose outermost
    ; message is the head and whose LOC is `target`'s at every branch — so the `C`
    ; line is (target's range, head code) whether the apparent type is a union or
    ; not. The union arm's own three codes (TS2757/TS2758, TS2759/TS2760, TS2762) and
    ; the awaited-type test exist to compose the chain BELOW the head and to add
    ; related information, neither of which the fifth yardstick compares. That is why
    ; this port emits one diagnostic and reports no row: the union branch changes
    ; nothing it can observe.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONE THING THAT DOES MOVE THE HEAD IS THE GET-ACCESSOR ARM, and it is
    ; ported: `f()` where `f` is a getter reports TS6234 rather than TS2349. Its guard
    ; is a call with ZERO arguments, which is what makes it a suggestion about the
    ; PARENTHESES rather than about the callee.
    ;
    ; ★ invocationErrorRecovery is not written: it adds related information on an
    ; originating import, and it reads exportTypeLinks — a table this port does not
    ; keep.
    procedure invocation_error(mutable this, mutable error_target: ref[AstNode], apparent_type: ref[Type], kind: int) reads ordinal_lock linked































    ; resolveCall, down to the one arm of chooseOverload this slice ports.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CALL STATE IS NOT A RECORD HERE AND THAT IS A CONSEQUENCE OF THE
    ; BOUNDARY. Upstream's `CallState` carries nine fields, five of which exist only
    ; so that the FAILURE pass can pick the best candidate to report on —
    ; candidatesForArgumentError, candidateForArgumentArityError,
    ; candidateForTypeArgumentError — and that pass is
    ; `getCandidateForOverloadFailure` plus `reportCallResolutionErrors`, this
    ; slice's wall. With one candidate and no failure reporting, what is left is four
    ; locals.
    ;
    ; ★★ THREE OF THE REFERENCE'S OWN LINES ARE CONSTANT UNDER THIS HARNESS and are
    ; written as their constant with the reason at the line: `reportErrors` is
    ; `!isInferencePartiallyBlocked && candidatesOutArray == nil` and no caller here
    ; passes an out array; `signatureHelpTrailingComma` needs
    ; CheckModeIsForSignatureHelp, which only the language service sets; and
    ; `argCheckMode` is Normal because its own guard excludes a single non-generic
    ; candidate.
    ;
    ; ★ reorderCandidates is the identity on ONE candidate — the loop body inserts at
    ; index 0 of an empty result — so it is not written as a function; the row stands
    ; at the >1 case, which is where its five index registers start to mean something.
    ; ── slice 131: the OVERLOAD SET ─────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ resolveCall's SHAPE CHANGES WITH MORE THAN ONE CANDIDATE, WHICH IS WHY
    ; THE PER-CANDIDATE BODY HAD TO MOVE OUT OF resolve_call. With one candidate a
    ; failure IS the answer, so the old body reported at the line that failed; with
    ; several, a failure is a `continue` and only the EMPTY loop is a failure of the
    ; call. Keeping the reports where they were would have marked a unit unported
    ; because the FIRST overload did not match.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THAT IS THE PART A ROW CANNOT SHOW: the row `reorder-candidates` named
    ; a 49-line function, and what it was actually holding was every call in the
    ; corpus to an OVERLOADED function — 210 units of stage 2 — because the port had
    ; no way to say *this candidate did not match, try the next*.
    ;
    ; ★ The state record is the reference's CallState minus its five failure slots:
    ; candidatesForArgumentError, candidateForArgumentArityError and
    ; candidateForTypeArgumentError exist only for the FAILURE PASS
    ; (getCandidateForOverloadFailure + reportCallResolutionErrors), which is still
    ; a row — so what a failed loop owes is one stop and not a best-candidate
    ; choice.
    ; ★The candidate list is the CALLER's (slice 344): it is read out and dropped
    ; inside `resolve_call`, so the frame that outlives the ordering owns it and
    ; the list goes back with that frame. A list built here and RETURNED cannot be
    ; frame-local — a returned value lives on the caller's page, which is the
    ; long-lived one.
    procedure reorder_candidates(mutable this, mutable signatures: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]], call_chain_flags: int, mutable result: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked




































































































