    ; ── slice 124: checkComputedPropertyName, and the LATE-BINDABLE NAME ──────
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS A PROCEDURE BECAUSE ITS SECOND HALF WAS A CHAPTER, AND THE
    ; CHAPTER IS THIS SLICE. The old note said a slice answering a computed name
    ; *would have to answer it in the late-binding chapter too*; that is exactly
    ; right, so both arrive here — this function and the isLateBindableName /
    ; hasBindableName trio below it, which is what four other stops were waiting
    ; on (check_function_or_method_declaration, check_accessor_declaration,
    ; check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name and the enum-member loop).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CACHE IS KEYED ON THE EXPRESSION AND NOT ON THE NAME NODE, which is
    ; the reference's own choice and load-bearing here: `[k]` appearing as a
    ; property name and the same expression checked through checkExpression must
    ; share one answer, or the diagnostic below is emitted twice. The second slot
    ; is slice 88's stop bit, for the reason TypeNodeLink's note gives in full — a
    ; nil here means EITHER not-yet-computed OR the check stopped, and recomputing
    ; a stopped check re-emits every diagnostic it raised on the way to the stop.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `in` FORK IS A GRAMMAR ARM WEARING A TYPE FUNCTION'S CLOTHES: inside a
    ; type literal, a class or an interface, `[k in T]` is a MAPPED type the parser
    ; has already mis-parsed as a computed name with a binary `in`, so the
    ; reference answers errorType and says nothing rather than reporting TS2464
    ; about a name the author never wrote. The accessor exclusion is the reference's
    ; too (`get [k in T]()` is not that shape).
    procedure check_computed_property_name(mutable this, mutable node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]? io reads ordinal_lock linked



















































































