    ; ── slice 79: resolveName — THE NAME RESOLVER ────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE KEYSTONE, NAMED TWICE BEFORE IT WAS WRITTEN. Slice 75 called it *"the
    ; real chain … whose first link is not a checker question"* and
    ; check_var_declared_names_not_shadowed's note above calls that caller *"the SAFE
    ; first caller of a future Resolve"*. Both sentences are what this slice is
    ; built on, and the second one is the whole reason the slice can exist at all
    ; while §3.11 is still open.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS NOT A CHECKER FUNCTION UPSTREAM: `c.resolveName` is a FIELD bound to
    ; `binder.NameResolver.Resolve`, a struct in package binder whose twelve
    ; callback fields the checker fills (createNameResolver). The indirection buys
    ; upstream two more consumers — the language service's auto-import extractor and
    ; the reference resolver, each with its own `Lookup` — and this port has
    ; neither, so the walk is a Checker method and the two callbacks that matter
    ; (`Lookup` = getSymbol, `GetSymbolOfDeclaration`) are the methods it already
    ; has. ★The struct is named here rather than modelled: a record of delegate
    ; fields with one filler is machinery no instrument can reach (§3.5be), and the
    ; day a second consumer arrives is the day the fields are the right shape.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MISSING GLOBALS TABLE IS THE ONE DIVERGENCE, IT IS ARGUED RATHER THAN
    ; REPORTED, AND THE ARGUMENT IS A CONTAINMENT PROOF — see lookup_globals. The
    ; short form: this port's walk is the reference's walk MINUS its last step, so
    ; the set of symbols it can answer is a SUBSET of the reference's, and every
    ; caller's report is therefore a subsequence of the reference's. A stop there
    ; instead would block every unit whose lookup misses locally, which is most of
    ; them — i.e. it would buy nothing and cost the slice.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THAT PROOF IS WHY EXACTLY ONE CALLER IS WIRED. The other two known
    ; ones fail it in opposite ways. `checkIdentifier` → `getResolvedSymbol` passes a
    ; nameNotFoundMessage, so a miss REPORTS TS2304 — inventing a diagnostic per lib
    ; name, corpus-wide, which is the one direction diagcheck does catch.
    ; `reportImplicitAny`'s TS7051 branch is subtler and fails just as hard: a miss
    ; falls through to a DIFFERENT report (TS7006/TS7019), so the port would not
    ; lose a line, it would emit the wrong one. Both keep their stops, and both
    ; notes now say which test they are waiting for.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS OMITTED IS OMITTED BY §3.5ao — a computed value whose only reader
    ; is unported is named at its own line rather than stored: `withinDeferredContext`
    ; (and with it getIsDeferredContext), `lastSelfReferenceLocation` (and
    ; isSelfReferenceLocation), `associatedDeclarationForContainingInitializerOr
    ; BindingName` (and with it the Parameter and BindingElement arms, whose whole
    ; body is that one assignment) and `propertyWithInvalidInitializer` (and with it
    ; the PropertyDeclaration arm). All four are read ONLY by the three
    ; message-gated callbacks, so with a nil message the reference computes them and
    ; throws them away.

    ; The bytes of a name against a C literal — `name == "const"`, `name ==
    ; InternalSymbolNameDefault`. AstNode.text_is' loop, over a (pointer, length)
    ; pair instead of over a node.
    function name_bytes_are(name_data: Slice[char], t: Slice[char]) returns bool linked















    ; `name == someNode.Text()`, which the class-expression, function-expression
    ; and infer-type arms all ask. The node's text is read through the same two
    ; accessor pairs AstNode.text_is uses.
    function name_bytes_match_node(name_data: Slice[char], n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool linked















    ; ast.IsConstAssertion. Its `isConstTypeReference(location.Type())` is the
    ; parser's, ported in slice 61 for the quick-type path.
    function is_const_assertion(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked









    ; ast.IsModuleOrEnumDeclaration.
    function is_module_or_enum_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked







    ; ast.FindAncestorKind, written out as the walk it is (§3.9d). It starts AT the
    ; node, as FindAncestor does.
    function find_ancestor_kind(node: ref[AstNode]?, kind: int) returns ref[AstNode]? linked











    ; ── checker.getSymbol — the `Lookup` the reference binds into the resolver ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ALIAS ARM IS THE ONE STOP INSIDE THE WALK, AND UNDER THIS HARNESS IT
    ; IS NOT A CORNER. `getSymbolFlags` follows an alias to its target and answers
    ; **SymbolFlagsAll** when that target is the unknown symbol — which, with a stub
    ; program that resolves no module, is what EVERY `import … from "m"` comes to. So
    ; the reference's answer for an alias found under any meaning at all is *yes,
    ; this symbol* — and this port cannot say so without resolveAlias, which is
    ; resolveExternalModuleName and the module dimension. ★It is not decidable from
    ; the flags either: `import A = N.B` resolves LOCALLY and its target's flags are
    ; whatever N.B is.
    ;
    ; ★ It fires only when the name being resolved is found in some table on the walk
    ; AND that entry is a pure alias whose own flags miss the meaning — i.e. when an
    ; import and a variable share a name. The stop is what keeps that unit off the
    ; matched count instead of answering null and losing the report silently.
    procedure lookup_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbols: ref[SymbolTable]?, mutable name_data: Slice[char], meaning: int) returns ref[Symbol]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked






















    ; ★★★ A LOOKUP THAT STOPPED IS NOT A MISS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A **WRONG
    ; SYMBOL** RATHER THAN A MISSING ONE (slice 105). `lookup_symbol` reports at the
    ; one place it cannot follow the reference — `getSymbolFlags` on an ALIAS whose
    ; target it cannot resolve — and then answers null. resolve_name's walk reads
    ; null as *not in this table* and takes one step OUTWARD, where upstream never
    ; steps at all: there `getSymbolFlags` answers SymbolFlagsAll for an alias it
    ; could not resolve, so the symbol IS returned and the walk ends.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL AND ONLY STAGE 2 COULD SEE IT. `import {E} from
    ; "./f1"; export type E = E;` binds ONE local symbol carrying both declarations
    ; and flagged ALIAS, plus an EXPORT symbol flagged TypeAlias. Upstream answers
    ; the local and takes getTypeReferenceType's alias arm; this port stopped on the
    ; local, walked on to the module's exports, found the TYPE ALIAS — and then
    ; slice 105's chapter resolved it against itself and INVENTED a TS2456 where the
    ; reference reports only the missing module. Two units of 18 314, and no
    ; stage-1 unit at all.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FIX IS THE MARK AND NOT THE MISSING ARM. `getSymbolFlags` is a chapter
    ; of its own (119 events / 56 units at stage 1) and porting it here would be a
    ; second slice; what this owes is that the stop it already records is READ, so
    ; the walk ends where the reference's ends and the unit gets an honest UNPORTED.
    procedure lookup_in_table(mutable this, mutable symbols: ref[SymbolTable]?, mutable name_data: Slice[char], meaning: int, mutable stopped: ref[bool], for_suggestion: bool) returns ref[Symbol]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked












    ; ── initializeChecker, slice 104: the globals table's BUILDABLE HALF ──────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DEFERRAL THIS DISCHARGES WAS WRITTEN AT THIS VERY LINE BY SLICE 91,
    ; AND IT NAMED ITS OWN EXPIRY: *"a GLOBAL (non-module) source file's own locals
    ; are merged into globals upstream, and `is_global_source_file` is exactly why
    ; the loop above skips them — so a script file's top-level names are unreachable
    ; here on BOTH routes. Giving this function the file's locals when the file is a
    ; global source file would be a strict improvement and still a subset of
    ; upstream's table; it is deliberately not done in this slice, because it would
    ; mix a measurable change into the one commit whose value is the walk."* That
    ; commit is thirteen slices old; this is the measurable change on its own.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT IS **NOT** HERE IS THE LIB, AND THAT IS STILL §3.11 RATHER THAN AN
    ; EFFORT ESTIMATE. `tests/oracle/types.go` builds its program out of
    ; lib.es5.d.ts plus the unit, and every file of it that is not a module has its
    ; top-level locals merged into ONE table. This Checker has the unit alone, so
    ; `Array`, `String`, `console` and every other lib name still miss. §3.11 says
    ; report that, do not route around it — and resolve_name's tail does exactly
    ; that, one function up, for the reason slice 91 wrote there.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE CONTAINMENT PROOF SURVIVES THE CHANGE, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON
    ; THIS CAN LAND WITHOUT A NEW STOP. Upstream's table is the union over the
    ; program's non-module files; ours is one of that union's members. A subset of a
    ; table cannot answer a name the superset does not have, so every symbol we now
    ; resolve is a symbol the reference resolves TO THE SAME DECLARATION — the walk
    ; and the tables are both functions of trees both sides have identically. A miss
    ; is still a report we do not make, which is a legal subsequence.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONE PLACE THE SUBSET ARGUMENT DOES NOT COVER IS A COLLISION, and it is
    ; a stop rather than a guess — see merge_global_symbol.
    procedure initialize_checker(mutable this) io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked




















































































































































    ; `len(file.ModuleAugmentations) != 0`, asked of the unit. ★★★ THE COLLECTOR IS
    ; THE PARSER's (`collectModuleReferences` in references.go) AND NOT THE
    ; BINDER's, which is why the question is re-asked here rather than read off a
    ; field: this port has no references pass, and the two conditions that decide
    ; whether an ambient module is an AUGMENTATION rather than a DECLARATION are
    ; the collector's own.
    ;
    ; ★ The nesting is ONE level and that is the collector's too: it recurses into
    ; an ambient module's body only when the file is NOT an external module, and
    ; only there can a nested ambient module with a non-relative name be an
    ; augmentation.
    function file_has_module_augmentation(this) returns bool linked




















    function statement_is_module_augmentation(this, node: ref[AstNode], in_ambient_module: bool) returns bool linked


















































    ; ── mergeModuleAugmentation (slice 137) ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS ASKED OF THE MODULE **NAME** AND WORKS ON ITS PARENT, which is the
    ; shape `file.ModuleAugmentations` has: the parser collects the string literal
    ; (or the `global` identifier), not the declaration. Every read below goes
    ; through the parent, so the argument is a HANDLE and never the subject.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST GUARD IS THE ONE THAT MAKES THE PASS IDEMPOTENT AND IT IS NOT
    ; OBVIOUS: `symbol.Declarations[0] != moduleNode` returns early, because two
    ; `declare module "m"` blocks in ONE file share a symbol that already carries
    ; both declarations — so the merge is performed for the FIRST declaration and
    ; skipped for every other. Drop it and the second block merges the accumulated
    ; symbol a second time, which appends every declaration twice.
    procedure merge_module_augmentation(mutable this, mutable module_name: ref[AstNode]) io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked
























































































    ; `file.ModuleAugmentations` — the parser's collector (`collectModuleReferences`),
    ; written out as the walk it is.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS **NOT** `statement_is_module_augmentation` READ AS A COLLECTOR, AND
    ; THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS SLICE MADE EXACTLY THAT MISTAKE. That predicate
    ; answers *does this statement CONTAIN an augmentation* — it recurses into an
    ; ambient module's body and reports its own container as true — so collecting on
    ; it handed `merge_module_augmentation` the name of `declare module "Map"` rather
    ; than of the `module "Observable"` nested inside it. The augmentation then
    ; resolved its own module name TO ITSELF, `mergeSymbol(x, x)` returned at its
    ; `source == target` guard, and five stage-2 units printed `any` where the
    ; reference prints `typeof import("Observable")` — with no diagnostic anywhere,
    ; because every step had done exactly what it was asked.
    ; ★★A PREDICATE THAT RECURSES ANSWERS ABOUT A SUBTREE; A COLLECTOR HAS TO ANSWER
    ; ABOUT A NODE. The two questions read alike and the shared helper is the trap.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's three-way fork is kept whole: an ambient module in an
    ; EXTERNAL file is an augmentation outright; one nested in a top-level ambient
    ; module is an augmentation when its name is non-relative; and one at the top
    ; level of a SCRIPT is an ambient module DECLARATION whose body is then walked
    ; with `inAmbientModule` true. Only the third recurses.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE BINDER IS THE WALKED FILE'S (slice 256). It was the checker's own `b`,
    ; the FIRST unit's, for all three questions — so with a module first in the
    ; program every `declare module "x"` of a later script file read as an
    ; augmentation, and its `global { … }` block (react16.d.ts's `namespace JSX`)
    ; was never merged: a JSX element typed `any` wherever the lib came second.
    procedure collect_module_augmentations(this, mutable out: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], node: ref[AstNode]?, fb: ref[Binder], in_ambient_module: bool) linked














































    ; initializeChecker's LOOP BODY, one file of the program. It was inline until
    ; slice 113 gave the Checker a second file.
    procedure merge_file_globals(mutable this, mutable f: ref[AstNode], fb: ref[Binder], mutable ambient: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]) io reads shared_lock linked








































































