    ; ── the spelling suggester ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getSuggestionForSymbolNameLookup, getSpellingSuggestionForName and
    ; core.GetSpellingSuggestion with its levenshteinWithMax, in one place because
    ; the middle one is three lines of plumbing.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARITHMETIC IS IN TENTHS AND THAT IS A PORT, NOT AN APPROXIMATION.
    ; Upstream's distances are float64 built from three increments — 0.1 for a
    ; case-only substitution, 1 for an insertion or a deletion, 2 for a real
    ; substitution — and the cutoff is `floor(len*0.4)+0.9`. Every one of those is
    ; a multiple of 0.1, so multiplying the whole system by ten makes it exact
    ; INTEGER arithmetic and removes the only floating-point code this port would
    ; own. `float64(len)*0.4` floors to `(len*4)/10` and `float64(len)*0.34`
    ; truncates to `(len*34)/100`: 0.4 and 0.34 are both rounded UP in binary, so
    ; the float product is never below the exact one and the two agree everywhere.
    ; ★The `big` sentinel is `max+1` rather than `max+0.01` for the same reason it
    ; exists at all — it only has to be strictly greater than the cutoff, and no
    ; path through it can produce a result at or below the cutoff.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS ASCII-ONLY UNDER A STOP UNTIL SLICE 199, AND THE STOP WAS ABOUT A
    ; TABLE THIS PORT COULD SIMPLY HAVE. What stood here read *upstream compares
    ; RUNES and folds with unicode.ToLower; both names here are compared byte by
    ; byte* — true, and the missing half is two Go functions whose tables are
    ; generated in UnicodeCase.scaly now. Both names are decoded to RUNES here and
    ; the folds are Go's own, so a Greek, Myanmar or supplementary-plane identifier
    ; is measured rather than reported.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ONE ASYMMETRY IS UPSTREAM'S AND IS REPRODUCED DELIBERATELY: the LENGTH
    ; tests read `len(candidateName)` — the Go string's BYTE count — against
    ; `len(runeName)`, the name's RUNE count. `GetSpellingSuggestion` converts only
    ; the name up front and asks `len()` of the candidate's string, so a
    ; multi-byte candidate is measured as longer than it reads. It decides which
    ; candidates are considered at all and which take the `< 3` fold path, so a
    ; port that "fixed" it would answer differently.

    ; strings.EqualFold, over two byte slices decoded as they are walked. ★The
    ; ASCII fast path is upstream's own and is not an optimisation here either: it
    ; is what makes `K` and `k` fold equal WITHOUT consulting the orbit, which
    ; would take `k` to KELVIN SIGN first.
    function names_equal_fold(a: Slice[char], b: Slice[char]) returns bool linked











































    ; The name decoded to runes — Go's `[]rune(s)`, whose invalid sequences each
    ; become one U+FFFD, which is what utf8_decode_in already answers.
    procedure name_runes(mutable this, d: Slice[char]) returns ref[Vector[int]] linked


    function name_runes_on(host: ref[Page], d: Slice[char]) returns ref[Vector[int]] linked




















    ; levenshteinWithMax, in tenths over RUNES. Answers -1 for "worse than max",
    ; which is the reference's own out-of-band answer.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARITHMETIC IS IN TENTHS AND THAT IS A PORT, NOT AN APPROXIMATION —
    ; the argument for it is in this family's header above.
    procedure levenshtein_with_max(mutable this, s1: ref[Vector[int]], n1: int, s2: ref[Vector[int]], n2: int, max_value: int) returns int linked


    function levenshtein_with_max_on(host: ref[Page], s1: ref[Vector[int]], n1: int, s2: ref[Vector[int]], n2: int, max_value: int) returns int linked














































































    ; The scratch rows — the two levenshtein rows AND the rune buffers name_runes
    ; fills, which are the same allocation. `slices.Grow` upstream reuses one pool
    ; across calls; here a fresh Vector per call is the same answer, and the call
    ; is once per candidate of a name that was not found.
    ; ★A zero-length name asks for a zero-length Vector, which allocates nothing —
    ; the empty-init rule in the root CLAUDE.md — so no caller has to guard it.
    ; ★ The row is built HERE rather than inline in the loop above, and that is the
    ; standing generic-operator defect in the root CLAUDE.md: a generic concept's
    ; `operator []` loses its body when its receiver is CONSTRUCTED in the routine
    ; that subscripts it. A call result is not a construction, and `tools/opzero`
    ; is what says so rather than this comment.
    procedure levenshtein_row(mutable this, size: int) returns ref[Vector[int]] linked


    function levenshtein_row_on(host: ref[Page], size: int) returns ref[Vector[int]] linked




    ; getCandidateName, the closure GetSpellingSuggestion is handed. Answers false
    ; when the candidate has no usable name — which is upstream's empty string.
    procedure spelling_candidate_name(mutable this, mutable candidate: ref[Symbol], meaning: int, mutable out_name: ref[Slice[char]]) returns bool io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked


































    ; core.GetSpellingSuggestion, over a candidate list this port materialises.
    ; ★`rune_n` is `len(runeName)` and every `cd.length` below is
