    ; ── slice 107: isBlockScopedNameDeclaredBeforeUse ────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FUNCTION IS THE TERM THREE REPORTS WAIT ON, AND ALL THREE ARE
    ; NEGATIVE: checkResolvedBlockScopedVariable's TS2448/TS2449/TS2450 and
    ; checkPropertyNotUsedBeforeDeclaration's TS2729/TS2449 fire when it answers
    ; FALSE. So a port that answered `true` everywhere would be silent and a port
    ; that answered `false` everywhere would invent on every block-scoped name in
    ; the corpus — which is why the stop it replaces was a stop and not a default.
    ;
    ; ★★ ITS SHAPE IS A POSITION TEST WITH TWO TAILS. `declaration.Pos() <=
    ; usage.Pos()` splits it: the BEFORE tail asks whether a textually earlier
    ; declaration is nonetheless unusable (a binding element referring to its own
    ; pattern, `var a = a`, a class named inside its own computed key or decorator,
    ; a property read from its own initializer), the AFTER tail whether a textually
    ; later one is nonetheless reachable (the use is deferred into a function, an
    ; instance-property initializer or an export specifier). Neither tail is the
    ; other's complement, and that is the whole reason the reference writes both.

    ; ast.IsBlockScope. The parent is a SECOND argument rather than a hop off the
    ; node, because the Block arm needs it: a function's own body block is not a
    ; block-scope container, so the question is about the PAIR and not about the
    ; node. Written with the reference's kind list in the reference's order.
    function is_block_scope(node: ref[AstNode]?, parent_node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool linked







































