    ; and not on the member, and the last arm is the one that makes a computed
    ; member a type question at all.
    procedure compute_constant_enum_member_value(mutable this, mutable member: ref[AstNode]) @page true @direct true returns EvalResult io reads shared_lock linked

































































    ; ── the enum's TYPES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; createComputedEnumType — a literal type PAIR (regular + fresh) carrying
    ; TypeFlagsEnum and no value at all, which is what a member whose initializer
    ; is not a constant expression gets. ★ It is the one producer of TypeFlagsEnum
    ; in this port, and the flag's name is misleading in the reference's own
    ; comment's words: *numeric computed enum member value*.
    procedure create_computed_enum_type(mutable this, symbol: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Type] linked










    ; getEnumLiteralType. ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S SECOND TABLE IS NOT HERE AND THE
    ; REASON IS THE KEY. Upstream keeps `enumNaNLiteralTypes` beside
    ; `enumLiteralTypes` because a Go map cannot be keyed on NaN — `NaN != NaN`,
    ; so every lookup misses and the cache would mint a type per occurrence. This
    ; port's table is a SCAN with a comparison written by hand, so NaN is asked
    ; about with `jsnum_is_nan` on both sides and hits like any other value; the
    ; two tables collapse into one with no observable difference, because what
    ; upstream's NaN table keys on (the enum symbol) is what this row already
    ; carries. ★It also fixes the thing the split leaves open: two NaNs with
    ; different PAYLOADS (`NaN` and `0/0` are different bit patterns) are one type
    ; here and one type there, for different reasons.
    procedure get_enum_literal_type(mutable this, mutable value: EvalResult, enum_symbol: ref[Symbol]?, symbol: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Type] linked












































    ; Two NUMBER values are the same enum-literal key when their bits agree, or
