    ; compareSymbolsWorker, WHOLE since slice 195.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SYMBOL-ID FALLBACK WAS A STOP THROUGH TWO WRONG ARGUMENTS, AND EACH
    ; ONE WAS RETIRED BY A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY A DECISION. The first —
    ; *every list this port sorts is built out of ONE SymbolTable, which is keyed by
    ; name*, so the fallback cannot be reached — was corrected in slice 179 when the
    ; report fired on five stage-2 units. The second — *there is nothing here to ask
    ; WHICH binder owns a declaration-less symbol, and an id drawn at the wrong
    ; counter would shift a later class's private-name mangling* — assumed the
    ; question was open; it is not. See the tail below: the arrivals are TRANSIENT
    ; symbols, and a transient symbol's binder is the unit's by the same rule
    ; `get_es_symbol_like_type_for_node` already follows.
    procedure compare_symbols(mutable this, mutable s1: ref[Symbol]?, mutable s2: ref[Symbol]?) returns int reads symbol_id_lock linked

























































    ; sortSymbols over a SUB-RANGE, which is what getNamedMembers' two calls are
    ; (`result[:containedCount]` and `result[containedCount:]`).
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS AN INSERTION SORT AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A SHRUG:
    ; the range is one type's member list, and `slices.SortFunc` is not stable
    ; either — what the two share is the ORDER they produce, which for a comparator
    ; that never answers 0 (see compare_symbols) is unique. Scaly's Array has no
    ; sort, and a quicksort over a list this size would be more code for no
    ; measurable difference.
    procedure sort_symbols(mutable this, mutable list: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]], from: int, to: int) reads symbol_id_lock linked



















    ; getNamedMembers, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO PASSES ARE NOT AN OPTIMISATION AND THE COMMENT UPSTREAM SAYS SO:
    ; a class's or interface's OWN members are stored ahead of the inherited ones,
    ; *"because explicitly declared members are more likely to contain
    ; discriminating differences"* in a type relation. The order is therefore
    ; observable by the relation and by nothing in this port yet, which is why the
    ; battery gates it with a reader named rather than with a colour.
    procedure get_named_members(mutable this, mutable members: ref[SymbolTable]?, container: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]? io reads symbol_id_lock linked

























































    ; ── THE LATE-BINDING TABLE (slice 129) ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THIS CHAPTER IS: a member whose name is COMPUTED gets a symbol from
    ; the binder under the internal name `computed`, and its REAL name is only
    ; knowable once the computed expression has a type. So a second, "late" binding
    ; phase runs over the container's declarations, types every computed name, and
    ; files the member under the name that type spells. getMembersOfSymbol and
    ; getExportsOfSymbol are its two doors and getLateBoundSymbol is the third.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS ONE MECHANISM SEEN FROM SEVEN PLACES, which is why the seven stops
    ; it retires were never seven chapters: `late-bound-members`,
    ; `late-bound-exports`, `late-bound-module-exports`,
    ; `late-bound-object-literal-member`, `get-late-bound-symbol`,
    ; `has-late-bindable-name` and `is-late-bindable-name` all sit on the same
    ; table, and the two stand-ins the members and exports doors carried
    ; (*does any declaration have a computed name? then stop*) were the same
    ; question asked without the answer.

    ; getExcludedSymbolFlags, whole — the set a declaration of these flags may NOT
    ; merge with. ★ReplaceableByMethod is a SUBTRACTION and stands last, which is
    ; what lets a JavaScript expando property be replaced by a method of the same
    ; name.
    function get_excluded_symbol_flags(flags: int) returns int linked







































    ; getAdjustedNodeForError — the declaration's NAME if it has one.
    ; getAdjustedNodeForError. ★★★`GetNameOfDeclaration`, not `Node.Name()` — the
    ; same slip (§3.5hr): the point is to move a duplicate-identifier report off a
    ; DECLARATION and onto its name, and a JS assignment declaration is exactly the
    ; kind whose name is not in a slot. Takes `this` for the accessor.
    function get_adjusted_node_for_error(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode] linked







    ; lookupOrIssueError, at the shape this port's diagnostics have.
    ;
    ; ★★★ UPSTREAM IT ANSWERS A DIAGNOSTIC SO THAT RELATED INFORMATION CAN BE HUNG
    ; ON IT; here a diagnostic is (pos, end, code) with no related information
    ; (§3.20, §3.24), so the whole of it is *report unless an identical one is
    ; already in the list* — which is the half that IS observable, because the
    ; second report of one duplicate-identifier pair would otherwise be a
    ; diagnostic the reference does not have.
    procedure lookup_or_issue_error(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], code: int) reads shared_lock linked




    procedure lookup_or_issue_error(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode], code: int, a0: String) reads shared_lock linked






