    ; late-binds.
    function get_members_of_declaration(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked















    ; getResolvedMembersOrExportsOfSymbol, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MODULE ARM CALLS THE WORKER AND NOT THE MEMO, which is the reference's
    ; own arrangement and is kept: getExportsOfModule memoizes, this does not, so a
    ; symbol that is both a module and a late-binding container resolves its export
    ; star twice. Routing it through the memo would be the safer-LOOKING change and
    ; would move the diagnostic ORDER of a shape the reference reports twice.
    procedure get_resolved_members_or_exports_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol], is_static: bool) @page true @direct true returns ref[SymbolTable]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked









































































































    ; getMembersOfSymbol — the fork is REAL since slice 129.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE STAND-IN THAT STOOD HERE ASKED THE RIGHT QUESTION AND HAD NO ANSWER,
    ; and its own header said so at length: *does any declaration of this container
    ; carry a computed name? then stop* — hasLateBindableName's first conjunct
    ; without the type test, conservative in the safe direction and a wall in front
    ; of 306 units. The late-binding table answers it (see its header above), so
    ; both halves of the fork now run.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONE MEASUREMENT THE STAND-IN LEAVES BEHIND IS WORTH KEEPING: for
    ; `interface I { [key]: string }` the interface symbol's members table has
    ; length ZERO, because the binder files a computed member under the internal
    ; name `computed` in a table this port could not reach FROM THE CONTAINER. That
    ; is exactly what getResolvedMembersOrExportsOfSymbol is for — it walks the
    ; DECLARATIONS, not the table — and it is why the marker that works for
    ; get_late_bound_symbol (which is handed the MEMBER's symbol) says nothing here.
    procedure get_members_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol]) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[SymbolTable]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked






    ; getExportsOfSymbol — the STATIC half of the fork, and it is REAL since slice
    ; 129 as well.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THREE ARMS ARE THE REFERENCE'S IN ITS OWN ORDER AGAIN. Slice 110's
    ; note explained why the MODULE question had to be asked FIRST — for a symbol
    ; that is both a late-binding container and a module (a class merged with a
    ; namespace) the early symbols come from getExportsOfModuleWorker rather than
    ; from `symbol.Exports`, so the fork this port could write would have answered
    ; the wrong table. getResolvedMembersOrExportsOfSymbol makes that choice
    ; internally, which is what lets the order go back to the reference's.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ASSIGNMENT-DECLARATION ENTRY has no counterpart on the members side:
    ; the binder files a dynamically named `this.x = v` under `%FEassignment` in the
    ; container's EXPORTS, and every declaration hanging off it is late-bound. It is
    ; the static half's third loop, inside the resolver.
    procedure get_exports_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol]) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[SymbolTable]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked








    ; getSignaturesOfSymbol, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE OVERLOAD SKIP IS THE WHOLE FUNCTION APART FROM THE CALL. A symbol with
    ; three declarations of one overloaded function contributes TWO signatures: the
    ; implementation is dropped, and it is recognised as the declaration that has a
    ; BODY, follows a declaration of the same kind under the same parent, and either
    ; starts exactly where that one ended or was REPARSED. The last disjunct is the
    ; JSDoc reparser's — a synthesized declaration has no source position to abut.
    procedure get_signatures_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]? io reads ordinal_lock linked






























































    ; isValidIndexKeyType, whole since slice 179.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS TWO ROWS STOOD ON A PRODUCER CLAIM THAT HAD STOPPED BEING TRUE — the
    ; note said *nothing in this port sets any of the three flags on a type*, and
    ; the template-literal and string-mapping minters plus is_pattern_literal_type
    ; itself all landed later. **A claim about what a port CANNOT MINT expires the
    ; slice that mints it, and nothing re-reads the readers.**
    procedure is_valid_index_key_type(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked














































    ; getIndexInfosOfIndexSymbol, and the split is by DECLARATION KIND.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE INDEX-SIGNATURE HALF IS THE WHOLE FUNCTION FOR A TYPESCRIPT FILE; the
    ; other half is `hasLateBindableIndexSignature`, i.e. `obj[expr] = v` or a
    ; computed member whose key type is a literal, and it needs
    ; checkExpressionCached / checkComputedPropertyName and then THREE
    ; isTypeAssignableTo calls to sort the key into the string, number or symbol
    ; bucket. That is the relation, and the whole late-bound tail behind it —
    ; getObjectLiteralIndexInfo, getUnionTypeEx — with it. The arm reports.
    ;
    ; ★★ `forEachType` OVER THE KEY IS ONE CALL HERE, not a loop: it distributes
    ; over a UNION and this port makes none, so the union case is a report exactly
    ; as check_type_for_duplicate_index_signatures' `Distributed()` is. ★The `never`
    ; case is NOT special here where it is there: forEachType visits a `never` type
    ; once like any other, and isValidIndexKeyType then rejects it.
    procedure get_index_infos_of_index_symbol(mutable this, mutable index_symbol: ref[Symbol], siblings: ref[SymbolTable]?) returns ref[Array[ref[IndexInfo]?]]? io reads ordinal_lock linked






















































































































































































































    ; getIndexInfosOfSymbol, whole.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference hands the SIBLING SYMBOLS to the call below and this port
    ; hands the TABLE they come out of. The list is read only by the late-bound
    ; half, which reports — so passing the table costs nothing and saves building a
    ; list nothing reads (§3.5be's rule about a slot with no reader, applied to an
    ; argument).
    procedure get_index_infos_of_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Array[ref[IndexInfo]?]]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked








    ; ── resolveStructuredTypeMembers and what is behind it ───────────────────

