    ; instantiateSymbol, and its two fast paths are the slice.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST ONE IS WHAT MAKES THE CHAPTER REACHABLE: a mapper that maps
    ; nothing but `this` against a member whose declaration is free of `this`
    ; answers the member itself. The second is the reference's own memo-shaped
    ; shortcut — a member whose type is already resolved and cannot contain a type
    ; variable is likewise unaffected.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THIRD BRANCH IS THE FIRST TRANSIENT SYMBOL THIS PORT HAS EVER MADE,
    ; and it is what turns get_type_of_symbol's *"not merely unreached,
    ; UNREPRESENTABLE"* into one representable arm out of four. It costs a bit on
    ; Symbol (`check_flags`), two slots on ValueSymbolLink (`target`, `mapper`) and
    ; getTypeOfInstantiatedSymbol; the other three CheckFlags arms still have no
    ; writer and are still left out.
    ;
    ; ★★ `links.nameType` IS THE ONE FIELD THE COPY DOES NOT CARRY, deliberately:
    ; ValueSymbolLink has no such slot, its reader is symbolToString's late-bound
    ; arm, and a slot copied for no reader is §3.5be's claim about the future.
    procedure instantiate_symbol(mutable this, mutable symbol: ref[Symbol]?, m: ref[TypeMapper]?) returns ref[Symbol]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked




























































































    ; instantiateSymbolTable. It answers a FRESH table whenever the source is
    ; non-empty, exactly as upstream — even when every entry came back unchanged —
    ; because the caller folds base members into it and must not touch the DECLARED
    ; one.
    ; createInstantiatedSymbolTable — instantiate_symbol_table over a symbol LIST
    ; rather than a table. The two are not one function because the reference's two
    ; are not: the members of an instantiated anonymous type come from
    ; getPropertiesOfObjectType, which answers a list, and its ORDER is what the
    ; resulting table's order is.
    procedure create_instantiated_symbol_table(mutable this, mutable symbols: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?, m: ref[TypeMapper]) returns ref[SymbolTable]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked


























    procedure instantiate_symbol_table(mutable this, mutable symbols: ref[SymbolTable]?, m: ref[TypeMapper]) returns ref[SymbolTable]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked






























    ; instantiateSignature — `instantiateSignatureEx(sig, m, m == c.permissiveMapper)`.
    ;
    ; ★ Since slice 122 the eraseTypeParameters argument is carried, because
    ; getSignatureInstantiation passes TRUE and that is exactly the value that
    ; skips the fresh-type-parameter branch below. The two older callers pass
    ; false and are unchanged.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND SINCE SLICE 151 THE ARGUMENT IS THE REFERENCE'S OWN EXPRESSION, where
    ; it had been folded to `false` with the note *"the permissive mapper is a
    ; FunctionTypeMapper this port has no arm for"*. It is a POINTER comparison
    ; against the memoised mapper — see the field's note on why the two are memoised
    ; at all — and its effect is that a signature instantiated PERMISSIVELY loses its
    ; own type parameters instead of getting fresh clones of them, which is what
    ; makes the wildcard comparison decide anything about a generic signature.
    procedure instantiate_signature(mutable this, mutable sig: ref[Signature]?, mutable m: ref[TypeMapper]) returns ref[Signature]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked










    ; ★★★ THE FRESH-TYPE-PARAMETER BRANCH IS SLICE 139'S AND IT IS THE STOP THAT
    ; STOOD HERE. The note said *cloneTypeParameter over the fresh set, then
    ; combineTypeMappers — the COMPOSITE mapper arm TypeMapperData's header names as
    ; having no writer. It lands with the arm* — and it does: the arm is written,
    ; the clone existed since slice 125 and `mapper` is the slot its own header
    ; named a caller for.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ORDER INSIDE THE BRANCH IS LOAD-BEARING AND UPSTREAM SAYS SO IN A
    ; COMMENT: the clones are minted FIRST, the combined mapper is built over
    ; {original -> clone} SECOND, and the mapper is written back onto every clone
    ; THIRD — so a clone's constraint is instantiated through a mapper that already
    ; knows about all of its siblings. Built in any other order, `<T, U extends T>`
    ; loses the sibling mention.
    procedure instantiate_signature_ex(mutable this, mutable sig: ref[Signature]?, mutable m_in: ref[TypeMapper], erase_type_parameters: bool) returns ref[Signature]? io reads ordinal_symbols linked



















































    ; instantiateIndexInfo.
    procedure instantiate_index_info(mutable this, mutable info: ref[IndexInfo]?, mutable m: ref[TypeMapper]) returns ref[IndexInfo]? io reads shared_lock linked













    ; resolveTypeReferenceMembers.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE PADDING IS THE WHOLE FUNCTION AND IT IS WHERE THE `this` MAPPER COMES
    ; FROM. `typeParameters` is allTypeParameters — outer, local, and the thisType
    ; last — while `typeArguments` is the resolved list WITHOUT it, so the two differ
    ; in length by exactly one and the padding appends the TYPE ITSELF. The mapper
    ; the caller then builds is `{thisType -> t}` for a non-generic class and
    ; `{T -> arg, …, thisType -> t}` for a generic one.
    procedure resolve_type_reference_members(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked

































    ; resolveClassOrInterfaceMembers — one line upstream and one here.
    procedure resolve_class_or_interface_members(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked


    ; getDefaultConstructSignatures, and the base-less branch is the whole
    ; function for every class in this corpus.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE SYNTHESIZED `new C(): C` AND IT HAS NO DECLARATION, which is
    ; what makes it portable at all: a class that declares no constructor still
    ; answers one construct signature, and that signature's parameter list is
    ; EMPTY, its return type is the class's instance type and its declaration slot
    ; is nil. Everything a signature normally needs from a node —
    ; getSignatureFromDeclaration, the parameter symbols, the minimum argument
    ; count — is therefore not asked.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE LOOP BELOW IS A STOP AND IT IS REACHED ONLY BY A CLASS THAT EXTENDS
    ; SOMETHING. `baseSignatures` comes from getSignaturesOfType on the base
    ; CONSTRUCTOR type, and get_base_constructor_type_of_class answers
    ; undefinedType for a base-less class — a type with no structured members, so
    ; the list is empty and the first branch takes every class this port can build
    ; today. A class that DOES extend stops one call earlier, inside
    ; get_base_constructor_type_of_class at `is-constructor-type`, so the stop here
    ; is the one that would fire if that one were closed; it is written rather than
    ; left out because the alternative is a function whose only branch looks total.
    ;
    ; ★ `isAbstract` is read even in the base-less branch, and it is the one thing
    ; the branch takes from the declaration: `abstract class C {}` answers a
    ; construct signature flagged Abstract, which is what makes `new C()` an error
    ; rather than a missing signature.
    procedure get_default_construct_signatures(mutable this, mutable class_type: ref[Type]) returns ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked






















































































































    ; cloneSignature, whole. ★The `composite` slot IS carried since slice 155 —
    ; the note that stood here said *"the day it gets one, this line arrives with
    ; it"*, and this is that day. It matters at exactly one caller:
    ; createUnionSignature clones and then OVERWRITES the slot, so a clone that
    ; dropped it would be right there and wrong everywhere else.
    procedure clone_signature(mutable this, mutable sig: ref[Signature]) returns ref[Signature] linked








    ; resolveAnonymousTypeMembers. Slice 80 wrote the middle branch, slice 87 the
    ; third, and the FIRST is the one that is still absent — deliberately.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST BRANCH IS NOT A STOP AND NOT A HOLE: IT HAS NO SUBJECT. It is
    ; the INSTANTIATED anonymous type, `d.target != nil`, and AnonymousTypeData
    ; carries neither `target` nor `mapper` — because the only writer of those two
    ; slots is getObjectTypeInstantiation, which stops. So the branch is
    ; unreachable by construction rather than unwritten, and adding the two slots
    ; ahead of their writer would be a claim about the future (§3.5be). Slice 86's
    ; stage-2 probe put a number on it: `get-object-type-instantiation` fires on 5
    ; units of 18 185, and none at stage 1.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MIDDLE BRANCH IS THE TYPE LITERAL AND IT IS THREE CALLS. `{ a: number }`
    ; and `{ (x: number): string }` are one symbol whose members table holds the
    ; properties, the `%FEcall` entry and the `%FEnew` one — so getMembersOfSymbol,
    ; getSignaturesOfSymbol twice and getIndexInfosOfSymbol is the whole branch.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THIRD BRANCH IS THE STATIC SIDE OF A CLASS, AND WHAT REACHES IT IS
    ; ONE SYMBOL KIND. The reference calls it *combinations of function, class, enum
    ; and module*, but the stop it replaces fired 422 times over 274 units at stage
    ; 1 with detail 32 — SymbolFlagsClass — and with NO OTHER detail, not once. A
    ; function's static side does not arrive here because getTypeOfSymbol answers a
    ; function type through a different door; an enum's and a module's do not
    ; arrive because their chapters stop earlier. So the four arms below are the
    ; reference's four, one of them is measured live, and the other three are
    ; written because leaving them out would make the live one look like the whole
    ; function.
    procedure resolve_anonymous_type_members(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) io reads symbol_id_lock linked








































































































































































































































































































































    ; resolveStructuredTypeMembers, whole — five arms of which one is written.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARM ORDER IS THE REFERENCE'S AND IT DECIDES THE SLICE. Reference is
    ; tested BEFORE ClassOrInterface, and get_declared_type_of_class_or_interface
    ; sets the Reference flag on every class and on every generic or this-using
    ; interface — so what falls through to the ported arm is exactly a non-generic
    ; interface free of `this`. The two facts are in different functions and only
    ; their conjunction says which types this slice covers, which is why the header
    ; states it there rather than here.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's two `panic("Unhandled case")` defaults are reports here, for
    ; the reason new_object_type's are: a panic would be a claim that the input is
    ; impossible, and this port's answer is that it has not been ported.
    ; resolveStructuredTypeMembers.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MARK DELTA AT THE BOTTOM IS SLICE 108'S, AND IT REPAIRS A MEMO THAT
    ; CACHED ITS OWN INCOMPLETENESS — the third table in this file to need that
    ; (check_expression_cached_ex and get_resolved_signature are the other two).
    ; `resolve_anonymous_type_members` writes a HALF-FILLED record before it builds
    ; the signatures, which is the reference's own arrangement and its own comment: a
    ; signature may name this very type through `typeof`, and the half-filled record
    ; is what closes that recursion. Upstream the tables are then always completed.
    ; **Here the very next line can STOP** — `get_base_constructor_type_of_class`
    ; reports `is-constructor-type` — and the procedure returns with
    ; `MembersResolved` SET and the signature tables EMPTY. The second ask then reads
    ; *no construct signatures* with no stop and no mark movement.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT COST NINE UNITS OF INVENTION AND ONLY STAGE 2 SAW THEM. Slice 108's
    ; `new`-expression tail reports TS2351 when a target has neither construct nor
    ; call signatures, and on those nine units it fired for a CLASS WITH A
    ; CONSTRUCTOR. Stage 1 is green throughout — the shape needs a class whose base
    ; type resolution stops AND a second `new` on the same class — which is slice 64's
    ; rule arriving as a defect rather than as a number.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE LINE IS THE ONE THE FILE ALREADY HAS. `resolve_base_types` clears the
    ; same flag for the reference's own reason (*a circularity in a type-parameter
    ; default can leave members half resolved, and this resets that*); the trigger
    ; here is a STOP rather than a circularity, and the remedy is identical. ★It is
    ; placed in the WRAPPER rather than at each early return because all three
    ; resolve_*_members procedures have the shape and the wrapper is the one place
    ; that sees every one of them.
    ; ── slice 127: the UNION and INTERSECTION member tables ──────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MEMBERS TABLE OF A UNION OR INTERSECTION IS EMPTY BY DESIGN, and
    ; that is the reference's own comment: *"The members and properties
    ; collections are empty for union types. To get all properties of a union type
    ; use getPropertiesOfType"*. What these two resolve is the SIGNATURES and the
    ; INDEX INFOS; the properties are synthesized one name at a time by
    ; createUnionOrIntersectionProperty, which is why a union has a property CACHE
    ; and not a property table.

    ; resolveUnionTypeMembers.
    procedure resolve_union_type_members(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) io reads symbol_id_lock linked



























    ; getUnionSignatures, and it is STILL written as the QUESTION its caller asks —
    ; `getUnionSignatures(core.Map(t.Types(), …))` — because the list of lists is
    ; built from the union's constituents at the one place that has them. The
    ; `signature_lists_of` helper below is upstream's `core.Map`, and the fold
    ; itself takes the list of lists so that findMatchingSignatures can walk it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EARLY EXIT IS STILL THE WHOLE FUNCTION ON THE ORDINARY UNION. `if
    ; len(signatureLists[i]) == 0 { return nil }` fires as soon as ONE constituent
    ; has no signature of this kind, and a union of data types has none anywhere.
    ; What slice 155 added is what happens when every constituent DOES have one.
    ;
    ; ★ The globalFunctionType arm substitutes `unknownSignature`, a ONE-element
    ; list — so for that constituent the list is never empty, which is exactly why
    ; the arm exists and why it cannot be folded away.
    procedure signature_lists_of(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type], kind: int) returns ref[Array[ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?]]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked

















































    ; getUnionSignatures, whole (slice 155).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SECOND HALF RUNS ONLY WHEN THE FIRST FOUND NOTHING, and its guard is
    ; `countLengthOverOne <= 1` — at most ONE constituent may carry overloads.
    ; Upstream argues it: overloads are conditional, and overloads in several
    ; constituents would need the POWER SET of their combinations, whose ordering
    ; is not obvious. So a single master list is combined pairwise with each other
    ; constituent's FIRST signature, and the fold gives up whole (`results = nil`)
    ; the moment two type-parameter lists disagree.
    procedure get_union_signatures(mutable this, mutable signature_lists: ref[Array[ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?]]?) returns ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked



























































































































































































    ; `slices.Clone(masterList)` — a fresh list, because the fold below REPLACES
    ; its rows and the master is a resolved type's own signature list.
    function clone_signature_list(this, src: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]) returns ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]] linked












    ; ── slice 155: the COMBINE family ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; combineUnionOrIntersectionMemberSignatures, its two halves, and
    ; createUnionSignature. All four exist so that a union or intersection of
    ; OBJECT types can answer a single call signature: `(a: A) => X` and
    ; `(b: B) => Y` intersected is one signature whose parameter is `A | B` —
    ; contravariance, which is why the parameter fold takes `!is_union`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE PARAMETER FOLD IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE SIGNATURE FOLD, and reading
    ; `!isUnion` as a transcription slip is the mistake it invites. A value
    ; usable as EITHER signature must accept EITHER parameter type, so a UNION of
    ; signatures INTERSECTS parameters; the `this` parameter takes the same fold
    ; for the same reason, and upstream says so at the line.

    procedure combine_union_or_intersection_member_signatures(mutable this, mutable left: ref[Signature], mutable right: ref[Signature], is_union: bool) returns ref[Signature]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked




























































































    ; combineUnionOrIntersectionParameters.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `needsExtraRestElement` IS WHAT MAKES THE FOLD TOTAL. If either side has
    ; an effective rest parameter and the LONGER one does not, the combined
    ; signature grows one slot past the longest — otherwise a call with more
    ; arguments than the longer signature declares would lose the rest the shorter
    ; one had.
    procedure combine_union_or_intersection_parameters(mutable this, mutable left: ref[Signature], mutable right: ref[Signature], mutable mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?, is_union: bool) @page true @direct true returns ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked












































































































































































    ; combineUnionOrIntersectionThisParam. ★The two null arms are not symmetric
    ; with the fold: a signature with no `this` parameter combines to the OTHER
