    ; ── the grammar check the file itself gets ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarSourceFile, whole:
    ;
    ;   return node.Flags&ast.NodeFlagsAmbient != 0 &&
    ;       c.checkGrammarTopLevelElementsForRequiredDeclareModifier(node)
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 47 DEFERRED THE SECOND HALF ON ONE PREDICATE AND SAID SO, AND
    ; THIS IS THAT BILL PAID. `ast.IsDeclarationNode` is `node.DeclarationData()
    ; != nil` — a property of the node LAYOUT, not a list anyone can read off a
    ; switch — so its note read *a hand-written kind list here would be a silent
    ; wrong answer in both directions: a missing kind drops a TS1046 the reference
    ; reports, an extra one invents a diagnostic where a plain statement stands*.
    ; It is now DERIVED (DeclarationKinds.scaly, generated by closing the
    ; reference's struct-embedding graph over DeclarationBase and verified against
    ; the reference's own DeclarationData()), and the derivation is what shows how
    ; right the deferral was: seven of the fifty-six kinds are ones no reader would
    ; have guessed, BinaryExpression and ObjectLiteralExpression among them.
    procedure check_grammar_source_file(mutable this) returns bool linked






    ; ★ The OR is the reference's own, and it is the proof that a VariableStatement
    ; is NOT a declaration node: upstream writes `ast.IsDeclarationNode(decl) ||
    ; decl.Kind == ast.KindVariableStatement`, which would be dead text if the
    ; predicate already admitted it. The derived table agrees — 244 is absent from
    ; it — so the two readings of the reference confirm each other.
    procedure check_grammar_top_level_elements_for_required_declare_modifier(mutable this) returns bool linked




















    ; checkGrammarTopLevelElementForRequiredDeclareModifier. The reference's own
    ; comment on the grammar it enforces is worth keeping, because the exempt list
    ; below is not derivable from anything else:
    ;
    ;   A declare modifier is required for any top level .d.ts declaration except
    ;   export=, export default, export as namespace, interfaces and imports:
    ;
    ;     DeclarationElement:
    ;        ExportAssignment
    ;        export_opt   InterfaceDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   TypeAliasDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   ImportDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   ExternalImportDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   AmbientDeclaration
    procedure check_grammar_top_level_element_for_required_declare_modifier(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool linked









    ; The kind half of the condition above, as a function for the reason
    ; ast.scaly's is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node is one.
    function kind_is_exempt_from_declare_modifier(k: int) returns bool linked




















    ; ── checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext ────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The whole body of the EmptyStatement and DebuggerStatement arms and the first
    ; line of the ExpressionStatement one, which is why a slice aimed at the
    ; statement histogram starts here.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONCE-BIT IS THE POINT OF THE FUNCTION, not bookkeeping around it. The
    ; reference's own comment: *If we are in a block, we only want to really report
    ; an error once to prevent noisiness* — so the bit lives on the BLOCK for the
    ; second diagnostic and on the NODE for the first, and a port that dropped it
    ; would emit one TS1036 per statement of an ambient block instead of one per
    ; block. ★And the bit is set to the RESULT of the report, not unconditionally:
    ; when grammarErrorOnFirstToken is suppressed by a parse diagnostic, nothing is
    ; marked and the next statement asks again — which is the same answer, since
    ; the suppression is a property of the file rather than of the statement.
    ;
    ; ★ The else branch of the reference is an assertion it has commented out
    ; ("we must be parented by a statement ... our parent will have already done
    ; it"), so there is nothing to port there and the function falls through.
    procedure check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(mutable this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool linked






































    ; ast.IsAccessor, which is two kinds.
    function is_accessor_kind(k: int) returns bool linked








    function has_reported_ambient(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool linked












    procedure mark_reported_ambient(mutable this, n: ref[AstNode]?) @persists true linked


    ; ── slice 101: the assignability relation, and the half of it that answers
    ;    without recursing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ROW SLICES 99 AND 100 BOTH NAMED AND NEITHER TOOK. Both closing
    ; paragraphs called `check-type-assignable-to 261` *the largest row that is not
    ; the table* — 2 234 units of stage 2, then 2 783 — and both passed it over with
    ; the same reason: `relater.go` is 5 006 lines of ONE recursive comparison with a
    ; dozen entry points, so it cannot be opened as a dispatch and closed arm by arm
    ; the way the last five chapters were. ★★★What CAN be opened is the half that
    ; answers WITHOUT recursing: `isTypeRelatedTo`'s fast paths and
    ; `isSimpleTypeRelatedTo`, the flag table that decides every comparison between
    ; two types neither of which is structured or instantiable. `var x: number = 1`
    ; is that comparison, and so is `var x: string = 1` — the first unblocks a unit,
    ; the second is TS2322.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ONE GUARD MAKES THE REPORT SAFE, AND IT IS NOT AN ARGUMENT PER ARM. A
    ; relation that answers FALSE is not the same claim as a relation that CANNOT
    ; answer: the reference reaches `false` only after the whole structural
    ; comparison has failed, and this port reaches it after two flag tests. So the
    ; report is emitted only when NOTHING STOPPED under the comparison —
    ; `report_error_results` takes the mark its caller read on entry and returns
    ; without a word when the count has moved. Every unported arm below is therefore
    ; free to be a `record_unported` and fall through: a stop cannot become an
    ; invented diagnostic, and diagcheck's relation — our C section is a SUBSEQUENCE
    ; of the reference's — is exactly the direction an invented line is visible in.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAFETY IS A CONTAINMENT THAT COSTS NOTHING:
    ; **the report is reachable only when NEITHER side is structured or
    ; instantiable**, because the line above it stops on both. That one fact kills
    ; almost every branch of the reporting machinery at once — `reportErrorResults`'
    ; four switch arms each need Object, JsxAttributes or Intersection;
    ; `reportRelationError`'s type-parameter arm needs TypeParameter or
    ; IndexedAccess; its did-you-mean arm needs a Union target. They are written
    ; where the reference forks and they report rather than being folded away, so
    ; the slice that opens the recursion meets them.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ONE PLACE THAT CONTAINMENT DOES **NOT** REACH IS `elaborateError`,
    ; AND FINDING THAT OUT IS WHAT THE ARM LIST BELOW IS FOR. An OBJECT source
    ; against a PRIMITIVE target does not stop — `isRelatedToEx` answers it before
    ; normalization, in a branch of its own — so an object literal, an array
    ; literal and a function expression DO arrive at the elaborator with
    ; `var x: string = {}`. Two of the four arms answer that case themselves (a
    ; primitive target is not elaborated, which is `elaborateObjectLiteral`'s own
    ; first line) and the report is kept; the rest is a row.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS NOT HERE, each named with what it needs: `recursiveTypeRelatedTo`
    ; and `structuredTypeRelatedTo` (the recursion, with the relation CACHE, the
    ; depth limits and `getRelationKey`), `unionOrIntersectionRelatedTo`,
    ; `hasExcessProperties`, `hasCommonProperties`, the whole ERROR CHAIN and with
    ; it every message that is not the relation's own, `elaborateObjectLiteral` /
    ; `elaborateArrayLiteral` / `elaborateArrowFunction` / `elaborateJsxComponents`,
    ; `isTypeIdenticalTo` (the identity relation's own recursion) and
    ; `getNormalizedType`'s four non-literal arms.
    ;
    ; ★ ONLY `RelationAssignable` IS EVER PASSED, and that is a fact about the
    ; CALLERS rather than about this table — the three sites wired here are all
    ; `checkTypeAssignableToAndOptionallyElaborate`. The relation tests are
    ; transcribed as the reference writes them, on CheckMode's rule: a partial
    ; reading would make the next arm's test read as a new decision rather than as a
    ; transcription.

    ; isTypeRelatedTo, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE OBJECT/OBJECT CACHE LOOKUP WAS LEFT OUT WITH A PROOF THAT EXPIRED,
    ; AND IT STOOD FOR SEVENTEEN SLICES. It read: *`relation.get(id)` can only
    ; answer what `relation.set` wrote, and the only writer in relater.go is
    ; `recursiveTypeRelatedTo` — the recursion this slice does not have.* That
    ; recursion landed in slice 155 and nothing re-read the argument; the map got
    ; its writer in slice 172 and its absence had been costing a factor of NINETY
    ; on the shapes that ask the relation repeatedly. The lookup here is still not
    ; written, and now for a different and smaller reason: this entry point is
    ; reached before any comparison has been recursed into, so a row can only be
    ; present if `recursive_type_related_to` put it there — and that function asks
    ; first thing. Adding it here would save one call, not one comparison.
    procedure is_type_related_to(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked

















































    ; isSimpleTypeRelatedTo, whole, and it is the content of this slice: fifteen
    ; arms over the two flag words, in the reference's order.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING BECAUSE THE FLAGS ARE NOT DISJOINT, which is
    ; the same sentence type_to_string's header carries one dimension over: an enum
    ; literal is a StringLiteral or a NumberLiteral as well, `boolean` is a Boolean
    ; and its two literals are BooleanLiterals, and `never` answers TRUE as a SOURCE
    ; and FALSE as a target — so the never arm has to sit between them.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ERROR REPORTER IS NOT A PARAMETER HERE, AND THE SIX ENUM ARMS ARE WHY.
    ; The reference threads an `ErrorReporter` through this function for exactly one
    ; consumer, `isEnumTypeRelatedTo`, which reports INTO THE CHAIN before the
    ; relation's own message and therefore decides the diagnostic's CODE. Every arm
    ; that needs it — the two enum-symbol arms and the three enum-LITERAL value
    ; comparisons — so the parameter has no reader here and every report inside
    ; is_enum_type_related_to is `errorReporter != nil`, i.e. dead under this
    ; port's one call shape. ★★THE SECOND HALF OF THIS NOTE EXPIRED IN SLICE 116
    ; (§3.5p). It said the enum arms *are unreachable besides: nothing in this port
    ; sets TypeFlagsEnum or TypeFlagsEnumLiteral on a type* — true from slice 72
    ; until the enum chapter, and false the moment create_computed_enum_type and
    ; get_enum_literal_type existed. Both are now live arms with live inputs.
    ; `source.AsLiteralType().value == target.AsLiteralType().value` — a Go `any`
    ; equality over the literal payload, asked at four sites in
    ; isSimpleTypeRelatedTo.
    ;
    ; ★★★ NaN IS NOT EQUAL TO ITSELF HERE AND THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF
    ; get_enum_literal_type's rule, ten screens up. Go's `==` on an `any` holding a
    ; float64 is IEEE equality, so two NaN-valued enum members are UNRELATED under
    ; this relation — while the literal TABLE deliberately treats them as one key,
    ; because a Go map lookup on NaN misses and upstream keeps a second table for
    ; exactly that. Two rules, one value, and reading either as the other is a
    ; defect no fixture in this corpus can show.
    function literal_values_equal(a: ref[Type], b: ref[Type]) returns bool linked
























    ; `sourceSymbol.Name != targetSymbol.Name` — the NAME test in front of
    ; isEnumTypeRelatedTo, asked null-safely because a type reaching the Enum arm
    ; without a symbol is representable in the type but not producible here.
    function symbol_names_equal(a: ref[Symbol]?, b: ref[Symbol]?) returns bool linked












    ; isEnumTypeRelatedTo, whole but for its MEMO. ★★ THE `enumRelation` CACHE IS
    ; NOT CARRIED and the argument is the one every dropped memo in this file
    ; needs: without an errorReporter this function is PURE — its only writes are
    ; through getEnumMemberValue, which is idempotent behind
    ; NodeCheckFlagsEnumValuesComputed — so the cache changes how long the answer
    ; takes and never what it is. ★It also carries the failed/succeeded
    ; distinction that exists ONLY to let a second call with a reporter re-report,
    ; which is the half this port has no caller for.
    ;
    ; ★ Both loops walk PROPERTIES rather than declarations, so a merged enum is
    ; compared as one symbol table, which is what the whole function is about.
    procedure is_enum_type_related_to(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Symbol]?, mutable target: ref[Symbol]?) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked



























































































    ; `sourceValue != targetValue` on two evaluator results — Go `any` equality
    ; again, so a NONE is equal only to a NONE and NaN is equal to nothing (see
    ; literal_values_equal's header for why this file has two answers to that).
    function eval_values_equal(a: EvalResult, b: EvalResult) returns bool linked


























    procedure is_simple_type_related_to(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked



























































































































































































































    ; isUnknownLikeUnionType. ★The ORDER of the first two constituents is the
    ; test — upstream reads `types[0]` and `types[1]`, not `some` — and it is
    ; sound because a union's constituents are sorted by type id and undefined
    ; and null are minted before anything a source file can write. ★The two
    ; ObjectFlags bits share their positions with two INTERSECTION flags; the
    ; `flags & Union` guard above them is what keeps the sharing safe.
    procedure is_unknown_like_union_type(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked




































    ; checkTypeRelatedToEx, minus three pieces with no input. The OVERFLOW branch
    ; needs the relation counter and the depth stacks, which belong to the
    ; recursion; the export-links RETRY is guarded by `headMessage != nil` and no
    ; site wired here passes one; and the diagnostic is built from the error CHAIN,
    ; which in this slice never holds more than the relation's own message — so it
    ; is emitted at its line, in report_relation_error, rather than assembled here.
    ; ★The reference's `reportErrors` parameter is `errorNode != nil` at every one
    ; of its own call sites, which is why this port carries the node alone.
    ; ★★★ THE RELATION STATE IS SAVED AND RESTORED HERE, AND THAT IS `getRelater`
    ; / `putRelater` WITH NO POOL. Upstream every top-level comparison takes a
    ; Relater of its own out of a pool and hands it back, so its overflow flag, its
    ; maybe stack and its two depth stacks belong to that ONE comparison; this port
    ; collapsed them onto the checker, where they are shared.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE RELATION RE-ENTERS ITSELF, so sharing them is not a theoretical
    ; hazard: `is_known_property` asks `get_applicable_index_info_for_name`, which
    ; goes through `find_applicable_index_info` to `is_applicable_index_type`, whose
    ; first line is `is_type_assignable_to` — a second top-level comparison started
    ; from inside the first. Without this save the inner one inherits the outer's
    ; in-progress pairs (so a cycle is reported where upstream has none) and its
    ; depth counters (so the 100-frame limit trips early), and `rel_overflow` — set
    ; once, never cleared — would make every later relation in the FILE answer
    ; false. **A field that upstream allocates per call needs the save at the line
    ; where upstream allocates it.**
    procedure check_type_related_to(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool io reads shared_lock linked


    ; the capture (see `relation_capture`) sits on BOTH entries: the elaborating one above and
