    procedure check_type_related_to_ex(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, head_code: int) returns bool io reads shared_lock linked
























    procedure check_type_related_to_ex_worker(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, head_code: int) returns bool io reads shared_lock linked






















































































































    ; getNormalizedType — the FIXPOINT, at the three of its six arms this port can
    ; take (slice 166). It replaces the two hand-inlined blocks that stood at
    ; is_related_to_ex's head, and it is what closes §3.5gm.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `writing` FLAG IS THE WHOLE ASYMMETRY AND IT IS THE REFERENCE'S OWN:
    ; the SOURCE is normalized with false and the TARGET with true. The reference's
    ; comment at the call names the reason for the substitution arm — *resolve
    ; substitution types to either the substitution (on the source side) or the
    ; type variable (on the target side)* — and the same flag now steers every
    ; distribution inside get_simplified_type, where reading a `T[A | B]` yields a
    ; union and writing to one yields an intersection.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LOOP IS NOT DECORATION. The three arms feed each other — a
    ; simplification can answer a substitution and a substitution's intersection
    ; can be simplifiable — and upstream runs them to a fixpoint. The straight-line
    ; pair this replaces took each arm at most once, which was the identity on
    ; everything the port could then mint and is not any more.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ALL SIX ARMS SINCE SLICE 202. The three that stood as the IDENTITY —
    ; the generic tuple (getNormalizedTupleType), the deferred reference
    ; (createTypeReference / getSingleBaseForNonAugmentingSubtype) and the
    ; union/intersection normalization (getNormalizedUnionOrIntersectionType) —
    ; were not neutral: `class TypeSymbol extends Symbol {}` normalizes to
    ; `Symbol`, so a `TypeSymbol → Symbol` relation is `source == target` upstream
    ; and never compares the members, where this port compared them, re-inferred
    ; the base method's return type INSIDE its own inference and reported TS2454
    ; twice (`recursiveComplicatedClasses`).
    procedure normalize_relation_operand(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type], writing: bool) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked

























































































































