    ; The FORKPIN's entry — see ForkEvent's header. It ANSWERS as well as records,
    ; for record_kind's reason: every exit of the structured branch is one line, so
    ; an exit cannot be added without a row. ★The verdict is 2 whenever anything
    ; stopped under the call, read off the MARK rather than off the answer — a
    ; sub-relation whose own arm gave up returns false, and false-because-unrelated
    ; and false-because-unanswered are the two states this chapter turns on.
    procedure record_fork(mutable this, source: ref[Type], target: ref[Type], route: int, branch: int, mark: int, answer: bool) returns bool linked














    ; unionOrIntersectionRelatedTo, whole — the half of the structured fork this
    ; slice ports, and the reason its own comment leads with an ORDER: *"we need to
    ; deconstruct unions before intersections (because unions are always at the
    ; top), and we need to handle 'each' relations before 'some' relations for the
    ; same kind of type."* The four tests below are therefore a CHAIN and not a
    ; switch, and re-ordering any two of them is a different function.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THREE INTERSECTION ARMS WERE STOPPED UNDER A PRODUCER PREMISE THAT
    ; HAD EXPIRED — *"`TypeData` has no Intersection arm, so nothing this checker
    ; can mint carries TypeFlagsIntersection"* — which stopped being true at slice
    ; 121, and the arms' own tag counts had been the counter-example for a month
    ; (142 and 87 units at slice 155's stage 2). They are ported here; §3.5gk.
    ; ★The COMPARABLE hoist keeps an argument of its own and it is about the
    ; RELATION rather than about the type: only RelationAssignable is ever passed
    ; today (see the relation table's own note), so the hoist is written for
    ; fidelity and has no input — which is a claim about a CALLER and expires the
    ; day one passes the comparable relation, not a claim about what can be minted.
    ;
    ; ★★ `branch_out` is the FORKPIN's second column and it is an out-cell rather
    ; than a return, because the answer is a bool and the arm that produced it is
    ; the thing no verdict can show — a broken arm and an arm with no input read
    ; identically without it.
    procedure union_or_intersection_related_to(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, intersection_state: int, mutable branch_out: ref[int]) returns bool io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked














































































































































































































































    ; someTypeRelatedToType, whole. ★★★Its first line is `source.Types()`, and the
    ; accessor is the one that answers for BOTH payloads: since slice 156 the
    ; INTERSECTION tail of unionOrIntersectionRelatedTo calls this, and the
    ; union-only accessor answers null for one — which is a stop where the
    ; reference has an answer. The note that stood here (*"the intersection caller
    ; is the stop above"*) was true only while that stop was there; it is §3.5gk's
    ; shape read at the moment the producer arrives.
    procedure some_type_related_to_type(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, intersection_state: int) returns bool io reads symbol_id_lock linked




































    ; eachTypeRelatedToType, whole — the source-union arm of the assignable
    ; relation, and the one that carries the reference's CORRESPONDENCE fast path:
    ; *"many unions are mappings of one another; in such cases, simply comparing
    ; members at the same index can shortcut the comparison."*
    ;
    ; ★★ `result &= related` IS AN `and` HERE and nothing is lost by it. The
    ; reference accumulates a TERNARY so that a Maybe survives to the recursion's
    ; own bookkeeping; Maybe is produced in exactly one place —
    ; `recursiveTypeRelatedTo` — which is the half of the fork this slice does not
    ; port, so no call below can return one.
    procedure each_type_related_to_type(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, intersection_state: int) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked






























































    ; getUndefinedStrippedTargetIfNeeded, WHOLE since slice 159.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ROW STOOD ON A PREMISE THE FILE ITSELF REFUTES — §3.5eo. It read
    ; *`extractTypesOfKind` — which is `filterType` over a union, the piece this
    ; port does not carry*, and `filter_type_inside_flags` four thousand lines
    ; above carries the header `extractTypesOfKind` in its own comment. The
    ; guard was transcribed whole and only the helper's call was a stop.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MASK IS THE REFERENCE'S COMPLEMENT AND IT IS SPELLED AS ITS
    ; NEGATIVE, which is this file's standing idiom for a `&^` (see the
    ; ObjectFlagsMembersResolved reset and the decorator-modifier subset test):
    ; `extractTypesOfKind(target, ^Undefined)` keeps a constituent whose flags are
    ; NOT a subset of Undefined, and `filter_type_outside_flags(target, Undefined)`
    ; keeps one carrying no Undefined bit at all. ★They differ only for a type
    ; carrying Undefined TOGETHER with another flag, and no such type is minted:
    ; `undefinedType` and `missingType` are intrinsics whose flag word is exactly
    ; Undefined. The equivalence is therefore a claim about the PRODUCERS, which is
    ; the shape §3.5gk says to write down rather than to leave to a reader.
    procedure get_undefined_stripped_target_if_needed(mutable this, source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]? linked






























    ; typeRelatedToSomeType, whole — the TARGET-union arm, and the one the
    ; arithmetic operand check reaches: `checkArithmeticOperandType` asks whether
    ; its operand is assignable to `number | bigint`, so every call of it arrives
    ; here with a two-constituent primitive union.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE PRIMITIVE-UNION SHORT CIRCUIT IS THE REFERENCE'S OWN AND IT IS WHAT
    ; MAKES THE POPULATION ANSWER RATHER THAN LOOP: *"when relating a literal type
    ; to a union of primitive types, we know the relation is false unless the union
    ; contains the base primitive type or the literal type in one of its
    ; fresh/regular forms."* It is transcribed whole, exclusions included — the
    ; numeric-literal one for non-subtype relations, and the enum-literal one,
    ; whose comment says why (*identically named enum types are related*).
    procedure type_related_to_some_type(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, intersection_state: int) returns bool io reads symbol_id_lock linked


























































































































    ; ══ THE ASSIGNABILITY RECURSION (slice 118) ═══════════════════════════════
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY THIS CHAPTER AND NOT THE FRONTIER'S HEAD. `frontier.sh` ranked
    ; `has-excess-properties` first under BOTH granularities (+18 / 60 units), and
    ; the row is worth ZERO: removing it and re-sweeping completes not one unit,
    ; because `common-property-check` stands eleven lines further down the SAME
    ; function and `recursive-type-related-to` eleven lines below that. All three
    ; are this one wall, measured — see §3.5fl.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THERE IS NO RELATION CACHE IN THIS PORT AND THE ANSWER DOES NOT NEED
    ; ONE. `recursiveTypeRelatedTo`'s cache is a memo: every row it reads was
    ; computed by the same comparison it is about to run. What the cache is NOT
    ; optional for is the reference's own TERMINATION story — and that story has
    ; three legs, of which the cache is only one: the maybe stack (a comparison
    ; already in progress is assumed to hold), `isDeeplyNestedType` (a chain of
    ; generic instantiations three deep is assumed to expand for ever) and the
    ; 100-frame limit. The other two ARE ported, so a cyclic type terminates here
    ; for the reference's reason and not by luck.
    ;
    ; ★★ Ternary collapses to bool, and the collapse is exact at every reader in
    ; this chapter: `Maybe` is produced only by the cycle and depth guards, every
    ; test in the reference is `!= TernaryFalse`, and `result &= related` over a
    ; set of values that are only ever False or not-False is a conjunction. The one
    ; place upstream tests `== TernaryTrue` is the `keyof` arm, and that arm reports.

    ; getGlobalType at arity ZERO. ★A SECOND function rather than an arity
    ; parameter on `get_global_generic_type`, because the two differ in their
    ; failure VALUE and not only in the number: the generic one answers
    ; `empty_generic_type`, a real type the callers instantiate, and the
    ; non-generic one answers `empty_object_type`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 154: THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE WAS A CLAIM ABOUT THE REFERENCE
    ; AND IT WAS WRONG. It read *there is no such stand-in for a non-generic global
    ; — `Object` is either the lib's interface or nothing, and `nothing` has to be
    ; distinguishable*, and getGlobalType's last two lines are
    ; `if arity != 0 { return c.emptyGenericType }; return c.emptyObjectType`: the
    ; stand-in exists, it is the same one the ARITY branch has, and a missing global
    ; is never nil. The cost was measured rather than reasoned about — 60 units of
    ; stage 2 sat on `global-apparent-type`, of which 57 carried TypeFlagsBigInt and
    ; 3 TypeFlagsBigIntLiteral, i.e. every one of them was `getGlobalBigIntType()`
    ; over a lib that declares no `BigInt` (ES5), which upstream answers
    ; emptyObjectType for **with no diagnostic at all**, its resolver being
    ; `getGlobalTypeResolver("BigInt", 0, false /*reportErrors*/)`. A stop was
    ; standing in for the reference's own fallback. ★★The generalisable half is
    ; §3.5gi's one dimension on: a stop can be WRONG rather than absent, and the
    ; sentence that justifies it is the only thing that ever gets read — nobody
    ; re-reads the function it is a claim about.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT report_errors BUYS IS THE ONE THING THIS PORT STILL CANNOT ANSWER.
    ; The three failure shapes upstream reports — TS2318 `Cannot find global type
    ; '0'`, `Global type '0' must have '1' type parameter(s)` and `Global type '0'
    ; must be a class or interface type` — are diagnostics, and a caller that asked
    ; for them gets a stop here instead. The five names that pass `true` (`Object`,
    ; `String`, `Number`, `Boolean` and getGlobalStrictFunctionType's two) are in
    ; every lib this corpus compiles against, so no unit reaches one; the two that
    ; pass `false` (`BigInt`, `Symbol`) are exactly the ones a lib may omit, and for
    ; those the fallback is the whole answer.
    procedure get_global_object_type(mutable this) returns ref[Type]? io reads shared_lock linked










    ; getGlobalType's whole body at arity 0 — slice 124 added five more callers
    ; and a sixth copy of twenty lines would have been the shape §3.5dm is about.
    ; ★It does NOT hold the memo: the memo is a FIELD per global, and a helper that
    ; took a cell would need a `ref[ref[Type]?]` out-parameter to write one. The
    ; wrappers hold their own.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE STRUCTURE IS THE REFERENCE'S AND NOT A CHAIN OF EARLY RETURNS: every
    ; failure falls THROUGH to the same `empty_object_type`, and `report_errors`
    ; decides only whether the fall-through is preceded by the diagnostic this port
    ; owes — see get_global_object_type's header for why that is a stop.
    ; ★A null still means *I stopped*, and the one null with no stop of its own is
    ; get_declared_type_of_symbol's, which is left as it was.
    procedure get_global_type_zero_arity_ex(mutable this, mutable name: Slice[char], flags: int, report_errors: bool) returns ref[Type]? io reads shared_lock linked



































