    ; isDistributionDependent — slice 157. Two lines, and the whole reason the
    ; target CONDITIONAL arm may not simply be attempted: a distributive
    ; conditional whose check type parameter appears in either RESULT cannot be
    ; decided branch by branch, because the distribution is what determines the
    ; branches.
    procedure is_distribution_dependent(mutable this, mutable root: ref[ConditionalRoot]) returns bool io reads ordinal_lock linked















    ; intersectTypes — slice 157. The reference's own two-argument shortcut: a nil
    ; operand is the identity, so `intersectTypes(a, nil)` is `a`.
    procedure intersect_types(mutable this, type1: ref[Type]?, type2: ref[Type]?) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked











    ; isPartialMappedType — a mapped type that ADDS the optional modifier, which
    ; is what makes an empty object type related to it: `{}` satisfies
    ; `{ [P in K]?: T }` because every property may be absent.
    function is_partial_mapped_type(t: ref[Type]) returns bool linked






    ; mappedTypeRelatedTo — the arm the default case takes when BOTH sides are
    ; generic mapped types, slice 157.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MODIFIERS DECIDE WHETHER THE COMPARISON HAPPENS AT ALL, and the
    ; three ways they can be related are not the same question: COMPARABLE asks
    ; nothing, IDENTITY asks for the same modifiers, and every other relation asks
    ; that the source be no MORE optional than the target — which is what makes
    ; `{ [P in K]: T }` assignable to `{ [P in K]?: T }` and not the other way.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `instantiateType(constraint, reportUnmeasurableMapper|reportUnreliableMapper)`
    ; IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THE NOTE THAT CALLED IT THE IDENTITY EXPIRED WITH
    ; `create_marker_type`. It read *both workers return their argument untouched
    ; and only raise a reliability flag for the three MARKER types, which this port
    ; never mints* — true when `measure_variance` was a stop and false since it was
    ; written: the markers ARE minted, and this instantiation is the only route by
    ; which a mapped type in a variance measurement raises Unreliable. Without the
    ; bit the variance comes out INVARIANT with nothing saying it is untrustworthy,
    ; `relate_variances` finds no `AllowsStructuralFallback` and answers false, and
    ; the structural comparison that relates the two instantiations never runs —
    ; `ObjectContaining<T>` with `new (sample: Partial<T>): Partial<T>` reported a
    ; TS2322 on both `cfoo = cafoo` and `cafoo = cfoo`.
    ; ★ The product is DROPPED upstream too in all but name: the two workers hand
    ; back their argument. What the call is for is the side effect — see
    ; `type_mapper_map`'s Reliability arm and `type_arguments_related_to`'s second
    ; instantiation, which is written for exactly the same reason.
    ; ★ `< 0` is the UNMEASURABLE side: an optionality below zero is a `-?` mapped
    ; type, whose relation is non-linear rather than merely unmeasured.
    procedure mapped_type_related_to(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked
















































































    ; relateVariances, the closure inside structuredTypeRelatedToWorker, lifted
    ; into a function of its own — it is called from two arms (the type reference
    ; and the alias probe) and closes over nothing this port carries.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT HAS THREE ANSWERS AND NOT TWO, WHICH IS WHY IT IS AN INT. The
    ; reference returns `(Ternary, bool)`: the boolean says whether the variance
    ; comparison DECIDED, and a false there means *fall through to the structural
    ; comparison below*. Collapsing that into a bool would turn every structural
    ; fallback into a `false` answer.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FALLBACK HAS TWO REASONS AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME. An `Unmeasurable`
    ; or `Unreliable` type parameter falls back because the measurement itself
    ; cannot be trusted; a COVARIANT `void` argument on the target falls back
    ; because `void` is structurally assignable to nothing, so a type parameter
    ; used only in return positions would refuse every source.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `varianceCheckFailed && !(reportErrors && some(invariant))` ARM, AND
    ; THE NOTE THAT ARGUED IT AWAY WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE CHAIN AND WRONG ABOUT THE
    ; ARM. It read *the arm and the error-chain save it guards are the CHAIN's,
    ; which this port does not carry, so the arm's product is the same `false` the
    ; tail already gives* — and what the reference's `false, false` does there is
    ; let the walk GO ON, which is not the same as reporting differently. Two
    ; things happen after it that an early `false` never reaches: the arms BELOW
    ; the alias probe run (the type-reference arm relates `Shape<1>` to
    ; `Shape<1|2>` covariantly and answers TRUE, where the alias's own `in out`
    ; Value is Invariant and fails), and the structural comparison runs for its
    ; elaboration. So the arm is written, and the answer it must NOT give is
    ; *related*: that is the latch — see `RelateVariancesStructuralLatched`.
    ; ★ The `originalErrorChain` half is the caller's here, because in the
    ; reference it is a LOCAL of the worker that the object tail reads; both call
    ; sites save the chain when they take the latched answer.
    procedure relate_variances(mutable this, mutable source_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, mutable target_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, variances: ref[Array[int]], relation: int, error_node: ref[AstNode]?, intersection_state: int) returns int io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked





































