    ; ── slice 130: the DISCRIMINATION WALK ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; discriminateTypeByDiscriminableItems, whole, and it is the routine BOTH
    ; discriminated chapters end in — the relation's `findMatchingDiscriminantType`
    ; and checker.go's contextual-type discrimination
    ; (`discriminateContextualTypeByObjectMembers`, still a stop).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THREE-STATE `include` IS THE ALGORITHM AND A BOOLEAN CANNOT CARRY IT.
    ; Per discriminant property the walk marks every constituent whose value does
    ; NOT match as Maybe, and only if SOME constituent matched are the Maybes
    ; turned into False — otherwise they all revert to True. That is what makes an
    ; erroneous discriminator (one no constituent carries) harmless rather than
    ; fatal: without the revert the set would empty out and the answer would be
    ; `never`. So the three values stay three values here even though the rest of
    ; this chapter collapses Ternary to bool.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS A RECORD RATHER THAN AN INTERFACE, and the arm it
    ; carries is the reference's `TypeDiscriminator`: a property list plus the
    ; relation to ask with. checker.go's implementation reads a JSX/object-literal
    ; member instead and arrives with its own chapter — the tag is what will tell
    ; them apart, on the union arm's convention.
    ; ★★★ SLICE 146 GENERALISED THE SIGNATURE and the note above is what said it
    ; would: the walk now takes the DISCRIMINATOR rather than a property list and
    ; a relation, because checker.go's implementation reads a member's INITIALIZER
    ; where the relation's reads a source property's TYPE. The three-state vector,
    ; the revert rule and the UnionReductionNone tail are untouched.
    procedure discriminate_type_by_discriminable_items(mutable this, mutable target: ref[Type], mutable d: ref[Discriminator]) @page true @returnpage true returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked

























































































































    ; TypeDiscriminator.len.
