    ; ── slice 126: the SIGNATURE relation ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; signaturesRelatedTo, whole but for the two `anyFunctionType` tests, which
    ; have no input: this port mints no such marker.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THREE ARMS ARE A DISPATCH ON HOW THE TWO LISTS LINE UP, not three
    ; different relations. The PAIRWISE arm is two instantiations of ONE anonymous
    ; type, or two references to one target, where the reference erases the type
    ; parameters because they are known to be the same and compares position by
    ; position; the SINGLE arm is the ordinary function-to-function case and erases
    ; only for the comparable relation; the N×M arm is the overload matrix. ★The
    ; empty-target reading the stub carried is still exactly right and is now the
    ; loop running zero times rather than a special case.
    ;
    ; ★★★ EVERY REPORT IN THIS CHAPTER IS A CHAIN LINK AND IS DROPPED, on the
    ; worker's rule: `Relater.reportError` appends to `errorInfo` and
    ; report_relation_error emits ONE diagnostic whose CODE is the head, so a link
    ; carries neither a code nor a span of its own and is invisible in a dump in
    ; both directions. That covers all three of this chapter's — *Cannot assign an
    ; abstract constructor type to a non-abstract constructor type*, *Cannot assign
    ; a 0 constructor type to a 1 constructor type* and *Type 0 provides no match
    ; for the signature 1*. ★★★`getErrorState`/`restoreErrorState` and
    ; `shouldElaborateErrors` DO NOT drop out of the N×M arm, and the note that said
    ; they did — *all three are chain plumbing and this port has no chain* — expired
    ; at slice 167. A target signature that matches its SECOND source candidate
    ; leaves the first one's report standing, and the relation emits on a non-empty
    ; chain whatever its verdict; the two are written in the loop.
