    ; reportErrorResults, whole — and it is four containment proofs and one call.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MARK IS THE PARAMETER THAT MATTERS. Its caller read it before the
    ; comparison began; a report behind a stop would be a diagnostic derived from
    ; an incomplete answer, and on a SUBSEQUENCE relation an invented line is the
    ; one direction that fails. Every unported arm in this chapter is a
    ; `record_unported` that falls through precisely because this line exists.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO OF THE SIX CONTAINMENTS BELOW EXPIRED WITH SLICE 118 AND THE REASON
    ; IS ONE SENTENCE: they were licensed by *the structured branch stops*, and it
    ; does not any more. `tryElaborateArrayLikeErrors` needs Object on BOTH sides,
    ; which is now the ordinary way into this function, and its message —
    ; `The_type_0_is_readonly_and_cannot_be_assigned_to_the_mutable_type_1` — is one
    ; of the five that `reportRelationError`'s suppression switch names, so it is a
    ; chain HEAD and not a link: unported, it is a LOST CODE and not an invisible
    ; one. It is written below as the narrow stop its two report conditions
    ; describe. ★The Object-type arm is the other half of the expiry and it needs
    ; nothing: `globalObjectType` exists since this slice, so the arm can fire —
    ; but its message is a link that `reportRelationError` prepends TS2322 on top
    ; of, and a dump carries the code and the span, so it is invisible in both
    ; directions and is named here rather than written.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE OTHER FOUR HAVE NO INPUT, each for its own reason and each written
    ; where the reference forks: the JSX arm needs ObjectFlagsJsxAttributes and
    ; `globalObjectType`, the JSX arm needs ObjectFlagsJsxAttributes and
    ; `getJsxType`, and the never-intersection arm needs an Intersection, which is
    ; structured. ★`getSingleBaseForNonAugmentingSubtype` and the type ALIAS test
    ; above them decide only WHICH type is NAMED — the reference's `source =
    ; originalSource` — and both are answers about an object: the first reads
    ; ObjectFlagsReference and the second a slot this port does not carry (see
    ; type_to_string's own note). The names decide the CODE only through the
    ; equality below, and both would move the two names together.
    ;
    ; ★ The trailing `This_type_parameter_might_need_an_extends_0_constraint` is
    ; RELATED INFORMATION on the diagnostic this function is about to emit, not a
    ; diagnostic of its own — the dump carries pos, end and code, so it cannot be
    ; seen here in either direction, and its guard needs a TypeParameter source
    ; besides.
    procedure report_error_results(mutable this, mutable original_source: ref[Type], mutable original_target: ref[Type], mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type], relation: int, mark: int) io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked































































































































































































































    ; tryElaborateArrayLikeErrors' report half — the two `if reportErrors` blocks
    ; the bool version above leaves out, and they report the SAME message (TS4104,
    ; *the type '0' is readonly and cannot be assigned to the mutable type '1'*) at
    ; two conditions. ★It answers nothing: the bool is the caller's question and
    ; reportUnmatchedProperty asks it separately, exactly as the reference runs the
    ; same walk twice with the flag flipped. ★★The two `return false` that follow
    ; each report are the BOOL's, not a control decision of this half — every other
    ; path through the reference's function reports nothing at all, so the walk
    ; below stops where the reference's reporting does.
    procedure try_elaborate_array_like_errors_reporting(mutable this, mutable source: ref[Type], mutable target: ref[Type]) io reads checker_strict_null_checks linked

















    ; reportRelationError, down to the message it selects.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE GENERALIZATION IS AN ARGUMENT AND NOT A CODE, so it is not written.
    ; The reference replaces a literal source by its base type for the message's
    ; first `{0}` (`Type '1' is not assignable to type 'string'` reads better as
    ; `Type 'number' is …`), and the dump carries pos, end and code — the same
    ; reading check_assignment_operator's headMessage note makes one dimension over.
    ; What the NAMES do decide is the TS2719 fork, and that one is transcribed.
    ;
    ; ★★ THREE ARMS WITH NO INPUT: the type-parameter target (TypeParameter and
    ; IndexedAccess are both Instantiable, and this function is reached only when
    ; neither side is), `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` (a plain equality against
    ; TSTrue — false under this harness, see the accessor), and the
    ; string-literal-to-UNION suggestion (a Union target is structured). ★And the
    ; four-way suppression switch on `getChainMessage(0)` reads a chain that is
    ; EMPTY: nothing in this slice appends to it before this point, which is what
    ; the enum arms' stops buy.
    ; getSingleBaseForNonAugmentingSubtype — *a reference to a class or interface
    ; that adds NOTHING to its single base*. Its one reader upstream is
    ; reportErrorResults, where it decides which of the two sources the report
    ; speaks about, and that is why this port needs it (see the suppression fork).
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CACHE IS THE `IdenticalBaseTypeCalculated` BIT AND IT IS NOT CARRIED:
    ; upstream memoises the answer on a cachedTypes map keyed by type id, and this
    ; port has no such map. The walk is `getBaseTypes` plus a members count, both
    ; of which are themselves memoised, so re-asking is cheap — but a NEGATIVE
    ; answer is re-derived each time, which is the whole cost.
    procedure get_single_base_for_non_augmenting_subtype(mutable this, mutable t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]? io reads checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions linked






















































































